window.SN_INDEX=[{"i": "excel-01", "t": "Getting Started with Excel for Data Analysis", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-01-getting-started-with-excel-for-data-analysis.html", "s": " Why Excel still matters in 2026 Even with Python, SQL, Power BI and AI assistants, Excel is still the first tool almost every analyst opens. It is where business teams keep budgets, targets, trackers and exports from other systems. If you can clean, analyse and present data in Excel quickly, you can add value from day one in any data role. Excel is also the easiest place to learn data thinking. Every idea you meet here comes back later: Idea in Excel Same idea later in the course A table with one header row A SQL table, a pandas DataFrame, a Power BI table SUMIFS, COUNTIFS GROUP BY in SQL, groupby() in pandas, DAX measures VLOOKUP / XLOOKUP SQL JOIN , pandas merge() , Power BI relationships Pivot table SQL aggregation, pivot_table() in pandas, Power BI matrix Conditional formatting Highlighting in dashboards Live class What was covered in class Live class 1 was the programme introduction. Live class 2 covered the basics of Excel, terminology, shortcuts, Excel properties, data types, data validation and formatting, and we started working on the first sheet. This lesson covers the first half; data validation and formatting continue in Lessons 2 and 3. The Excel window, part by part The main parts of the Excel window. The active cell is C4, and the formula bar shows the formula inside it. Part What it does Tip Ribbon Commands grouped into tabs: Home, Insert, Formulas, Data, Review, View Press Alt to see a key letter on every command Name Box Shows the address of the active cell"}, {"i": "excel-02", "t": "Data Entry, Data Validation & Data Cleaning", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-02-data-entry-data-validation-and-data-cleaning.html", "s": " Garbage in, garbage out Most time in real analytics is not spent on fancy charts; it is spent fixing data: extra spaces, inconsistent spellings ( Bangalore , Bengaluru , bengaluru ), numbers stored as text, duplicates and blanks. Surveys of data teams regularly report that cleaning and preparing data takes well over half of an analyst's time. There are two ways to win this fight: Prevent problems at entry time, and clean whatever slips through. Both are needed. Fast, consistent data entry AutoFill and the fill handle Select a cell and drag the small square at its bottom-right corner (the fill handle ). Excel continues the pattern it recognises. You type Drag gives you Jan Feb, Mar, Apr … Monday Tuesday, Wednesday … 1 , 2 (select both) 3, 4, 5 … Q1 Q2, Q3, Q4, Q1 … 01-04-2026 02-04-2026, 03-04-2026 … (right-drag for months or years) Item 1 Item 2, Item 3 … =B2*12 =B3*12 , =B4*12 … Double-click the fill handle to fill a formula down as far as the column next to it has data. This is the fastest way to copy a formula to 10,000 rows. Tip Custom lists File → Options → Advanced → Edit Custom Lists lets you add your own sequences, for example North, South, East, West or your company's department order. AutoFill and custom sort then follow that order. Flash Fill (Ctrl + E) Flash Fill learns a pattern from one or two examples you type. It is perfect for splitting names, extracting codes or reformatting text. X Flash Fill: type the first answer in each column, then press Ctrl + E A B C"}, {"i": "excel-03", "t": "Number, Custom & Conditional Formatting", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-03-number-custom-and-conditional-formatting.html", "s": " Formatting changes how a value looks, not what it is This is the most important idea in this lesson. When you format 0.256 as 26% , the cell still contains 0.256 . Every formula that uses the cell sees 0.256 . X Same stored value, different formats A B C 1 Stored value Format applied What you see 2 0.256 Percentage, 0 decimals 26% 3 0.256 Percentage, 1 decimal 25.6% 4 1,234,568 Number with separator 1,234,567.89 5 46139 Date 27-Apr-2026 6 46139 Long date Monday, 27 April 2026 7 0.6875 Time 16:30 C2 = TEXT (A2, &quot;0%&quot; ) C3 = TEXT (A3, &quot;0.0%&quot; ) C4 = TEXT (A4, &quot;#,##0.00&quot; ) C5 = TEXT (A5, &quot;dd-mmm-yyyy&quot; ) C6 = TEXT (A6, &quot;dddd, d mmmm yyyy&quot; ) C7 = TEXT (A7, &quot;hh:mm&quot; ) Column C uses the TEXT function to show exactly what each format produces. ( TEXT returns text, so use it for labels, not for numbers you still need to calculate with.) Watch out Rounding by format vs rounding by formula Formatting 2.456 with 1 decimal shows 2.5 but keeps 2.456 . Adding three such cells can show 7.4 while the visible numbers add up to 7.5 . If the rounded value must be used in calculations (invoices, tax), round it with a formula: =ROUND(A2,1) . Cell formatting the professional way Open Format Cells with Ctrl + 1 . It has six tabs: Number, Alignment, Font, Border, Fill, Protection. Goal How Bold header with fill colour Home → Bold ( Ctrl + B ) and Fill Color, or Cell Styles → Heading Wrap long text Home → Wrap Text ( Alt + H, W ) Centre a title"}, {"i": "excel-04", "t": "Formulas, Cell References & Aggregate Functions", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-04-formulas-cell-references-and-aggregate-functions.html", "s": " Formula vs function Definition Two words people mix up A formula is any calculation that starts with = . It can use cell references, numbers, operators and functions: =B2*C2 , =(B2-C2)/C2 , =SUM(B2:B10)*1.18 . A function is a named, ready-made calculation inside a formula: SUM , AVERAGE , IF . A function has a name, brackets and arguments separated by commas. Anatomy of a function call. Arguments in square brackets are optional. When you type a function name and ( , Excel shows a tooltip with its syntax, for example SUMIFS(sum_range, criteria_range1, criteria1, [criteria_range2, criteria2], …) . Press Ctrl + A after the bracket to open the Function Arguments dialog, which explains every argument and previews the result. Operators and order of calculation Operator Meaning Example Result + - * / Add, subtract, multiply, divide =10+4*2 18 ^ Power =2^10 1024 % Percent =500*18% 90 &amp; Join text =\"Q\"&amp;2 Q2 = &lt;&gt; &gt; &lt; &gt;= &lt;= Comparison, returns TRUE/FALSE =B2&gt;=100 TRUE or FALSE : Range A1:A10 10 cells , Union (inside functions) =SUM(A1:A3,C1:C3) Adds both ranges Excel follows the usual maths order, remembered as BODMAS/PEMDAS : brackets, then ^ , then * and / , then + and - , then &amp; , then comparisons. X Order of operations A B C 1 Formula Result Why 2 =10+4*2 18 Multiply first: 10 + 8 3 =(10+4)*2 28 Brackets first: 14 × 2 4 =2^3^2 64 Excel works left to right: (2^3)^2 = 64 5 =-2^2 4 Negation before power in Excel: (-2)^2 = 4 6 =100*(1+18%) 118 Add 18% GS"}, {"i": "excel-05", "t": "Text Functions, Logical Functions & IF", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-05-text-functions-logical-functions-and-if.html", "s": " Why text functions matter Real data arrives as text: order IDs like IN-2025-00042 , product codes, email addresses, \"Last, First\" names, addresses and free-text comments. Text functions let you pull out the part you need (the year, the region code, the domain) so you can analyse it. Measuring and extracting text Function What it returns Example Result LEN(text) Number of characters =LEN(\"Pune\") 4 LEFT(text, n) First n characters =LEFT(\"IN-2025-00042\",2) IN RIGHT(text, n) Last n characters =RIGHT(\"IN-2025-00042\",5) 00042 MID(text, start, n) n characters from position start =MID(\"IN-2025-00042\",4,4) 2025 FIND(find, text, [start]) Position of text (case-sensitive, no wildcards) =FIND(\"-\",\"IN-2025-00042\") 3 SEARCH(find, text, [start]) Position (not case-sensitive, wildcards allowed) =SEARCH(\"pune\",\"PUNE-411001\") 1 X Extracting parts of an order ID A B C D E F 1 Order ID Country =LEFT(A2,2) Year =MID(A2,4,4) Order no. =RIGHT(A2,5) Length Year as number 2 IN-2025-00042 IN 2025 00042 13 2025 3 IN-2023-01519 IN 2023 01519 13 2023 4 IN-2024-02988 IN 2024 02988 13 2024 B2 = LEFT (A2, 2 ) C2 = MID (A2, 4 , 4 ) D2 = RIGHT (A2, 5 ) E2 = LEN (A2) F2 = VALUE ( MID (A2, 4 , 4 )) Each pattern is copied down/across to the other cells in its column. Text functions always return text . MID(A2,4,4) returns the text \"2025\" , not the number 2025. Wrap with VALUE() (or multiply by 1) to get a number you can compare or add. When the position changes: FIND + MID Codes rarely have fixed lengths. Combi"}, {"i": "excel-06", "t": "Sorting, Filtering & Dynamic Arrays", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-06-sorting-filtering-and-dynamic-arrays.html", "s": " The sample table We will use this small sales table (sheet Reps , range A1:F9 ) so every result is easy to check by hand. Revenue is =D2*E2 . X Reps: sample sales table A B C D E F 1 Rep Region Product Units Price Revenue 2 Asha North Laptop 5 55,000 275,000 3 Ravi West Phone 12 18,000 216,000 4 Meera South Laptop 3 55,000 165,000 5 Kabir North Tablet 8 25,000 200,000 6 Zoya East Phone 15 18,000 270,000 7 Farhan West Laptop 4 55,000 220,000 8 Neha South Tablet 10 25,000 250,000 9 Rohan North Phone 9 18,000 162,000 F2 =D2*E2 Each pattern is copied down/across to the other cells in its column. Sorting Quick sort Click any cell in a column and use Data → Sort A to Z or Sort Z to A (or Home → Sort &amp; Filter). Excel detects the table around the cell and keeps each row together. Watch out Never sort a single selected column If you select only one column and sort, Excel asks whether to \"expand the selection\". Choosing \"Continue with the current selection\" sorts that column alone and scrambles your data: names no longer match their revenue. Always click a single cell inside the table, or select the whole table. Multi-level (level-wise) sort Data → Sort opens the Sort dialog, where you add levels. Excel sorts by the first level, then breaks ties with the next. The Sort dialog with two levels: Region A to Z, then Revenue largest to smallest. X Result: Region A→Z, then Revenue high→low A B C D 1 Rep Region Product Revenue 2 Zoya East Phone 270,000 3 Asha North Laptop 275,000 4 Kabir"}, {"i": "excel-07", "t": "Lookup Functions: VLOOKUP, XLOOKUP and INDEX-MATCH", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-07-lookup-functions-vlookup-xlookup-and-index-match.html", "s": " Why lookups matter Business data is split across tables: orders in one, products in another, customers in a third. A lookup fetches a value from one table using a key that both tables share, such as a product ID or employee ID. A lookup takes a key from one table, finds it in another table, and returns a value from the same row. Tip You are already learning SQL joins A lookup is a left join for one column at a time: keep every row of the main table and bring in matching information from the other table. You will see the same idea as JOIN in SQL (SQL Lesson 8), merge() in pandas, and relationships in Power BI. We will use this lookup table on a sheet called Prod : X Prod: product lookup table (A1:E7) A B C D E 1 Product ID Product Category Price Stock 2 P101 Office Chair Furniture 4500 32 3 P102 Standing Desk Furniture 18500 8 4 P103 Laser Printer Technology 12900 15 5 P104 Notebook Pack Office Supplies 240 540 6 P105 Smartphone Technology 21999 27 7 P106 Bookcase Furniture 7600 0 VLOOKUP =VLOOKUP(lookup_value, table_array, col_index_num, [range_lookup]) lookup_value : what you are looking for (the key). table_array : the table to search; the key must be in its first column . col_index_num : which column of that table to return (1 = first column). range_lookup : FALSE (or 0) for an exact match. Use TRUE only for approximate matching on sorted bands. X Order lines looking up product details with VLOOKUP A B C D E F 1 Order Product ID Qty Product =VLOOKUP(B2,Prod!$A$2:$E$7,2,FA"}, {"i": "excel-08", "t": "Pivot Tables from Basic to Advanced", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-08-pivot-tables-from-basic-to-advanced.html", "s": " What a pivot table does A pivot table groups rows by one or more fields and summarises a number for each group (sum, count, average…). It is the fastest way to answer \"how much, by what?\" questions, with no formulas. A pivot table turns thousands of order lines into a compact summary: rows grouped by Region, columns by Category, values summed. Creating a pivot table Step by step Step by step Make sure the data is structured (Lesson 1): one header row, no blank rows, no merged cells. Ideally convert it to a Table ( Ctrl + T ) so new rows are included automatically. Click any cell in the data → Insert → PivotTable (or Alt, N, V ). Choose New Worksheet → OK. An empty pivot appears with the PivotTable Fields pane on the right. Drag fields into the four areas: drag region to Rows , category to Columns , sales to Values . Right-click any number → Number Format → #,##0 so the format sticks when the pivot refreshes. The PivotTable Fields pane: a list of fields at the top, four areas at the bottom. Area What it does Example Rows One row per unique value Region, Category, Order Date Columns One column per unique value Year, Segment Values The numbers being summarised Sum of Sales, Count of Order ID Filters Filter the whole pivot by a field Segment = Corporate The pivot cache, Refresh and Refresh All A pivot table does not read your sheet directly. When you create it, Excel copies the data into a hidden pivot cache , and the pivot reads from that cache. This is why pivots are fast, and"}, {"i": "excel-09", "t": "Date, Time & Number Functions and Named Ranges", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-09-date-time-and-number-functions-and-named-ranges.html", "s": " Dates and times are numbers Excel stores a date as the number of days since 1 January 1900 (that day is 1). A time is a fraction of a day : 12:00 noon is 0.5 and 06:00 is 0.25. A date with a time is simply a whole number plus a fraction. X What is really stored A B C 1 You see Stored as Explanation 2 27-04-2026 46139 Days since 1-Jan-1900 3 12:00 0.5 Half a day 4 06:30 0.2708 6.5 hours ÷ 24 5 27-04-2026 18:00 46139.75 Date + time B2 =A2 B3 = TIME ( 12 , 0 , 0 ) B4 = TIME ( 6 , 30 , 0 ) B5 = DATE ( 2026 , 4 , 27 )+ TIME ( 18 , 0 , 0 ) This is why date arithmetic works: X Date arithmetic A B C D E 1 Order date Ship date Days to ship =B2-A2 Due date (+30 days) Overdue? (as of 15-Jun-2026) 2 27-04-2026 02-05-2026 5 27-05-2026 Overdue 3 20-05-2026 21-05-2026 1 19-06-2026 OK 4 01-06-2026 08-06-2026 7 01-07-2026 OK C2 =B2-A2 D2 =A2+ 30 E2 = IF ( DATE ( 2026 , 6 , 15 )&gt;D2, &quot;Overdue&quot; , &quot;OK&quot; ) Each pattern is copied down/across to the other cells in its column. Tip TODAY() and NOW() =TODAY() returns today's date and =NOW() the date and time. They are volatile : they recalculate every time the workbook changes, so results move every day. For reports that must not change, type the \"as of\" date into a cell and refer to it, as in the overdue example above. Shortcuts: Ctrl + ; types today's date as a fixed value. Building and taking apart dates Function Returns Example (A2 = 27-04-2026) Result DATE(y, m, d) A date from parts =DATE(2026,4,27) 27-04-2026 YEAR , MONTH ,"}, {"i": "excel-10", "t": "Charts and Advanced Charts", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-10-charts-and-advanced-charts.html", "s": " Start with the question, not the chart Choose a chart by the question you are answering. Creating and editing a chart Step by step The fast way Select the data including headers (or a summary table / pivot table). For a quick chart press Alt + F1 (embedded) or F11 (new chart sheet). Or use Insert → Recommended Charts to preview options. Click the chart: three buttons appear at its top-right: Chart Elements (+), Chart Styles (brush) and Chart Filters (funnel). Chart Design tab: Switch Row/Column, Select Data, Change Chart Type, Move Chart, Quick Layout. Format pane: double-click any element (axis, series, label) or press Ctrl + 1 with it selected. Element Good practice Title Say the insight: \"Technology drives 77% of sales\", not \"Sales by Category\" Axes Start bar/column axes at zero; format numbers (K, L, %); remove unnecessary decimals Gridlines Light grey or none Data labels Use them instead of an axis when there are few bars Legend Only if there is more than one series; better: label lines directly Colour One highlight colour for the point you want to make, grey for the rest Template Right-click → Save as Template to reuse your style The core chart types Here are the core charts built from Superstore data. The Excel steps follow each one. Column (comparison), line (trend), clustered and stacked bar (comparison of parts), and scatter (relationship). Chart Excel path Use when Clustered column / bar Insert → Column or Bar Comparing categories; use bar when labels are long or "}, {"i": "excel-11", "t": "Interactive Dashboard: Superstore Case Study", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-11-interactive-dashboard-superstore-case-study.html", "s": " The brief Case study Superstore sales review The Head of Sales wants a one-page Excel dashboard to review performance for 2023–2025. They want to see, at a glance: total sales, profit, profit margin and orders; how sales are trending; which regions, categories and sub-categories perform best and worst; and the effect of discounts on profit. They want to filter by year, region and segment. The dashboard will be emailed every month, so it must be protected against accidental changes. Step 0: questions and KPIs first KPI / question Calculation Visual Total sales Sum of sales KPI card Total profit and margin Sum of profit; profit ÷ sales KPI cards Number of orders Distinct count of order_id KPI card Sales trend Sales by month Line chart Regional performance Sales and profit by region Bar chart Product mix Sales by category and sub-category Bar chart / treemap Discount impact Profit by discount level Column chart Filters Year, Region, Segment Timeline + slicers Agreeing on this table before building saves hours of rework. It is also your \"requirements document\" in interviews. Here is the finished result we are aiming for (drawn from the real data): The finished Superstore dashboard: KPI cards on top, trend and breakdown charts below, slicers on the left. Step 1: prepare the data Import superstore_sales.csv with Data → From Text/CSV (Power Query, Lesson 12), or open it and convert the range to a Table named tblSales ( Ctrl + T ). Add helper columns that the pivots will need: Colum"}, {"i": "excel-12", "t": "Power Query, Power Pivot, Analysis ToolPak & What-If Analysis", "k": "Excel", "f": "lessons/excel-12-power-query-power-pivot-analysis-toolpak-and-what-if-analysi.html", "s": " Why \"Power\" tools? Formulas and pivots are great until the same cleaning has to be repeated every month, the data has several related tables, or it grows past a million rows. The Power tools solve exactly these problems, and they are the same engines used in Power BI , so everything you learn here transfers directly. The modern Excel analytics stack. Power Query and the Data Model are shared with Power BI. Power Query: repeatable cleaning Power Query (Data → Get &amp; Transform Data ) imports data and records every cleaning step you click. Next month, put the new file in place and click Refresh : all steps run again automatically. Step by step Your first query Data → Get Data → From File → From Text/CSV → choose superstore_sales.csv → Transform Data . The Power Query Editor opens. Check data types in each column header (ABC = text, 123 = whole number, 1.2 = decimal, calendar = date). Fix any wrong ones (right-click header → Change Type). Remove columns you do not need (select → right-click → Remove, or Choose Columns ). Filter rows with the header drop-downs (for example remove test orders). Add a column: Add Column → Date → Year , or Custom Column with a formula such as [sales] - [profit] . Rename the query (right panel) to Sales → Home → Close &amp; Load To… → Table, PivotTable report, or Only Create Connection + Add to Data Model . The Power Query Editor. Every action you take is recorded in Applied Steps on the right. Transformations you will use most Transformation Wher"}, {"i": "sql-01", "t": "Databases, RDBMS & SQL Basics", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-01-databases-rdbms-and-sql-basics.html", "s": " What is a database? A database is an organised collection of data stored so that it can be found, updated and analysed efficiently and safely by many people at the same time. A DBMS (database management system) is the software that manages it. Your bank balance, every Swiggy order, every Aadhaar-linked record and every hospital appointment lives in a database. Spreadsheets are personal tools; databases are shared systems built for scale, safety and many users. Types of databases Type Organises data as Examples Typical use Relational (RDBMS) Tables with rows and columns, linked by keys SQL Server, PostgreSQL, MySQL, Oracle, SQLite Orders, payments, HR, finance: most business data Document JSON-like documents MongoDB, Cosmos DB Product catalogues, app content Key-value Key → value pairs Redis, DynamoDB Caching, sessions Column-family / warehouse Columns, built for analytics Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift, Databricks SQL Analytics on huge data Graph Nodes and edges Neo4j Social networks, fraud rings Vector Number vectors (embeddings) pgvector, Pinecone AI search and RAG (GenAI track) This track focuses on relational databases and SQL , the language almost every data role uses daily. You will practise in Microsoft SQL Server with SSMS , as in class. The SQL you learn transfers to PostgreSQL, MySQL, Snowflake and BigQuery with small differences in functions. How a relational database organises data The hierarchy inside SQL Server: a server holds databases, a database holds schemas"}, {"i": "sql-02", "t": "ER Diagrams, Relationships, Keys & Normalisation", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-02-er-diagrams-relationships-keys-and-normalisation.html", "s": " Why design matters A database that is designed badly is slow, wastes space and, worst of all, lets data contradict itself. Imagine the customer's city stored in every order row: if the customer moves, you must update thousands of rows, and missing one creates two different \"truths\". Good design stores each fact once and connects tables with keys . Entity–Relationship (ER) diagrams An ER diagram is a picture of the data model before any table is created. Definition Building blocks Entity : a real-world thing you store data about: Customer, Order, Product, Employee. Becomes a table . Attribute : a property of an entity: name, city, price. Becomes a column . Relationship : how entities are associated: a Customer places Orders. Implemented with foreign keys . Cardinality : how many of one entity relate to another: one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-many. Participation (optionality) : whether the relationship is mandatory (every order must have a customer) or optional (a customer may have zero orders). Crow's-foot notation The most common notation in industry draws symbols at the ends of each relationship line: Crow's-foot symbols. Read each end as \"minimum … maximum\". Reading example: Customer ||——o&lt; Order means \"each order belongs to exactly one customer; each customer places zero or many orders\". Types of relationships One-to-one (1:1) Each row in A matches at most one row in B. Example: Employee ↔ Employee_Bank_Details. Often used to split sensitive or rarely used columns in"}, {"i": "sql-03", "t": "Data Types, Operators, NULLs, Variables & Comments", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-03-data-types-operators-nulls-variables-and-comments.html", "s": " Why data types matter Every column has exactly one data type . The type decides what values are allowed, how much storage they use, how they sort and compare, and which functions work on them. A column of dates stored as text sorts \"01-02-2026\" before \"31-01-2025\" and cannot be used for date arithmetic, the same problem you met in Excel. SQL Server data types The main families of SQL Server data types. Numbers Type Range / precision Storage Use for TINYINT 0 to 255 1 byte Ratings, small codes SMALLINT −32,768 to 32,767 2 bytes Small counts INT about ±2.1 billion 4 bytes IDs, quantities (default choice) BIGINT about ±9.2 × 10¹⁸ 8 bytes Very large IDs, event logs DECIMAL(p, s) / NUMERIC exact, p digits with s after the point 5–17 bytes Money , rates: DECIMAL(12,2) holds up to 9,999,999,999.99 MONEY exact, 4 decimals 8 bytes Currency (DECIMAL is more portable) FLOAT , REAL approximate binary floating point 8 / 4 bytes Scientific measurements, ML scores BIT 0, 1 or NULL 1 bit Yes/No flags Watch out Never store money in FLOAT FLOAT cannot represent many decimal fractions exactly (0.1 + 0.2 is not exactly 0.3), so totals drift by tiny amounts. Use DECIMAL(p, s) for currency. Text Type Description Example CHAR(n) Fixed length, padded with spaces CHAR(2) for state codes MH VARCHAR(n) Variable length, up to n bytes (n ≤ 8000) VARCHAR(100) for names in English NVARCHAR(n) Variable length Unicode (Hindi, Tamil, emoji) NVARCHAR(100) for names in any language VARCHAR(MAX) , NVARCHAR(MAX)"}, {"i": "sql-04", "t": "DDL, DML & Constraints: CREATE, ALTER, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-04-ddl-dml-and-constraints-create-alter-insert-update-delete.html", "s": " The life cycle of a table DDL creates and changes structure; DML fills and changes the rows inside it. CREATE TABLE SQL Copy CREATE TABLE departments ( dept_id INT PRIMARY KEY , dept_name VARCHAR ( 50 ) NOT NULL UNIQUE , location VARCHAR ( 50 ) DEFAULT &#x27;Bengaluru&#x27; ); CREATE TABLE staff ( emp_id INT PRIMARY KEY , full_name VARCHAR ( 100 ) NOT NULL , email VARCHAR ( 100 ) NOT NULL UNIQUE , dept_id INT NOT NULL REFERENCES departments (dept_id), salary DECIMAL ( 10 , 2 ) CHECK (salary &gt; 0 ), join_date DATE DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE, is_active BOOLEAN DEFAULT TRUE ); Result Commands completed successfully. Commands completed successfully. In SQL Server you would usually write IDENTITY(1,1) for an auto-numbered key, BIT for the flag and DEFAULT GETDATE() for today. Here is the same staff table in full T-SQL style, with named constraints (names make error messages and later changes much easier): SQL Copy CREATE TABLE dbo.staff ( emp_id INT IDENTITY ( 1 , 1 ) NOT NULL , full_name NVARCHAR ( 100 ) NOT NULL , email VARCHAR ( 100 ) NOT NULL , dept_id INT NOT NULL , salary DECIMAL ( 10 , 2 ) NULL , join_date DATE NOT NULL CONSTRAINT df_staff_join DEFAULT ( CAST ( GETDATE () AS DATE )), is_active BIT NOT NULL CONSTRAINT df_staff_active DEFAULT ( 1 ), CONSTRAINT pk_staff PRIMARY KEY (emp_id), CONSTRAINT uq_staff_email UNIQUE (email), CONSTRAINT fk_staff_dept FOREIGN KEY (dept_id) REFERENCES dbo.departments (dept_id), CONSTRAINT ck_staff_salary CHECK (salary &gt; 0 ) ); Constraints"}, {"i": "sql-05", "t": "SELECT, WHERE, ORDER BY & Filtering Operators", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-05-select-where-order-by-and-filtering-operators.html", "s": " The shape of a SELECT statement SQL Copy SELECT column_list -- what to show FROM table_name -- where it comes from WHERE row_conditions -- which rows to keep GROUP BY grouping_columns -- (Lesson 7) HAVING group_conditions -- (Lesson 7) ORDER BY sort_columns; -- how to sort the result Only SELECT is required ( SELECT 1 + 1; works). Everything else is optional but must appear in this order. SELECT: columns, expressions and aliases SQL Copy SELECT TOP 5 order_id, order_date, customer_name, sales, profit, sales - profit AS cost, ROUND ( 100.0 * profit / sales, 1 ) AS margin_pct, &#x27;INR&#x27; AS currency FROM superstore; Result order_id order_date customer_name sales profit cost margin_pct currency IN-2023-00001 2023-01-01 Ishaan Gupta 36,000 3,472.55 32,527.45 9.6 INR IN-2023-00001 2023-01-01 Ishaan Gupta 3,850 1,169.54 2,680.46 30.4 INR IN-2023-00001 2023-01-01 Ishaan Gupta 62,080 6,044.47 56,035.53 9.7 INR IN-2023-00002 2023-01-03 Kabir Singh 5,216 450.16 4,765.84 8.6 INR IN-2023-00003 2023-01-03 Sneha Khan 24,591 -5,512.33 30,103.33 -22.4 INR 5 rows × 8 columns SELECT * returns every column. Use it to explore, never in production code (it breaks when columns change and reads more data than needed). An alias renames a column or table in the result: AS margin_pct . AS is optional ( sales - profit cost ) but makes code clearer. Aliases with spaces need brackets or quotes: AS [Margin %] . Expressions can use operators, functions and literals. Table aliases SQL Copy SELECT s.or"}, {"i": "sql-06", "t": "SQL Functions: Aggregate, Text, Date & Number", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-06-sql-functions-aggregate-text-date-and-number.html", "s": " Two kinds of functions Scalar functions return one value per row. Aggregate functions collapse many rows into one value (per group). Aggregate functions Function Returns NULL handling COUNT(*) Number of rows Counts every row COUNT(col) Number of non-NULL values Skips NULLs COUNT(DISTINCT col) Number of distinct non-NULL values Skips NULLs SUM(col) Total Skips NULLs AVG(col) Average of non-NULL values Skips NULLs (they are not treated as 0) MIN(col) , MAX(col) Smallest / largest (numbers, text, dates) Skips NULLs STDEV(col) , VAR(col) Sample standard deviation / variance Skips NULLs STRING_AGG(col, sep) Values joined into one string Skips NULLs SQL Copy SELECT COUNT (*) AS order_lines, COUNT ( DISTINCT order_id) AS orders, COUNT ( DISTINCT customer_id) AS customers, ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) AS total_sales, ROUND ( AVG (sales), 0 ) AS avg_line_sales, MIN (order_date) AS first_order, MAX (order_date) AS last_order, ROUND ( STDEV (sales), 0 ) AS sd_sales FROM superstore; Result order_lines orders customers total_sales avg_line_sales first_order last_order sd_sales 6323 3200 785 201,413,569 31,854 2023-01-01 2025-12-31 60,414 1 rows × 8 columns SQL Copy SELECT category, STRING_AGG (sub_category, &#x27;, &#x27; ) AS sub_categories FROM ( SELECT DISTINCT category, sub_category FROM superstore) AS t GROUP BY category ORDER BY category; Result category sub_categories Furniture Tables, Furnishings, Bookcases, Chairs Office Supplies Storage, Appliances, Art, Binders, Paper Technology C"}, {"i": "sql-07", "t": "GROUP BY, HAVING, CASE WHEN, ROLLUP, CUBE & COALESCE", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-07-group-by-having-case-when-rollup-cube-and-coalesce.html", "s": " GROUP BY: one row per group GROUP BY collapses rows that share the same value(s) into one row, and aggregate functions summarise each group. It is the SQL version of a pivot table (Excel Lesson 8) and of groupby() in pandas. GROUP BY region: rows with the same region are collected into one group, then SUM is computed for each group. SQL Copy SELECT region, COUNT ( DISTINCT order_id) AS orders, ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) AS sales, ROUND ( SUM (profit), 0 ) AS profit, ROUND ( 100.0 * SUM (profit) / SUM (sales), 1 ) AS margin_pct FROM superstore GROUP BY region ORDER BY sales DESC ; Result region orders sales profit margin_pct South 831 53,952,769 4,702,012 8.7 West 811 51,958,521 4,719,833 9.1 North 792 49,018,161 4,555,830 9.3 East 766 46,484,119 4,374,490 9.4 4 rows × 5 columns Grouping by several columns SQL Copy SELECT category, sub_category, COUNT (*) AS lines, ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) AS sales, ROUND ( SUM (profit), 0 ) AS profit FROM superstore GROUP BY category, sub_category ORDER BY category, sales DESC ; Result category sub_category lines sales profit Furniture Tables 505 14,046,224 -816,042 Furniture Bookcases 477 11,274,849 -422,786 Furniture Chairs 493 8,091,474 342,043 Furniture Furnishings 442 1,429,307 78,632 Office Supplies Appliances 492 6,333,529 944,927 Office Supplies Storage 507 2,659,340 397,228 Office Supplies Paper 559 493,232 75,040 Office Supplies Binders 494 401,219 61,074 Office Supplies Art 462 245,985 36,526 Technology Copiers 468 78,749,926 8,436,"}, {"i": "sql-08", "t": "Joins and a Retail Case Study", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-08-joins-and-a-retail-case-study.html", "s": " Why joins? In a normalised database (Lesson 2) customer details live in customers , products in products , and orders in orders and order_items . To answer \"sales by customer segment and product category\" you must join them back together on their keys. A join is SQL's version of XLOOKUP (Excel Lesson 7) and pandas merge() . A tiny example first Two small tables make every join type easy to see: SQL Copy CREATE TABLE students (student_id INT PRIMARY KEY , name VARCHAR ( 20 )); INSERT INTO students VALUES ( 1 , &#x27;Asha&#x27; ), ( 2 , &#x27;Ravi&#x27; ), ( 3 , &#x27;Meera&#x27; ), ( 4 , &#x27;Kabir&#x27; ); CREATE TABLE enrolments (student_id INT , course VARCHAR ( 20 )); INSERT INTO enrolments VALUES ( 1 , &#x27;SQL&#x27; ), ( 1 , &#x27;Python&#x27; ), ( 2 , &#x27;Excel&#x27; ), ( 5 , &#x27;Power BI&#x27; ); SELECT &#x27;students&#x27; AS t, COUNT (*) AS n FROM students UNION ALL SELECT &#x27;enrolments&#x27; , COUNT (*) FROM enrolments; Result Commands completed successfully. (4 rows affected) Commands completed successfully. (4 rows affected) t n students 4 enrolments 4 2 rows × 2 columns Asha (1) has two enrolments, Ravi (2) has one, Meera (3) and Kabir (4) have none, and enrolment for student 5 has no matching student (an \"orphan\" row). The join types as Venn diagrams. Shaded areas are the rows returned (A = left table, B = right table). INNER JOIN: only matches SQL Copy SELECT s.student_id, s.name, e.course FROM students AS s INNER JOIN enrolments AS e ON e.student_id "}, {"i": "sql-09", "t": "Set Operators: UNION, UNION ALL, INTERSECT & EXCEPT", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-09-set-operators-union-union-all-intersect-and-except.html", "s": " Joins add columns; set operators add rows A join puts tables side by side (more columns). A set operator stacks or compares the rows of two query results with the same columns. The four set operators. A and B are the result sets of two queries. Rules for set operators Both queries must return the same number of columns . Corresponding columns must have compatible data types (text with text, numbers with numbers). Column names come from the first query . Only one ORDER BY , at the very end, applies to the combined result. UNION , INTERSECT and EXCEPT remove duplicates; UNION ALL does not. UNION ALL: append UNION ALL stacks results exactly as they are, like Power Query's Append or pasting one table under another. It is the fastest set operator because it does not check for duplicates. SQL Copy SELECT &#x27;Furniture&#x27; AS source, COUNT (*) AS lines, ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) AS sales FROM superstore WHERE category = &#x27;Furniture&#x27; UNION ALL SELECT &#x27;Technology&#x27; , COUNT (*), ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) FROM superstore WHERE category = &#x27;Technology&#x27; UNION ALL SELECT &#x27;Office Supplies&#x27; , COUNT (*), ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) FROM superstore WHERE category = &#x27;Office Supplies&#x27; ORDER BY sales DESC ; Result source lines sales Technology 1892 156,438,413 Furniture 1917 34,841,853 Office Supplies 2514 10,133,304 3 rows × 3 columns A very common real use: combining monthly or regional tables with the same structure. SQL Copy SELECT * FROM sales_j"}, {"i": "sql-10", "t": "Subqueries, Derived Tables & EXISTS", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-10-subqueries-derived-tables-and-exists.html", "s": " What is a subquery? A subquery (inner query, nested query) is a SELECT written inside another statement, in brackets. The outer query uses its result. Where subqueries can appear and what they return. By what it returns Example Used with Scalar (one value) (SELECT AVG(sales) FROM superstore) =, &gt;, &lt; … or as a column Multi-row (one column, many rows) (SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE …) IN , NOT IN , ANY , ALL , EXISTS Table (many columns and rows) (SELECT region, SUM(sales) … GROUP BY region) FROM as a derived table Scalar subqueries \"Which order lines are above the average line value?\" SQL Copy SELECT COUNT (*) AS lines_above_average, ( SELECT ROUND ( AVG (sales), 0 ) FROM superstore) AS average_line_sales FROM superstore WHERE sales &gt; ( SELECT AVG (sales) FROM superstore); Result lines_above_average average_line_sales 1603 31,854 1 rows × 2 columns The inner query runs first and returns one number; the outer query compares each row with it. You cannot write WHERE sales &gt; AVG(sales) directly, because aggregates are not allowed in WHERE (Lesson 5's processing order). \"Show each region's sales and its share of the grand total\": SQL Copy SELECT region, ROUND ( SUM (sales), 0 ) AS sales, ROUND ( 100.0 * SUM (sales) / ( SELECT SUM (sales) FROM superstore), 1 ) AS pct_of_total FROM superstore GROUP BY region ORDER BY sales DESC ; Result region sales pct_of_total South 53,952,769 26.8 West 51,958,521 25.8 North 49,018,161 24.3 East 46,484,119 23.1 4 rows × 3 column"}, {"i": "sql-11", "t": "OFFSET-FETCH and Window Functions I: OVER, PARTITION BY & Ranking", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-11-offset-fetch-and-window-functions-i-over-partition-by-and-ra.html", "s": " OFFSET and FETCH: pagination OFFSET n ROWS FETCH NEXT m ROWS ONLY skips n rows and returns the next m. It requires ORDER BY . It is the standard (ANSI) way to page results, used for \"page 3 of results\" in applications and for \"rows 11 to 20\" questions. SQL Copy SELECT customer_name, city, segment FROM customers ORDER BY customer_id OFFSET 10 ROWS FETCH NEXT 5 ROWS ONLY ; Result customer_name city segment Sai Iyer Indore Consumer Neha Kulkarni Bengaluru Consumer Diya Khan Bhubaneswar Home Office Kabir Das Kolkata Consumer Reyansh Joshi Mumbai Corporate 5 rows × 3 columns Page OFFSET FETCH 1 0 page size 2 page size page size page p (p − 1) × page size page size TOP n cannot skip rows; OFFSET-FETCH can. PostgreSQL/MySQL write LIMIT m OFFSET n . On very deep pages (OFFSET 1,000,000) databases still read and discard all skipped rows, so large systems use keyset pagination ( WHERE id &gt; last_seen_id ORDER BY id ). The \"Nth highest value\" question can be answered with it too: the second-highest line value is ORDER BY sales DESC OFFSET 1 ROWS FETCH NEXT 1 ROWS ONLY on distinct values: SQL Copy SELECT DISTINCT sales AS second_highest_sales FROM superstore ORDER BY sales DESC OFFSET 1 ROWS FETCH NEXT 1 ROWS ONLY ; Result second_highest_sales 551,530 1 rows × 1 columns What is a window function? A window function computes a value for each row using a set of related rows (its window ), without collapsing the rows like GROUP BY does. GROUP BY collapses rows into one per group. A window"}, {"i": "sql-12", "t": "Window Functions II: Running Totals, LEAD, LAG & Frames", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-12-window-functions-ii-running-totals-lead-lag-and-frames.html", "s": " Window frames Inside OVER , ORDER BY defines the order and an optional frame defines which rows around the current row the function uses. SQL Copy SUM (x) OVER ( PARTITION BY p ORDER BY d ROWS BETWEEN &lt;start&gt; AND &lt; end &gt;) Frame boundary Meaning UNBOUNDED PRECEDING From the first row of the partition n PRECEDING n rows before the current row CURRENT ROW The current row n FOLLOWING n rows after UNBOUNDED FOLLOWING To the last row of the partition Frames relative to the current row (highlighted). A running total uses everything up to the current row; a 3-row moving average uses the previous two rows plus the current one. Common mistakes The default frame trap When ORDER BY is present and no frame is written, SQL Server uses RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW . RANGE treats rows with the same ORDER BY value as peers and includes them all, which gives surprising results with ties, and LAST_VALUE then returns the current row's value. For running totals always write ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW explicitly; it is also much faster. Running totals First build the monthly sales table, then add a running (cumulative) total and a share: SQL Copy WITH monthly AS ( SELECT YEAR (order_date) AS yr, MONTH (order_date) AS mth, SUM (sales) AS sales FROM superstore GROUP BY YEAR (order_date), MONTH (order_date) ) SELECT yr, mth, ROUND (sales, 0 ) AS sales, ROUND ( SUM (sales) OVER ( PARTITION BY yr ORDER BY mth ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND C"}, {"i": "sql-13", "t": "CTEs, Views, Temporary Tables, Stored Procedures & Functions", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-13-ctes-views-temporary-tables-stored-procedures-and-functions.html", "s": " CTE: a named, readable subquery A Common Table Expression is a named result set defined with WITH that exists for one statement . It makes complex logic read top to bottom, like paragraphs, instead of inside-out. SQL Copy WITH name1 AS ( SELECT … ), name2 AS ( SELECT … FROM name1 … -- later CTEs can use earlier ones ) SELECT … FROM name2 …; From nested subqueries to CTEs Question: \"Which regions had above-average 2025 sales per customer?\" SQL Copy WITH customer_sales AS ( -- step 1: sales per customer in 2025 SELECT c.region, c.customer_id, SUM (oi.sales) AS sales FROM order_items AS oi JOIN orders AS o ON o.order_id = oi.order_id JOIN customers AS c ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id WHERE o.order_date &gt;= &#x27;2025-01-01&#x27; AND o.order_date &lt; &#x27;2026-01-01&#x27; GROUP BY c.region, c.customer_id ), region_avg AS ( -- step 2: average per region SELECT region, COUNT (*) AS customers, AVG (sales) AS avg_sales_per_customer FROM customer_sales GROUP BY region ) SELECT region, customers, ROUND (avg_sales_per_customer, 0 ) AS avg_sales_per_customer, ROUND (( SELECT AVG (sales) FROM customer_sales), 0 ) AS overall_avg FROM region_avg WHERE avg_sales_per_customer &gt; ( SELECT AVG (sales) FROM customer_sales) -- step 3: compare ORDER BY avg_sales_per_customer DESC ; Result region customers avg_sales_per_customer overall_avg West 156 142,513 125,805 South 160 130,010 125,805 2 rows × 4 columns customer_sales is used twice , which with plain subqueries would mean copy-pastin"}, {"i": "sql-14", "t": "Transactions, Indexes, Performance & Security", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-14-transactions-indexes-performance-and-security.html", "s": " Transactions and ACID A transaction is a group of statements treated as one unit of work: all succeed (commit) or none do (rollback) . Moving ₹5,000 between two accounts is two updates; if the server crashes between them, money would vanish without a transaction. A bank transfer as a transaction. Either both updates are saved, or both are undone. Property Meaning Example A tomicity All or nothing Both account updates happen, or neither C onsistency Data stays valid (constraints hold) Total money before = total after; no negative balance if a CHECK forbids it I solation Concurrent transactions do not see each other's half-finished work Another user does not see the debit without the credit D urability Once committed, it survives a crash Transaction log is written to disk first Transaction commands Command Purpose BEGIN TRANSACTION ( BEGIN TRAN ) Start a transaction COMMIT Make all changes permanent ROLLBACK Undo everything since BEGIN (or since a savepoint) SAVE TRANSACTION name Create a savepoint inside the transaction ROLLBACK TRANSACTION name Undo back to the savepoint only @@TRANCOUNT How many transactions are open (should be 0 when you leave) By default SQL Server runs in autocommit mode: every single statement is its own transaction. Wrap several statements in BEGIN TRAN … COMMIT to group them. Seeing a rollback SQL Copy CREATE TABLE accounts (acct_id INT PRIMARY KEY , holder VARCHAR ( 20 ), balance DECIMAL ( 12 , 2 )); INSERT INTO accounts VALUES ( 1 , &#x27;Asha&#x27;"}, {"i": "sql-15", "t": "End-to-End SQL Case Study & Interview Practice", "k": "SQL", "f": "lessons/sql-15-end-to-end-sql-case-study-and-interview-practice.html", "s": " The business problem Case study Retail growth review You are the data analyst at an Indian retailer selling Furniture, Office Supplies and Technology through four regions. Sales grew from 2023 to 2025, but the CFO suspects profit is not keeping pace. Management wants answers to five questions: (1) Is the data trustworthy? (2) How are sales and profit really trending? (3) Where are we losing money and why? (4) Who are our best customers and are they staying? (5) What should we do next quarter? A repeatable SQL analysis workflow. Step 1: understand the data SQL Copy SELECT &#x27;orders&#x27; AS tbl, COUNT (*) AS n, MIN (order_date) AS first_date, MAX (order_date) AS last_date FROM orders UNION ALL SELECT &#x27;order_items&#x27; , COUNT (*), NULL , NULL FROM order_items UNION ALL SELECT &#x27;customers&#x27; , COUNT (*), NULL , NULL FROM customers UNION ALL SELECT &#x27;products&#x27; , COUNT (*), NULL , NULL FROM products; Result tbl n first_date last_date orders 3200 2023-01-01 2025-12-31 order_items 6323 NULL NULL customers 800 NULL NULL products 78 NULL NULL 4 rows × 4 columns Grain : order_items has one row per product line in an order; orders one row per order; customers one row per customer. KPIs like \"orders\" must use COUNT(DISTINCT order_id) , and \"average order value\" needs order-level totals (Lesson 10). Step 2: data-quality checks Always check before analysing. A compact data-quality report: SQL Copy SELECT ( SELECT COUNT (*) FROM order_items WHERE sales IS NULL OR "}, {"i": "powerbi-01", "t": "Getting Started with Power BI & Your First HR Report", "k": "Power BI", "f": "lessons/powerbi-01-getting-started-with-power-bi-and-your-first-hr-report.html", "s": " What is Power BI? Power BI is Microsoft's business-intelligence platform: it connects to data, cleans and models it, and turns it into interactive reports and dashboards that people can share. In 2026 it is part of Microsoft Fabric , Microsoft's all-in-one analytics platform, and it is one of the most requested tools in analyst job posts, alongside Excel, SQL and Tableau. The Power BI components. You build in Desktop, publish to the Service, and consume on the web or mobile. Component What it is Who uses it Power BI Desktop Free Windows application to build data models and reports ( .pbix ) Analysts, developers Power BI Service (app.powerbi.com) Cloud site to publish, share, schedule refresh, create dashboards and apps Analysts and report consumers Power BI Mobile iOS/Android apps to view reports and get alerts Managers on the go Power BI Report Builder Paginated, print-ready reports (invoices, statements) Report developers On-premises data gateway Secure bridge so the cloud can refresh data from your own servers Admins Power BI Report Server Self-hosted option for organisations that cannot use the cloud IT Copilot AI assistant that writes DAX, builds report pages and summarises insights (needs a Fabric/Premium capacity) Everyone Tip Report vs dashboard In Power BI a report is one or more pages of interactive visuals built on a data model. A dashboard (Service only) is a single page of pinned tiles from different reports. Many teams use reports only, or share them as an app "}, {"i": "powerbi-02", "t": "Power Query, Star Schema & Data Modelling (Adventure Cycles)", "k": "Power BI", "f": "lessons/powerbi-02-power-query-star-schema-and-data-modelling-adventure-cycles.html", "s": " Why modelling matters more than visuals Beautiful visuals on a bad model give wrong or slow answers. In Power BI the data model decides what numbers mean: how tables relate, which table filters which, and how fast DAX runs. The golden rule: model as a star schema . A star schema: one central fact table of events/numbers surrounded by descriptive dimension tables. Filters flow from dimensions (one side) to the fact (many side). Fact table Dimension table Describes Events or transactions (a sale, a click, a shipment) The \"who, what, where, when\" of those events Rows Many (millions) Fewer (thousands) Columns Keys + numeric measures (quantity, amount, cost) Keys + descriptive attributes (name, category, city) Changes Grows constantly Changes rarely Example fact_sales dim_product , dim_customer , dim_date Why a star beats one big flat table: smaller file (text stored once), faster visuals, one place to fix a name, correct filtering across several facts, and simpler DAX. A snowflake (dimensions normalised into sub-dimensions) works but adds joins; prefer a star in Power BI. The Adventure Cycles dataset A fictional bicycle company sells bikes, components, clothing and accessories in India, the UAE and Singapore. The model has one fact table and four dimensions (plus the date table): Python Copy import pandas as pd D = &quot;adventure_cycles/&quot; fact = pd.read_csv (D + &quot;fact_sales.csv&quot; , parse_dates=[ &quot;order_date&quot; ]); prod = pd.read_csv (D + &quot;dim_product."}, {"i": "powerbi-03", "t": "DAX Fundamentals: Measures, Filter Context & CALCULATE", "k": "Power BI", "f": "lessons/powerbi-03-dax-fundamentals-measures-filter-context-and-calculate.html", "s": " What is DAX? DAX (Data Analysis Expressions) is the formula language used in Power BI, Excel's Data Model and Analysis Services. It looks like Excel formulas, but it works on columns and tables and recalculates for whatever the user selects in a visual. DAX is the skill that separates a report builder from a Power BI developer. Calculated column Measure Evaluated Row by row when data is refreshed On the fly , for the current filter selection Stored in the model Yes (uses memory) No (only the formula) Result depends on slicers? No Yes Use for New attributes to slice by (Age Band, Hire Year, Profit per row) Totals, ratios, KPIs, growth Appears in Rows, columns, slicers, filters Values area, cards, tooltips The same table, two ways. A calculated column adds a value to every row. A measure returns one number computed from whichever rows are currently filtered. Tip Rule of thumb If you want to put it on an axis or slicer , make a column. If you want to put it in the Values well , make a measure. In a star schema, almost every number is a measure. DAX syntax in five minutes DAX Copy Total Sales = SUM ( fact_sales[sales_amount] ) Total Sales is the measure name (spaces allowed, no brackets when defining it). = starts the formula. SUM is a function ; fact_sales[sales_amount] is a fully qualified column (table name, then column in square brackets). Always include the table name for columns; never include it for measures: [Total Sales] . Strings use double quotes; comments use -- or /"}, {"i": "powerbi-04", "t": "DAX Time Intelligence, Ranking, Parameters & Dynamic Measures", "k": "Power BI", "f": "lessons/powerbi-04-dax-time-intelligence-ranking-parameters-and-dynamic-measure.html", "s": " What time intelligence needs Time-intelligence functions move or expand the date filter : \"this year to date\", \"the same period last year\". They only work when: You have a date table with every date (Lesson 2), it is marked as a date table , and it is related to the fact table's date column. Always use the date table's Date column inside time functions, never the fact table's date. Time intelligence changes the date filter, then CALCULATE re-evaluates the measure. SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR shifts the selected dates back exactly one year. Core measures DAX Copy Total Sales = SUM ( fact_sales[sales_amount] ) -- Cumulative totals Sales YTD = TOTALYTD ( [Total Sales], dim_date[date] ) Sales QTD = TOTALQTD ( [Total Sales], dim_date[date] ) Sales MTD = TOTALMTD ( [Total Sales], dim_date[date] ) -- Indian financial year ending 31 March: Sales FYTD = TOTALYTD ( [Total Sales], dim_date[date], &quot;31-3&quot; ) -- Previous periods Sales LY = CALCULATE ( [Total Sales], SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR ( dim_date[date] ) ) Sales PM = CALCULATE ( [Total Sales], DATEADD ( dim_date[date], - 1 , MONTH ) ) Sales PQ = CALCULATE ( [Total Sales], DATEADD ( dim_date[date], - 1 , QUARTER ) ) -- Growth YoY % = DIVIDE ( [Total Sales] - [Sales LY], [Sales LY] ) MoM % = DIVIDE ( [Total Sales] - [Sales PM], [Sales PM] ) YoY Δ = [Total Sales] - [Sales LY] Function Returns Notes TOTALYTD ( expr, dates, [year_end] ) Year-to-date total Shortcut for CALCULATE ( expr, DATESYTD ( dates ) ) DATESYTD / DATESQTD / DATESMTD The dat"}, {"i": "powerbi-05", "t": "Visuals, Filters, Drill-through, Tooltips, Buttons & Report Design", "k": "Power BI", "f": "lessons/powerbi-05-visuals-filters-drill-through-tooltips-buttons-and-report-de.html", "s": " Choosing the visual Match the visual to the question. Visual Fields Good for Watch out Card / multi-row card Values One or few headline numbers Format display units (K, M, Cr) and add a clear label Clustered bar / column Axis, Values, Legend Comparisons Sort descending, start at zero, ≤ 15 bars Stacked / 100% stacked Axis, Legend, Values Composition Only the bottom segment is easy to compare Line / area X-axis (date), Y-axis, Legend Trends Limit to ~5 lines; use a continuous date axis Line + clustered column Shared axis, column values, line values Two measures with different scales (secondary axis) Label both axes Waterfall Category, Y-axis Bridge from start to end (profit walk) Needs an ordered category Scatter / bubble X, Y, Size, Details, Play axis Relationships and outliers Add Details so each point is an entity Treemap Group, Values Hierarchical share of total Many tiny rectangles are unreadable Donut / pie Legend, Values 2–3 parts More than 4 slices = use a bar Matrix Rows, Columns, Values Pivot tables with drill and subtotals Use conditional formatting for heat maps Table Columns Exact values, detail list Keep it short; a table is not a dashboard KPI / Gauge Value, Target, Trend axis Progress to a target Gauges waste space; prefer KPI cards Funnel Stage, Values Drop-off between stages Map / Filled map Location, Size, Legend Geography Geocoding needs clean place names ( City, State, Country ) Slicer Field Filter the page Styles: list, dropdown, tiles, between (range), "}, {"i": "powerbi-06", "t": "Power BI Service, Connectivity Modes, Security & Capstone", "k": "Power BI", "f": "lessons/powerbi-06-power-bi-service-connectivity-modes-security-and-capstone.html", "s": " Connectivity modes How does Power BI get its data: copy it into the model (Import) or ask the source each time (DirectQuery)? Import copies data into Power BI's compressed in-memory model; DirectQuery sends a query to the source for every interaction. Mode Data stored in Power BI? Refresh Typical use Import Yes (compressed copy) Scheduled (up to 8/day Pro, 48/day Premium) Most reports; Excel, CSV, SQL DirectQuery No Live per interaction Large SQL databases, near-real-time dashboards Live connection No: connects to an existing semantic model (Analysis Services or a published Power BI dataset) Live One governed model shared by many reports Composite model Mix: some tables Import, some DirectQuery, plus other semantic models Both Large fact in DirectQuery, small dimensions imported (dual storage mode) Direct Lake (Fabric) Reads Delta tables in OneLake directly Automatic on data change Import-like speed with no copy; Fabric only Tip A practical rule Start with Import . Move to DirectQuery or Direct Lake only if data volume or freshness requirements force you to. Reduce the data first: only needed columns, aggregated tables, filtered date range. Publishing and the Service Step by step Publish a report In Desktop: Home → Publish → sign in with a work/school account → choose a workspace (not \"My workspace\" if you want to share). The Service now has a semantic model (the data and model) and a report (the visuals): the same objects in two items. Open the report in the browser, check "}, {"i": "tableau-01", "t": "Getting Started with Tableau: Interface, Data Connections and Your First Views", "k": "Tableau", "f": "lessons/tableau-01-getting-started-with-tableau-interface-data-connections-and.html", "s": " What is Tableau? Tableau is a visual analytics platform: you drag fields onto shelves and Tableau writes the query, aggregates the data and draws the chart. It is owned by Salesforce and, with Power BI, is one of the two BI tools most often named in Indian analyst job posts. Where Power BI leans on DAX formulas and the Microsoft stack, Tableau is known for fast visual exploration and highly customisable charts. The Tableau product family. You build in Desktop (or Public), share on Cloud/Server/Public, and prepare data in Prep. Product What it is Cost (2026) Tableau Public (desktop app + website) Free version of Desktop; workbooks are saved publicly on public.tableau.com Free Tableau Desktop Full authoring tool; saves locally, connects to databases Paid (Creator licence) Tableau Cloud Tableau's hosted service for sharing, refresh schedules and permissions Paid, per user Tableau Server The same as Cloud, but installed on your company's own servers Paid Tableau Prep Builder Visual tool to clean, join and reshape data before analysis Included with Creator Tableau Pulse / Tableau Agent AI features: metric digests in plain language and an assistant that helps write calculations and build views Cloud add-ons Tip Which one should you install? Learners should install Tableau Public (free). It has almost every authoring feature of Desktop. The two limits: workbooks are saved to your public profile (so never use confidential data) and it connects mainly to files (Excel, CSV, text, JSON"}, {"i": "tableau-02", "t": "Charts in Tableau: From Bar and Line to Maps, Dual Axis, Histograms and Pareto", "k": "Tableau", "f": "lessons/tableau-02-charts-in-tableau-from-bar-and-line-to-maps-dual-axis-histog.html", "s": " Choose the chart from the question Match the business question to the chart family. All examples below use the Superstore India data. The tables show the numbers your Tableau view should display, computed here with pandas so you can check your work. Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np df = pd.read_csv ( &quot;superstore_sales.csv&quot; , parse_dates=[ &quot;order_date&quot; ]) df[ &quot;year&quot; ] = df.order_date.dt.year print ( len (df), &quot;order lines,&quot; , df.order_id.nunique (), &quot;orders,&quot; , df.customer_id.nunique (), &quot;customers&quot; ) Output 6323 order lines, 3200 orders, 785 customers Bar charts Variant Build Use Bar Dimension on Rows, measure on Columns Ranking categories Stacked bar Add a second dimension to Color Total and composition Side-by-side bar Second dimension on Columns before the measure (or Show Me → side-by-side) Comparing subgroups directly 100% stacked Stacked bar + Quick Table Calculation → Percent of Total (compute using the colour dimension) Mix comparisons Python Copy seg = pd.crosstab (df.category, df.segment, values=df.sales, aggfunc= &quot;sum&quot; ) / 1e7 seg.round ( 2 ) Output segment Consumer Corporate Home Office category Furniture 1.70 1.03 0.75 Office Supplies 0.47 0.32 0.23 Technology 7.69 4.56 3.39 3 rows × 3 columns Stacked bar: Sales by Category, coloured by Segment (₹ crore). Line and area charts Put a continuous date (green MONTH(Order Date)) on Columns and Sales on Rows for one timeline. Put YEAR on "}, {"i": "tableau-03", "t": "Calculations in Tableau: Calculated Fields, Table Calculations and LOD Expressions", "k": "Tableau", "f": "lessons/tableau-03-calculations-in-tableau-calculated-fields-table-calculations.html", "s": " Three kinds of calculation Where each kind of calculation is computed. Create one with Analysis → Create Calculated Field (or right-click a field → Create → Calculated Field). Field names go in square brackets; comments start with // . Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np df = pd.read_csv ( &quot;superstore_sales.csv&quot; , parse_dates=[ &quot;order_date&quot; , &quot;ship_date&quot; ]) print (df[[ &quot;sales&quot; , &quot;profit&quot; , &quot;discount&quot; ]]. describe (). round ( 1 ).loc[[ &quot;mean&quot; , &quot;min&quot; , &quot;max&quot; ]]) Output sales profit discount mean 31854.1 2902.4 0.1 min 42.0 -75873.8 0.0 max 619290.0 149424.9 0.4 Row-level vs aggregate: the profit-margin trap The most common beginner bug in Tableau (and Power BI) is averaging ratios. Calculated field Formula What it means Margin (row) [Profit] / [Sales] Margin of each order line; in the view it becomes SUM or AVG of those ratios: wrong Margin (aggregate) SUM([Profit]) / SUM([Sales]) Total profit ÷ total sales for whatever the mark represents: right Python Copy by_cat = df.groupby ( &quot;category&quot; ). apply ( lambda g: pd.Series ({ &quot;aggregate margin %&quot; : g.profit.sum () / g.sales.sum () * 100 , &quot;average of row margins %&quot; : (g.profit / g.sales). mean () * 100 }), include_groups= False ) by_cat.round ( 2 ) Output aggregate margin % average of row margins % category Furniture -2.35 -2.38 Office Supplies 14.95 13.76 Technology 11.29 9.95 3 rows × 2 columns The "}, {"i": "tableau-04", "t": "Filters, Parameters, Sets, Groups and Hierarchies in Tableau", "k": "Tableau", "f": "lessons/tableau-04-filters-parameters-sets-groups-and-hierarchies-in-tableau.html", "s": " Filters Filter How Notes Dimension filter Drag a dimension to Filters → General / Wildcard / Condition / Top tabs e.g. Region = South Measure filter Drag a measure to Filters → choose aggregation → range e.g. SUM(Profit) &lt; 0 (loss-making items) Date filter Relative dates (last 3 months), range of dates, or discrete years/months Relative dates update automatically Context filter Right-click a filter → Add to Context (grey pill) Runs first; Top N and FIXED LODs respect it Data source filter Data Source page → Filters → Add Applies to every sheet; good for security and speed Extract filter When creating an extract Shrinks the extract itself Table-calc filter Filter on a table calc such as LAST()=0 or rank Hides marks without changing other calculations Show Filter (right-click the pill → Show Filter) puts an interactive control on the sheet: single value list, dropdown, multiple values, slider, wildcard match. Apply to Worksheets → All Using This Data Source / Selected Worksheets makes one filter drive several sheets on a dashboard. Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np df = pd.read_csv ( &quot;superstore_sales.csv&quot; , parse_dates=[ &quot;order_date&quot; ]) loss = df.groupby ( &quot;sub_category&quot; ). profit.sum (). sort_values () print ( &quot;Loss-making sub-categories (measure filter SUM(Profit) &lt; 0):&quot; ) print ((loss[loss &lt; 0 ] / 1e5). round ( 1 )) Output Loss-making sub-categories (measure filter SUM(Profit) &lt; 0): sub_category Tables -8.2 Boo"}, {"i": "tableau-05", "t": "Data Modelling in Tableau: Relationships, Joins, Unions, Blending, Extracts and Tableau Prep", "k": "Tableau", "f": "lessons/tableau-05-data-modelling-in-tableau-relationships-joins-unions-blendin.html", "s": " The two layers of a Tableau data source Relationships live in the logical layer; joins and unions live inside a logical table (physical layer). When you drag a second table onto the canvas, Tableau draws a relationship (a flexible \"noodle\") and asks for the matching fields. Tableau then generates the right join for each sheet, using only the tables that sheet needs and aggregating each table at its own level. The Adventure Cycles star schema The same star schema used in the Power BI lessons: one fact table of 9,000 sales lines and four dimension tables. Python Copy import pandas as pd base = &quot;adventure_cycles/&quot; fact = pd.read_csv (base + &quot;fact_sales.csv&quot; ) dims = {n: pd.read_csv (base + f&quot;dim_{n}.csv&quot; ) for n in [ &quot;product&quot; , &quot;territory&quot; , &quot;customer&quot; , &quot;date&quot; ]} print ( &quot;fact_sales:&quot; , fact.shape, list (fact.columns)) for n, d in dims.items (): print ( f&quot;dim_{n}:&quot; , d.shape, list (d.columns)[: 6 ]) Output fact_sales: (9000, 9) [&#x27;order_number&#x27;, &#x27;order_date&#x27;, &#x27;product_key&#x27;, &#x27;customer_key&#x27;, &#x27;territory_key&#x27;, &#x27;quantity&#x27;, &#x27;unit_price&#x27;, &#x27;sales_amount&#x27;, &#x27;total_cost&#x27;] dim_product: (45, 6) [&#x27;product_key&#x27;, &#x27;product&#x27;, &#x27;subcategory&#x27;, &#x27;category&#x27;, &#x27;list_price&#x27;, &#x27;standard_cost&#x27;] dim_territory: (6, 3) [&#x27;territory_key&#x27;, &#x27;territory&#x27;, &#x2"}, {"i": "tableau-06", "t": "Dashboards, Actions, Stories and Publishing in Tableau (Capstone)", "k": "Tableau", "f": "lessons/tableau-06-dashboards-actions-stories-and-publishing-in-tableau-capston.html", "s": " Plan before you build The order that keeps dashboards clear: questions first, layout last. People read a screen in a Z-pattern : top-left first. Put the KPI cards across the top, the main trend or map in the middle, and the detail (tables, Top N) at the bottom. Keep to about 5–7 views and one colour meaning per dashboard (for example orange = loss everywhere). Building the dashboard Element Use Size Fixed (e.g. 1200 × 800) for predictable layouts, Automatic or Range for flexible screens Tiled vs floating Tiled objects snap into a grid; floating objects sit on top (legends, logos). Prefer tiled. Containers Horizontal and vertical containers group objects; Distribute Contents Evenly keeps KPI cards equal Objects Text (titles), Image (logo), Web Page (not on Tableau Public), Blank (spacing), Navigation and Download buttons, Extension Show/Hide button Collapsible filter panel on a floating container Device layouts Device Preview → Phone: stack KPIs and one chart vertically KPI cards Each card is a sheet: the measure on Text , Mark type Text, a formatted label such as ₹&lt;SUM(Sales)&gt; cr , and a small YoY calculation in the second line with ▲/▼ and colour. Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np df = pd.read_csv ( &quot;superstore_sales.csv&quot; , parse_dates=[ &quot;order_date&quot; ]) y = df.groupby (df.order_date.dt.year). agg (sales=( &quot;sales&quot; , &quot;sum&quot; ), profit=( &quot;profit&quot; , &quot;sum&quot; ), orders=( &quot;order_id&quot; , &quot;nunique&"}, {"i": "ai-analytics-01", "t": "Generative AI for Analysts: How It Works, Prompting & AI vs GenAI vs Agentic AI", "k": "AI Analytics", "f": "lessons/ai-analytics-01-generative-ai-for-analysts-how-it-works-prompting-and-ai-vs.html", "s": " The AI family tree How the terms relate. Each ring is a subset of the one before it. Agentic AI uses generative models as its brain but adds goals, tools and loops. Term What it means Example AI Machines doing tasks that normally need human intelligence Rule-based chess engine, spam filter Machine learning (ML) Systems that learn patterns from data instead of following hand-written rules Predict loan default from past loans Deep learning ML with many-layered neural networks Image recognition, speech-to-text Generative AI (GenAI) Models that create new content (text, images, code, audio) ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, Copilot, Midjourney LLM Large language model: the GenAI engine for text and code GPT, Claude, Gemini, Llama Agentic AI An LLM plus goals, tools and a loop : it plans, calls tools (run SQL, search, edit files), checks results and continues until the goal is met An analyst agent that explores a database and writes the report AGI Hypothetical AI at human level across tasks Not here yet; ignore the hype AI vs GenAI vs agentic AI Traditional ML Generative AI Agentic AI Output A prediction (number, label) New content (text, code, image) A completed multi-step task Input Structured data A prompt (text, files, images) A goal in plain language Control You train and deploy a model You ask once, get one answer The system decides the next step Example \"Will this customer churn?\" → 0.82 \"Summarise these reviews\" → paragraph \"Find why sales fell in Q3 and draft a memo\" → runs queri"}, {"i": "ai-analytics-02", "t": "AI with SQL: Text-to-SQL, Docker, Metabase & MinusX", "k": "AI Analytics", "f": "lessons/ai-analytics-02-ai-with-sql-text-to-sql-docker-metabase-and-minusx.html", "s": " What is text-to-SQL? Text-to-SQL turns a business question in plain language into a SQL query: \"Which region had the highest profit in 2025?\" → SELECT region, SUM(profit) … . LLMs are good at SQL syntax, but correct SQL for your database needs your context. Text-to-SQL pipeline. Quality depends mostly on the context you supply, not on the model alone. What goes wrong without context Problem Example Prevention Wrong tables/columns Uses customer_name in orders , where it does not exist Provide the schema (tables, columns, types, keys) Wrong grain / fan-out Joins orders to items and then COUNT(*) for \"orders\" State the grain: \"one row per order line\"; add \"count distinct order_id\" rule Wrong definitions \"Active customer\", \"net sales\", \"this year\" Provide a metric dictionary Dialect mismatch LIMIT instead of TOP in SQL Server Name the database and version Dates Uses YEAR(date) = 2025 (slow) or the wrong fiscal year State calendar vs fiscal year and preferred patterns Hallucinated values WHERE region = 'Northern' when data says North Provide sample values for categorical columns Unsafe actions DELETE / DROP suggestions Read-only connection; review before running A schema prompt that works Text Copy You are a senior analytics engineer. Write SQL for Microsoft SQL Server 2022. SCHEMA (all in dbo): customers(customer_id PK, customer_name, segment, city, state, region) - segment in (&#x27;Consumer&#x27;,&#x27;Corporate&#x27;,&#x27;Home Office&#x27;); region in (&#x27;North&#x27;,&#x2"}, {"i": "ai-analytics-03", "t": "AI in Excel & Office: Copilot, Gemini, Claude, Python in Excel & Agents", "k": "AI Analytics", "f": "lessons/ai-analytics-03-ai-in-excel-and-office-copilot-gemini-claude-python-in-excel.html", "s": " The AI-in-Excel landscape Four ways AI shows up in spreadsheets. Step 0: make your workbook AI-ready AI assistants can only reason about data they can see and parse . The same rules that make pivot tables work (Excel Lesson 1) make AI work: Convert data to an Excel Table ( Ctrl + T ) with a clear name ( tblSales ) and one header row. Descriptive column names ( order_date , sales_inr ) instead of Col1 ; add units. No merged cells, blank rows or notes inside the data; keep totals outside. One sheet per dataset; a small Data dictionary sheet that defines columns and metrics (AI reads it too). Remove or mask sensitive columns before connecting an AI service. Save to OneDrive/SharePoint (Copilot needs cloud-saved files with AutoSave on). Copilot in Excel Needs a Microsoft 365 Copilot licence and your data formatted as a Table or range with headers. Task Example prompt Explain \"Explain what column H does and how it is calculated\" Formulas \"Add a column that shows profit margin %\" / \"Write a formula that looks up the manager's name from the Managers table\" Insights \"Show me insights about sales by region\" → Copilot adds pivot tables and charts Highlight \"Highlight rows where discount &gt; 20% and profit is negative\" (conditional formatting) Sort / filter \"Sort by sales descending and filter to Technology\" Pivot &amp; charts \"Create a pivot table of sales by category and year with YoY %\" Clean up \"Fix the inconsistent city names\" / \"Split full name into first and last name\" Analyse "}, {"i": "ai-analytics-04", "t": "The AI-Assisted Analyst Workflow, Agents & Governance (Capstone)", "k": "AI Analytics", "f": "lessons/ai-analytics-04-the-ai-assisted-analyst-workflow-agents-and-governance-capst.html", "s": " The AI-assisted analyst workflow Seven steps. AI accelerates several of them, but the analyst owns the first and the last two. Step What you do Where AI helps Your responsibility 1. Frame Decision, audience, metric definitions, constraints Brainstorm questions, critique your framing Own the decision question 2. Brief Schema, definitions, sample rows, rules Turn notes into a clean brief No sensitive data; correct definitions 3. Analyse Profile, clean, aggregate, model Draft SQL/Python, explain code, suggest checks Run it; read it 4. Verify Reconcile totals, test edge cases, second method Write test queries, find anomalies Sign off the numbers 5. Visualise Choose and label charts Suggest chart types, write plotting code Honest, readable charts 6. Write Insight, caveat, action Draft commentary, simplify language Every claim traceable to a number 7. Recommend Action, owner, metric, date Pre-mortem: \"how could this fail?\" Business judgement Case: why do telecom customers leave? Case study Brief A telecom company has 3,000 subscribers in telecom_churn.csv (tenure, contract type, internet service, payment method, charges, support calls, churn Yes/No). The retention head asks: Who is most likely to churn, and what should we do about it? We will follow the workflow and use AI-style assistance where it helps, checking everything. Steps 1–2: frame and brief Text Copy Decision: choose which customers get a retention offer next quarter (budget: 300 offers). Metric: churn rate = churners "}, {"i": "python-01", "t": "Introduction to Python: Why, Install & Jupyter Notebooks", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-01-introduction-to-python-why-install-and-jupyter-notebooks.html", "s": " What is programming? A program is a set of precise instructions a computer follows. Computers only understand machine code (0s and 1s), so we write instructions in a programming language that humans can read, and a translator turns them into something the machine can run. Languages sit on levels. High-level languages like Python are close to human language; low-level ones are close to the machine. Compiled vs interpreted Compiled (C, C++, Rust, Go) Interpreted (Python, JavaScript, R) How it runs Whole program translated to machine code before running Translated and executed line by line while running Speed Usually faster at run time Slower per operation, much faster to develop Errors Many caught before running Some appear only when that line runs Output A separate executable file Source file runs under an interpreter Python is usually called interpreted . In reality CPython (the standard version) first compiles your code to bytecode ( .pyc files) and then a virtual machine executes it. Python code that needs speed calls libraries written in C (NumPy, pandas), so data work is fast anyway. Why Python for data? The Python data ecosystem. You will learn each of these libraries in this track. Property of Python Meaning Readable Indentation and English-like keywords; fewer lines than most languages Interpreted, dynamically typed No compile step; variables take the type of the value they hold High level Memory is managed for you (garbage collection) Multi-paradigm Procedural, objec"}, {"i": "python-02", "t": "Variables, Data Types, Type Conversion, print(), f-strings & input()", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-02-variables-data-types-type-conversion-print-f-strings-and-inp.html", "s": " Variables A variable is a name that refers to a value stored in memory. You create it by assigning with = : no declaration, no type needed. Python Copy price = 1250 # an int tax_rate = 0.18 # a float product = &quot;Laptop&quot; # a str in_stock = True # a bool total = price * ( 1 + tax_rate) print (product, &quot;costs&quot; , total) Output Laptop costs 1475.0 A variable is a label attached to an object. Re-assigning moves the label; it does not change the old object. Naming rules and conventions Rule Example Letters, digits and _ only; cannot start with a digit sales_2025 ✓, 2025_sales ✗ Case-sensitive Total and total are different Cannot be a keyword ( if , for , class , None , True …) class ✗, class_name ✓ Convention : snake_case for variables and functions, PascalCase for classes, UPPER_CASE for constants monthly_income , CustomerOrder , GST_RATE Use meaningful names avg_order_value not aov2 or x Avoid shadowing built-ins Do not name variables list , sum , max , str , id , input Python Copy a = b = c = 0 # chained assignment x, y, z = 1 , 2.5 , &quot;three&quot; # multiple assignment (tuple unpacking) x, y = y, x # swap without a temp variable print (a, b, c, x, y, z) Output 0 0 0 2.5 1 three Data types Everything in Python is an object with a type . Check it with type() . Type Name Example Notes int Integer 42 , -7 , 1_000_000 Unlimited size float Decimal number 3.14 , 2e-3 64-bit binary floating point str Text \"Pune\" , 'Pune' Immutable sequence of characters bool Bool"}, {"i": "python-03", "t": "Operators, Strings, Indexing, Slicing & String Methods", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-03-operators-strings-indexing-slicing-and-string-methods.html", "s": " Operators Arithmetic Operator Meaning Example Result + - * Add, subtract, multiply 7 * 3 21 / True division (always float) 7 / 2 3.5 // Floor division 7 // 2 , -7 // 2 3, −4 % Remainder (modulo) 7 % 2 , -7 % 3 1, 2 ** Power 2 ** 10 1024 Python Copy print ( 7 / 2 , 7 // 2 , - 7 // 2 , 7 % 2 , - 7 % 3 , 2 ** 10 , 9 ** 0.5 ) print ( divmod ( 17 , 5 )) # (quotient, remainder) in one call hours = 135 ; print ( f&quot;{hours // 60} h {hours % 60} min&quot; ) # split minutes into hours and minutes print ( 10 % 3 == 1 , 12 % 2 == 0 ) # test odd/even and divisibility Output 3.5 3 -4 1 2 1024 3.0 (3, 2) 2 h 15 min True True Comparison and logical Python Copy x = 15 print (x &gt; 10 , x == 15 , x != 15 , 10 &lt; x &lt;= 20 ) # chained comparison: 10 &lt; x and x &lt;= 20 print (x &gt; 10 and x &lt; 20 , x &lt; 5 or x &gt; 12 , not x &gt; 10 ) print ( &quot;apple&quot; &lt; &quot;banana&quot; , &quot;Zebra&quot; &lt; &quot;apple&quot; ) # strings compare by character codes: uppercase before lowercase Output True True False True True True False True True Short-circuit evaluation : A and B stops at A if A is falsy; A or B stops at A if A is truthy. They return the deciding operand , not just True/False: Python Copy print ( 0 or &quot;default&quot; , &quot;&quot; or &quot;n/a&quot; , &quot;x&quot; and &quot;y&quot; , None and 1 ) name = &quot;&quot; ; print (name or &quot;Anonymous&quot; ) # a common idiom for default values print ( 5 &gt; 3 and print ( &quot;evaluated&quot; ) is None ) # "}, {"i": "python-04", "t": "Control Flow: if, elif, else, Nested Conditions & match", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-04-control-flow-if-elif-else-nested-conditions-and-match.html", "s": " Decisions and indentation A program normally runs line by line. Control flow lets it choose between paths. In Python, a colon : starts a block and indentation (4 spaces by convention) says which lines belong to it. An if statement tests a condition. If it is True the indented block runs; otherwise Python moves on (or runs else). Python Copy marks = 82 if marks &gt;= 40 : print ( &quot;Pass&quot; ) print ( &quot;Well done!&quot; ) print ( &quot;This line always runs&quot; ) Output Pass Well done! This line always runs Python Copy if True : print ( &quot;not indented&quot; ) # IndentationError: expected an indented block after &#x27;if&#x27; statement Common mistakes Indentation rules Use 4 spaces (never mix tabs and spaces). Everything in the same block has the same indentation . A missing colon or wrong indentation causes SyntaxError / IndentationError . if / elif / else Python Copy marks = 82 if marks &gt;= 90 : grade = &quot;A+&quot; elif marks &gt;= 75 : grade = &quot;A&quot; elif marks &gt;= 60 : grade = &quot;B&quot; elif marks &gt;= 40 : grade = &quot;C&quot; else : grade = &quot;Fail&quot; print (marks, &quot;-&gt;&quot; , grade) Output 82 -&gt; A Python checks the conditions top to bottom and runs the first one that is true, then skips the rest. This is Excel's nested IF / IFS and SQL's CASE WHEN (Excel Lesson 5, SQL Lesson 7). Common mistakes Order matters If you test marks &gt;= 40 first, an 82 gets \"C\" because the first true branch wins. Put the most specific / st"}, {"i": "python-05", "t": "Loops: while, for, range, break, continue & Patterns", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-05-loops-while-for-range-break-continue-and-patterns.html", "s": " Why loops? A loop repeats a block of code. Processing 10,000 rows, trying each file in a folder, retrying a failed request, or checking each number in a list are all loops. Two kinds of loop. for repeats over a known sequence; while repeats as long as a condition stays true. The while loop Python Copy count = 1 while count &lt;= 5 : print ( &quot;Count:&quot; , count) count += 1 # without this line the loop would never end! print ( &quot;Done&quot; ) Output Count: 1 Count: 2 Count: 3 Count: 4 Count: 5 Done Every while loop needs: (1) a starting value, (2) a condition, (3) a change inside the loop that eventually makes the condition False. Python Copy # Compound growth: how many years until 1,00,000 doubles at 8%? amount, years = 100000 , 0 while amount &lt; 200000 : amount *= 1.08 years += 1 print ( f&quot;{years} years -&gt; {amount:,.0f}&quot; ) # the &#x27;rule of 72&#x27;: 72 / 8 = 9 years Output 10 years -&gt; 215,892 while True with break Use an \"infinite\" loop that you leave deliberately when the input is right: Python Copy attempts = [ &quot;abc&quot; , &quot;-4&quot; , &quot;12&quot; ] # simulated user answers i = 0 while True : answer = attempts[i]; i += 1 if answer.isdigit () and int (answer) &gt; 0 : print ( &quot;Accepted:&quot; , answer); break print ( &quot;Invalid:&quot; , answer) Output Invalid: abc Invalid: -4 Accepted: 12 The for loop for item in sequence: takes each item in turn. Python Copy for city in [ &quot;Pune&quot; , &quot;Delhi&quot; , &quot;Kochi"}, {"i": "python-06", "t": "Lists: Creating, Slicing, Methods, Sorting & Comprehensions", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-06-lists-creating-slicing-methods-sorting-and-comprehensions.html", "s": " What is a list? A list is an ordered, mutable collection of items written in square brackets. Items can be of any type, and may repeat. Python Copy cities = [ &quot;Pune&quot; , &quot;Delhi&quot; , &quot;Kochi&quot; , &quot;Delhi&quot; ] mixed = [ 101 , &quot;Kavya&quot; , 97000.5 , True , None ] empty = [] print (cities, len (cities), type (cities).__name__) print ( list ( &quot;data&quot; ), list ( range ( 5 )), list (( 1 , 2 , 3 )), [ 0 ] * 5 ) Output [&#x27;Pune&#x27;, &#x27;Delhi&#x27;, &#x27;Kochi&#x27;, &#x27;Delhi&#x27;] 4 list [&#x27;d&#x27;, &#x27;a&#x27;, &#x27;t&#x27;, &#x27;a&#x27;] [0, 1, 2, 3, 4] [1, 2, 3] [0, 0, 0, 0, 0] A list is a row of numbered boxes. Items are referenced by position; the list itself can grow, shrink and change. Indexing and slicing Works exactly like strings (Lesson 3), but lists can also be changed . Python Copy nums = [ 10 , 20 , 30 , 40 , 50 , 60 ] print (nums[ 0 ], nums[- 1 ], nums[ 1 : 4 ], nums[: 2 ], nums[- 2 :], nums[:: 2 ], nums[::- 1 ]) nums[ 0 ] = 99 # change one item nums[ 1 : 3 ] = [ 21 , 31 , 32 ] # replace a slice (the length may change) print (nums) print ( 3 in nums, 99 in nums, nums.count ( 40 ), nums.index ( 40 )) Output 10 60 [20, 30, 40] [10, 20] [50, 60] [10, 30, 50] [60, 50, 40, 30, 20, 10] [99, 21, 31, 32, 40, 50, 60] False True 1 4 Adding and removing items Method What it does Example Returns append(x) Add one item at the end a.append(5) None extend(iterable) or += Add all items from another sequence a.extend([6,"}, {"i": "python-07", "t": "Tuples & Sets: Immutable Records, Unpacking and Set Algebra", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-07-tuples-and-sets-immutable-records-unpacking-and-set-algebra.html", "s": " Tuples A tuple is an ordered, immutable sequence written with commas (brackets are usually optional). Python Copy point = ( 28.61 , 77.20 ) single = ( 5 ,) # the trailing comma makes a one-item tuple empty = () rec = &quot;E101&quot; , &quot;Kavya Iyer&quot; , &quot;HR&quot; , 97000 # packing: no brackets needed print (point, type (single).__name__, type (( 5 )).__name__, rec[ 1 ], rec[- 1 ], rec[ 1 : 3 ], len (rec)) print ( rec.count ( &quot;HR&quot; ), rec.index ( &quot;HR&quot; ), ( 1 , 2 ) + ( 3 ,), ( 0 ,) * 3 , 5 in ( 1 , 5 , 9 )) try : rec[ 0 ] = &quot;E102&quot; except TypeError as e: print ( &quot;Error:&quot; , e) Output (28.61, 77.2) tuple int Kavya Iyer 97000 (&#x27;Kavya Iyer&#x27;, &#x27;HR&#x27;) 4 1 2 (1, 2, 3) (0, 0, 0) True Error: &#x27;tuple&#x27; object does not support item assignment Unpacking Python Copy emp_id, name, dept, salary = rec # one name per item a, b = 1 , 2 ; a, b = b, a # swap first, *others = ( 10 , 20 , 30 , 40 ) # * collects the rest into a list *head, last = ( 10 , 20 , 30 , 40 ) x, (y, z) = 1 , ( 2 , 3 ) # nested unpacking _, dept_only, *_ = rec # _ means &quot;ignore&quot; print (emp_id, name, a, b, first, others, head, last, x, y, z, dept_only) for city, sales in [( &quot;Pune&quot; , 120 ), ( &quot;Delhi&quot; , 95 )]: # unpack in loops print (city, sales) Output E101 Kavya Iyer 2 1 10 [20, 30, 40] [10, 20, 30] 40 1 2 3 Kavya Iyer Pune 120 Delhi 95 Why tuples? Use Example Fixed records (lat, lon) , (year, month) , a row from a datab"}, {"i": "python-08", "t": "Dictionaries: Key-Value Data, Counting and Grouping", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-08-dictionaries-key-value-data-counting-and-grouping.html", "s": " What is a dictionary? A dictionary ( dict ) maps keys to values : look up by name instead of by position. It keeps insertion order (Python 3.7+), and lookups are O(1) on average. A dictionary is a set of key → value pairs. The key is hashed, so the lookup does not scan the data. Python Copy emp = { &quot;name&quot; : &quot;Kavya Iyer&quot; , &quot;dept&quot; : &quot;HR&quot; , &quot;salary&quot; : 97000 } print (emp[ &quot;name&quot; ], emp.get ( &quot;dept&quot; ), emp.get ( &quot;city&quot; ), emp.get ( &quot;city&quot; , &quot;unknown&quot; ), len (emp), &quot;salary&quot; in emp) try : emp[ &quot;city&quot; ] except KeyError as e: print ( &quot;KeyError:&quot; , e) Output Kavya Iyer HR None unknown 3 True KeyError: &#x27;city&#x27; d[key] raises KeyError when missing; d.get(key, default) never does. Changing a dictionary Python Copy emp[ &quot;city&quot; ] = &quot;Pune&quot; # add emp[ &quot;salary&quot; ] += 3000 # update emp.update ({ &quot;dept&quot; : &quot;Finance&quot; , &quot;grade&quot; : &quot;M2&quot; }) removed = emp.pop ( &quot;grade&quot; ) # remove and return del emp[ &quot;city&quot; ] print (emp, removed) print ( emp.setdefault ( &quot;level&quot; , &quot;L3&quot; ), emp.setdefault ( &quot;dept&quot; , &quot;X&quot; ), emp) # set only if missing Output {&#x27;name&#x27;: &#x27;Kavya Iyer&#x27;, &#x27;dept&#x27;: &#x27;Finance&#x27;, &#x27;salary&#x27;: 100000} M2 L3 Finance {&#x27;name&#x27;: &#x27;Kavya Iyer&#x27;, &#x27;dept&#x27;: &#x27;Finance&#x27;, "}, {"i": "python-09", "t": "Functions: Reusable Code, Arguments, Scope and Docstrings", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-09-functions-reusable-code-arguments-scope-and-docstrings.html", "s": " Why functions? A function is a named, reusable block of code. It removes copy-paste, makes code testable, and lets you give a name to an idea ( clean_phone , margin_pct ). A function takes inputs (arguments), runs its body, and gives back an output (return value). Python Copy def greet (name): return f&quot;Hello, {name}!&quot; print ( greet ( &quot;Asha&quot; ), greet ( &quot;Ravi&quot; )) Output Hello, Asha! Hello, Ravi! Parameters and arguments Python Copy def margin_pct (profit, sales, decimals= 1 ): &quot;&quot;&quot;Profit as a percentage of sales.&quot;&quot;&quot; return round ( 100 * profit / sales, decimals) print ( margin_pct ( 25 , 200 )) # positional print ( margin_pct (sales= 200 , profit= 25 )) # keyword: order does not matter print ( margin_pct ( 25 , 200 , decimals= 3 )) # default overridden Output 12.5 12.5 12.5 Kind Example Notes Positional f(1, 2) Matched by order Keyword f(b=2, a=1) Matched by name; clearer Default def f(a, b=10) Optional argument; defaults go last *args def f(*args) Extra positional arguments as a tuple **kwargs def f(**kwargs) Extra keyword arguments as a dict Keyword-only def f(a, *, b) b must be named Python Copy def total (*nums): return sum (nums) def describe (**info): return &quot;, &quot; . join ( f&quot;{k}={v}&quot; for k, v in info.items ()) def report (title, *values, sep= &quot; | &quot; , **opts): return title + &quot;: &quot; + sep.join ( map (str, values)) + f&quot; {opts}&quot; print ( total ( 1 , 2 , 3 ), total (), des"}, {"i": "python-10", "t": "Comprehensions, Lambda, map/filter and Generators", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-10-comprehensions-lambda-map-filter-and-generators.html", "s": " List comprehensions A comprehension builds a collection from another in one expression: [expression for item in iterable if condition] . A comprehension reads like the sentence \"give me X for each item, if the condition holds\". Python Copy nums = [ 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 ] squares = [n * n for n in nums] evens = [n for n in nums if n % 2 == 0 ] labels = [ &quot;even&quot; if n % 2 == 0 else &quot;odd&quot; for n in nums] # if/else goes BEFORE the for loop_version = [] for n in nums: if n % 2 == 0 : loop_version.append (n * n) print (squares, evens, labels, [n * n for n in nums if n % 2 == 0 ] == loop_version) Output [1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36] [2, 4, 6] [&#x27;odd&#x27;, &#x27;even&#x27;, &#x27;odd&#x27;, &#x27;even&#x27;, &#x27;odd&#x27;, &#x27;even&#x27;] True Python Copy words = [ &quot; Pune &quot; , &quot;delhi&quot; , &quot; KOCHI&quot; , &quot;goa &quot; ] print ([ w.strip (). title () for w in words]) print ([(x, y) for x in ( 1 , 2 ) for y in ( &quot;a&quot; , &quot;b&quot; )]) # two loops matrix = [[ 1 , 2 , 3 ], [ 4 , 5 , 6 ]] print ([v for row in matrix for v in row], [ list (col) for col in zip (*matrix)]) # flatten, transpose Output [&#x27;Pune&#x27;, &#x27;Delhi&#x27;, &#x27;Kochi&#x27;, &#x27;Goa&#x27;] [(1, &#x27;a&#x27;), (1, &#x27;b&#x27;), (2, &#x27;a&#x27;), (2, &#x27;b&#x27;)] [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6] [[1, 4], [2, 5], [3, 6]] Set and dictionary comprehensions Python Copy print ({ len (w) for w in [ &quot;tea&quot; , &quot;cup&quot; , &quot;mug&quot; , &quot;plate&qu"}, {"i": "python-11", "t": "Object-Oriented Python: Classes, Objects, Inheritance and Dataclasses", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-11-object-oriented-python-classes-objects-inheritance-and-datac.html", "s": " Why classes? A class bundles data (attributes) and behaviour (methods) into one unit. An object is one instance of a class. Libraries you already use are classes: a pandas DataFrame has data (rows) and methods ( .groupby() ). A class is the blueprint; objects are the things built from it, each with its own data. Python Copy class Account: bank = &quot;SkillBank&quot; # class attribute: shared by all objects def __init__ (self, owner, balance= 0 ): # constructor: runs when you create an object self.owner = owner # instance attributes: belong to this object self.balance = balance def deposit (self, amount): if amount &lt;= 0 : raise ValueError ( &quot;amount must be positive&quot; ) self.balance += amount return self.balance def withdraw (self, amount): if amount &gt; self.balance: raise ValueError ( &quot;insufficient funds&quot; ) self.balance -= amount return self.balance a, b = Account ( &quot;Asha&quot; , 500 ), Account ( &quot;Ravi&quot; , 900 ) a.deposit ( 250 ); b.withdraw ( 100 ) print (a.owner, a.balance, b.owner, b.balance, Account.bank, a.bank) try : a.withdraw ( 5000 ) except ValueError as e: print ( &quot;Error:&quot; , e) Output Asha 750 Ravi 800 SkillBank SkillBank Error: insufficient funds self is the object the method was called on. Python passes it automatically: a.deposit(250) is Account.deposit(a, 250) . Special (dunder) methods Methods with double underscores customise how objects behave with print , == , len , + and more. Python Copy class Account: def _"}, {"i": "python-12", "t": "Files, Exceptions, JSON and CSV", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-12-files-exceptions-json-and-csv.html", "s": " Files and paths Use pathlib.Path for paths (works on Windows, macOS and Linux) and a with block to open files: the file is closed automatically, even if an error happens. Python Copy from pathlib import Path folder = Path ( &quot;demo_files&quot; ); folder.mkdir (exist_ok= True ) notes = folder / &quot;notes.txt&quot; # / joins path parts with open (notes, &quot;w&quot; , encoding= &quot;utf-8&quot; ) as f: # &quot;w&quot; overwrites, &quot;a&quot; appends, &quot;r&quot; reads (default) f.write ( &quot;Line one\\n&quot; ) f.writelines ([ &quot;Line two\\n&quot; , &quot;Line three\\n&quot; ]) with open (notes, &quot;a&quot; , encoding= &quot;utf-8&quot; ) as f: print ( &quot;Line four&quot; , file=f) print ( notes.read_text (encoding= &quot;utf-8&quot; )) print ( notes.exists (), notes.name, notes.stem, notes.suffix, notes.stat ().st_size, &quot;bytes&quot; ) Output Line one Line two Line three Line four True notes.txt notes .txt 39 bytes Python Copy with open (notes, encoding= &quot;utf-8&quot; ) as f: for i, line in enumerate (f, 1 ): # iterate line by line: constant memory for huge files print (i, line.rstrip ( &quot;\\n&quot; )) with open (notes, encoding= &quot;utf-8&quot; ) as f: lines = f.read (). splitlines () print (lines, sorted (p.name for p in folder.glob ( &quot;*.txt&quot; ))) Output 1 Line one 2 Line two 3 Line three 4 Line four [&#x27;Line one&#x27;, &#x27;Line two&#x27;, &#x27;Line three&#x27;, &#x27;Line four&#x27;] [&#x27;notes.txt&#x27;] Mode Meaning \"r\" Read "}, {"i": "python-13", "t": "Modules, Packages, Virtual Environments and Git", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-13-modules-packages-virtual-environments-and-git.html", "s": " Modules A module is any .py file. Importing it gives you its functions, classes and variables. Python Copy import math # whole module, use with prefix from statistics import mean, median # specific names import datetime as dt # alias from collections import Counter as C print ( math.sqrt ( 144 ), math.pi, mean ([ 3 , 5 , 10 ]), median ([ 3 , 5 , 10 ]), dt.date ( 2025 , 4 , 1 ). strftime ( &quot;%d %b %Y&quot; ), C ( &quot;aab&quot; )) print ( math.ceil ( 4.2 ), math.floor ( 4.8 ), math.log10 ( 1000 ), round ( math.exp ( 1 ), 4 ), math.comb ( 5 , 2 )) Output 12.0 3.141592653589793 6 5 01 Apr 2025 Counter({&#x27;a&#x27;: 2, &#x27;b&#x27;: 1}) 5 4 3.0 2.7183 10 Style When import pandas as pd Standard aliases ( np , pd , plt , sns ) from pathlib import Path A few names used often from module import * Avoid : hides where names come from Writing your own module Python Copy from pathlib import Path Path ( &quot;metrics.py&quot; ). write_text ( &#x27;&#x27;&#x27;&quot;&quot;&quot;Reusable business metrics.&quot;&quot;&quot; GST = 0.18 def margin_pct(profit, sales): return round(100 * profit / sales, 1) if sales else None def with_gst(amount): return round(amount * (1 + GST), 2) if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;: # runs only when executed directly, not when imported print(&quot;self-test:&quot;, margin_pct(25, 200)) &#x27;&#x27;&#x27; , encoding= &quot;utf-8&quot; ) import importlib, sys if &quot;&quot; not in sys.path: sys.path.insert ( 0 , &quot;&quot; ) # notebooks and scripts a"}, {"i": "python-14", "t": "Debugging, Testing, Regular Expressions and Code Quality", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-14-debugging-testing-regular-expressions-and-code-quality.html", "s": " Reading a traceback Read a traceback from the bottom up : the last line names the error, the lines above show where it happened. Python Copy def average (values): return sum (values) / len (values) def report (data): return f&quot;Average: {average(data[&#x27;scores&#x27;]):.1f}&quot; print ( report ({ &quot;scores&quot; : []})) Output ZeroDivisionError: division by zero The last line says ZeroDivisionError ; the frame above points at average , called from report . The fix is a guard for empty input: Python Copy def average (values): return sum (values) / len (values) if values else None print ( average ([]), average ([ 4 , 6 ])) Output None 5.0 A debugging routine A systematic debugging loop beats random changes. Technique How Print / f-string debugging print(f\"{i=} {total=}\") shows names and values breakpoint() Pauses and opens the pdb debugger at that line IDE debugger VS Code / PyCharm: breakpoints, step over/into, watch variables Jupyter %debug Post-mortem debugging after an error in a cell Rubber-duck Explain the code line by line to someone (or an AI) Bisect Comment out halves of the code to find the failing part Python Copy total = 0 for i, v in enumerate ([ 5 , 10 , 15 ]): total += v print ( f&quot;{i=} {v=} {total=}&quot; ) # the = specifier prints the name and the value Output i=0 v=5 total=5 i=1 v=10 total=15 i=2 v=15 total=30 pdb commands: n (next line), s (step into), c (continue), p var (print), l (list code), q (quit). Testing A test is code that checks other"}, {"i": "python-15", "t": "NumPy: Fast Arrays, Vectorisation and Broadcasting", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-15-numpy-fast-arrays-vectorisation-and-broadcasting.html", "s": " Why NumPy? A Python list holds pointers to separate objects; a NumPy array stores numbers of one type in one contiguous block of memory and runs operations in compiled C. The result: code that is shorter and usually many times faster (the gap grows with heavier maths). Python Copy import numpy as np, time n = 1_000_000 py = list ( range (n)); arr = np.arange (n) t0 = time.perf_counter (); sq_list = [x * x for x in py]; t1 = time.perf_counter (); sq_arr = arr * arr; t2 = time.perf_counter () print ( f&quot;list comprehension {1000*(t1-t0):.1f} ms | numpy {1000*(t2-t1):.1f} ms | same result: {sq_list[-1] == sq_arr[-1]}&quot; ) print (np.__version__) Output list comprehension 44.7 ms | numpy 34.8 ms | same result: True 2.5.3 Creating arrays Python Copy a = np.array ([ 3 , 1 , 4 , 1 , 5 ]) m = np.array ([[ 1 , 2 , 3 ], [ 4 , 5 , 6 ]]) print (a, a.dtype, a.shape, a.ndim, a.size) print (m, m.shape, m.ndim, m.dtype) print ( np.zeros ( 3 ), np.ones (( 2 , 2 )), np.full ( 3 , 7 ), np.eye ( 2 )) print ( np.arange ( 0 , 10 , 2.5 ), np.linspace ( 0 , 1 , 5 ), np.array ([ 1 , 2.5 ]).dtype, np.array ([ 1 , 2 ], dtype=np.float32).dtype) Output [3 1 4 1 5] int64 (5,) 1 5 [[1 2 3] [4 5 6]] (2, 3) 2 int64 [0. 0. 0.] [[1. 1.] [1. 1.]] [7 7 7] [[1. 0.] [0. 1.]] [0. 2.5 5. 7.5] [0. 0.25 0.5 0.75 1. ] float64 float32 Shapes of 1-D, 2-D and 3-D arrays. axis 0 runs down the rows, axis 1 across the columns. All items share one dtype ( int64 , float64 , bool , &lt;U5 strings…). Mixing ints and floats"}, {"i": "python-16", "t": "pandas I: Series, DataFrames, Reading Data, Selecting and Filtering", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-16-pandas-i-series-dataframes-reading-data-selecting-and-filter.html", "s": " Series and DataFrame pandas gives Python spreadsheet-like tables. A Series is one labelled column; a DataFrame is a table of Series sharing one index (row labels). A DataFrame is a set of Series (columns) that share an index (row labels). Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np pd.set_option ( &quot;display.max_columns&quot; , 20 ); pd.set_option ( &quot;display.width&quot; , 160 ) # show wide tables in full s = pd.Series ([ 120 , 95 , 140 ], index=[ &quot;North&quot; , &quot;South&quot; , &quot;East&quot; ], name= &quot;sales&quot; ) print (s, s[ &quot;South&quot; ], s.max (), s.idxmax (), sep= &quot;\\n&quot; ) df = pd.DataFrame ({ &quot;city&quot; : [ &quot;Pune&quot; , &quot;Delhi&quot; , &quot;Kochi&quot; ], &quot;sales&quot; : [ 120 , 95 , 140 ], &quot;target&quot; : [ 100 , 110 , 130 ]}) df[ &quot;hit&quot; ] = df[ &quot;sales&quot; ] &gt;= df[ &quot;target&quot; ] print (df) print (df.shape, list (df.columns), df.index, df.dtypes.to_dict (), pd.__version__) Output North 120 South 95 East 140 Name: sales, dtype: int64 95 140 East city sales target hit 0 Pune 120 100 True 1 Delhi 95 110 False 2 Kochi 140 130 True (3, 4) [&#x27;city&#x27;, &#x27;sales&#x27;, &#x27;target&#x27;, &#x27;hit&#x27;] RangeIndex(start=0, stop=3, step=1) {&#x27;city&#x27;: dtype(&#x27;O&#x27;), &#x27;sales&#x27;: dtype(&#x27;int64&#x27;), &#x27;target&#x27;: dtype(&#x27;int64&#x27;), &#x27;hit&#x27;: dtype(&#x27;bool&#x27;)} 2.3.3 Reading data Python Copy s = pd.read_csv ( &quot;superstore_"}, {"i": "python-17", "t": "pandas II: Data Cleaning: Missing Values, Types, Duplicates, Text and Outliers", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-17-pandas-ii-data-cleaning-missing-values-types-duplicates-text.html", "s": " Why cleaning matters Analysts often say 60–80% of project time goes into preparing data. Wrong types, gaps, duplicates and typos silently break totals, joins and models: garbage in, garbage out . A repeatable cleaning workflow. We will clean a deliberately messy extract: Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np pd.set_option ( &quot;display.max_columns&quot; , 20 ); pd.set_option ( &quot;display.width&quot; , 160 ) raw = pd.DataFrame ({ &quot;order_id&quot; : [ &quot;A1&quot; , &quot;A2&quot; , &quot;A3&quot; , &quot;A3&quot; , &quot;A4&quot; , &quot;A5&quot; , &quot;A6&quot; , &quot;A7&quot; , &quot;A8&quot; ], &quot;city&quot; : [ &quot; pune&quot; , &quot;Pune &quot; , &quot;DELHI&quot; , &quot;DELHI&quot; , &quot;New Delhi&quot; , &quot;kochi&quot; , None , &quot;Bengaluru&quot; , &quot;Bangalore&quot; ], &quot;amount&quot; : [ &quot;1,200&quot; , &quot;950&quot; , &quot;₹3,400&quot; , &quot;₹3,400&quot; , &quot;abc&quot; , &quot;780&quot; , &quot;15000&quot; , &quot;2,050&quot; , None ], &quot;order_date&quot; :[ &quot;2025-01-05&quot; , &quot;05/01/2025&quot; , &quot;2025-01-07&quot; , &quot;2025-01-07&quot; , &quot;2025-02-30&quot; , &quot;2025-01-09&quot; , &quot;2025-01-10&quot; , &quot;2025-01-11&quot; , &quot;2025-01-12&quot; ], &quot;qty&quot; : [ 2 , 1 , 3 , 3 , None , 1 , 50 , 2 , 1 ], }) print (raw) print ( raw.dtypes.to_dict ()) Output order_id city amount order_date qty 0 A1 pune 1,200 2025-01-05 2.0 1 A2 Pune 950 05/01/2025 1.0 2 A3 DELHI ₹3,400 2025-01"}, {"i": "python-18", "t": "pandas III: GroupBy, Pivot Tables, Merge/Join and Reshape (melt/unpivot)", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-18-pandas-iii-groupby-pivot-tables-merge-join-and-reshape-melt.html", "s": " Split–apply–combine groupby splits rows into groups, applies a calculation to each group, and combines the results. It is SQL's GROUP BY and Excel's PivotTable. groupby splits rows by key, applies an aggregation per group, and combines the answers. Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np pd.set_option ( &quot;display.max_columns&quot; , 20 ); pd.set_option ( &quot;display.width&quot; , 160 ) s = pd.read_csv ( &quot;superstore_sales.csv&quot; , parse_dates=[ &quot;order_date&quot; ]) print ( s.groupby ( &quot;region&quot; )[ &quot;sales&quot; ]. sum (). sort_values (ascending= False )) print ( s.groupby ([ &quot;region&quot; , &quot;category&quot; ])[ &quot;profit&quot; ]. sum (). round ( 0 ). unstack ()) Output region South 53952768.5 West 51958520.5 North 49018161.0 East 46484119.0 Name: sales, dtype: float64 category Furniture Office Supplies Technology region East -209426.0 434731.0 4149185.0 North -236825.0 331848.0 4460806.0 South -259193.0 349958.0 4611247.0 West -112710.0 398257.0 4434286.0 Named aggregations Python Copy summary = ( s.groupby ( &quot;category&quot; ) . agg (orders=( &quot;order_id&quot; , &quot;nunique&quot; ), lines=( &quot;row_id&quot; , &quot;size&quot; ), sales=( &quot;sales&quot; , &quot;sum&quot; ), profit=( &quot;profit&quot; , &quot;sum&quot; ), avg_discount=( &quot;discount&quot; , &quot;mean&quot; ), max_line=( &quot;sales&quot; , &quot;max&quot; )) . assign (margin_pct= lambda d: ( 100 * d.profit / d.sales). round ( 1 )) . sort_values "}, {"i": "python-19", "t": "pandas IV: Dates, Time Series, Resampling and Rolling Windows", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-19-pandas-iv-dates-time-series-resampling-and-rolling-windows.html", "s": " Parsing dates Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np pd.set_option ( &quot;display.max_columns&quot; , 20 ); pd.set_option ( &quot;display.width&quot; , 160 ) d = pd.to_datetime ( pd.Series ([ &quot;2025-01-15&quot; , &quot;2025-03-01&quot; , &quot;2025-12-31&quot; ])) print ( d.dt.year.tolist (), d.dt.month_name (). tolist (), d.dt.day_name (). tolist (), d.dt.quarter.tolist (), d.dt.isocalendar (). week.tolist ()) print ( pd.to_datetime ( &quot;05/01/2025&quot; , dayfirst= True ). date (), pd.to_datetime ( &quot;05/01/2025&quot; ). date ()) # 5 Jan vs 1 May! print ( pd.to_datetime ( &quot;2025-01-15 14:30&quot; ). strftime ( &quot;%d %b %Y, %I:%M %p&quot; ), pd.Timestamp ( &quot;2025-02-28&quot; ).is_month_end) Output [2025, 2025, 2025] [&#x27;January&#x27;, &#x27;March&#x27;, &#x27;December&#x27;] [&#x27;Wednesday&#x27;, &#x27;Saturday&#x27;, &#x27;Wednesday&#x27;] [1, 1, 4] [3, 9, 1] 2025-01-05 2025-05-01 15 Jan 2025, 02:30 PM True Watch out Day-first dates Indian and European data often write 05/01/2025 for 5 January. Pass dayfirst=True or, better, an explicit format=\"%d/%m/%Y\" . Check a few parsed values every time. .dt accessor Gives .year , .month , .day , .hour Parts .day_name() , .month_name() Text names .dayofweek (Mon = 0), .quarter , .isocalendar().week Calendar positions .to_period(\"M\") , .to_period(\"Q\") Month / quarter periods .floor(\"D\") , .normalize() Truncate time .is_month_end , .days_in_month Calendar flags Date arithmetic Python Copy s = pd.read_cs"}, {"i": "python-20", "t": "Data Visualisation I: Matplotlib and Choosing the Right Chart", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-20-data-visualisation-i-matplotlib-and-choosing-the-right-chart.html", "s": " Choose the chart from the question Start from the question, not the chart type. The object-oriented API Matplotlib has two styles. Use the object-oriented one: create a Figure (the canvas) and one or more Axes (plots), then call methods on the axes. Monthly Superstore sales: a line chart shows the trend and the seasonal peaks. Python Copy import matplotlib.pyplot as plt fig, ax = plt.subplots (figsize=( 9 , 3.6 )) # one figure, one axes ax.plot (x, y) # draw ax.set_title ( &quot;...&quot; ); ax.set_xlabel ( &quot;...&quot; ); ax.set_ylabel ( &quot;...&quot; ) fig.savefig ( &quot;chart.png&quot; , dpi= 200 , bbox_inches= &quot;tight&quot; ) plt.show () # in scripts; notebooks display automatically Element Method Title, axis labels ax.set_title , ax.set_xlabel , ax.set_ylabel Limits, ticks ax.set_ylim(0) , ax.set_xticks , ax.tick_params(rotation=45) Legend label=\"...\" in the plot call, then ax.legend() Grid, spines ax.grid(axis=\"y\") , ax.spines[[\"top\",\"right\"]].set_visible(False) Text, arrows ax.text , ax.annotate Reference lines ax.axhline(y) , ax.axvline(x) , ax.axvspan Number format ax.yaxis.set_major_formatter(...) Bar charts Sorted horizontal bars make category comparisons easy; one highlight colour draws the eye. Grouped and stacked bars compare two dimensions: Sales by region and category, as grouped bars (compare within region) and stacked bars (compare totals). Distributions: histogram and box plot House prices are right-skewed: the mean sits above the median. Box plo"}, {"i": "python-21", "t": "Data Visualisation II: Seaborn Statistical Charts and Interactive Plotly", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-21-data-visualisation-ii-seaborn-statistical-charts-and-interac.html", "s": " Seaborn in one idea Seaborn works directly with long DataFrames : you name the columns for x , y , hue (colour), col / row (small multiples), and it groups, aggregates and adds legends for you. Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np, seaborn as sns, matplotlib.pyplot as plt hr = pd.read_csv ( &quot;hr_employees.csv&quot; ) hr[ &quot;left&quot; ] = (hr[ &quot;attrition&quot; ] == &quot;Yes&quot; ). astype (int) print (hr.shape, f&quot;attrition rate {hr.left.mean():.1%}&quot; , sns.__version__) print ( hr.groupby ( &quot;overtime&quot; )[ &quot;left&quot; ]. mean (). round ( 3 ). to_dict (), hr.groupby ( &quot;department&quot; )[ &quot;left&quot; ]. mean (). round ( 3 ). sort_values (). to_dict ()) Output (1500, 16) attrition rate 25.9% 0.13.2 {&#x27;No&#x27;: 0.216, &#x27;Yes&#x27;: 0.367} {&#x27;Operations&#x27;: 0.219, &#x27;Finance&#x27;: 0.227, &#x27;HR&#x27;: 0.229, &#x27;IT&#x27;: 0.243, &#x27;R&amp;D&#x27;: 0.26, &#x27;Sales&#x27;: 0.304} Question Seaborn function Distribution of one variable histplot , kdeplot , ecdfplot Distribution by group boxplot , violinplot , stripplot , boxenplot Counts of categories countplot Average per category (with CI) barplot , pointplot Two numeric variables scatterplot , regplot , lmplot , jointplot Many variables pairplot , heatmap of df.corr() Small multiples displot , catplot , relplot with col= / row= Distributions Income distribution for leavers vs stayers: leavers cluster at lower incomes. common_norm=False scales each grou"}, {"i": "python-22", "t": "Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA) Case Study: Telecom Customer Churn", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-22-exploratory-data-analysis-eda-case-study-telecom-customer-ch.html", "s": " The EDA checklist A structured EDA moves from the business question to actions. Business question. A telecom operator loses customers every month. Which customers churn, why, and where should retention money go first? Target: churn (Yes/No). Metric: churn rate = churned ÷ customers. 1. Load and first look Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np, seaborn as sns, matplotlib.pyplot as plt pd.set_option ( &quot;display.max_columns&quot; , 20 ); pd.set_option ( &quot;display.width&quot; , 160 ) tc = pd.read_csv ( &quot;telecom_churn.csv&quot; ) tc[ &quot;churned&quot; ] = (tc[ &quot;churn&quot; ] == &quot;Yes&quot; ). astype (int) print (tc.shape); print ( tc.head ( 3 )) print ( tc.dtypes.astype (str). to_dict ()) print ( f&quot;Overall churn rate: {tc.churned.mean():.1%} ({tc.churned.sum()} of {len(tc)})&quot; ) Output (3000, 11) customer_id senior_citizen tenure_months contract internet_service payment_method monthly_charges total_charges support_calls churn churned 0 T00001 0 71 Two year Fiber Net banking 81.28 5848.06 1 No 0 1 T00002 1 56 Month-to-month Fiber Auto-debit 115.12 6482.44 0 No 0 2 T00003 0 38 Two year Fiber Auto-debit 85.90 3186.34 1 No 0 {&#x27;customer_id&#x27;: &#x27;str&#x27;, &#x27;senior_citizen&#x27;: &#x27;int64&#x27;, &#x27;tenure_months&#x27;: &#x27;int64&#x27;, &#x27;contract&#x27;: &#x27;str&#x27;, &#x27;internet_service&#x27;: &#x27;str&#x27;, &#x27;payment_method&#x27;: &#x27;str&#x27;, &#x27;monthly_charges&#x27;: &#x27;float64&#x27;, &#x27;to"}, {"i": "python-23", "t": "Python and SQL: sqlite3, DuckDB, SQLAlchemy and pandas.read_sql", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-23-python-and-sql-sqlite3-duckdb-sqlalchemy-and-pandas-read-sql.html", "s": " Why connect Python to SQL? Company data lives in databases and warehouses (MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server, Snowflake, BigQuery, Databricks). The usual pattern: filter and aggregate in SQL (close to the data), then analyse, model and chart in Python . SQL pulls and summarises; Python analyses, models and presents. sqlite3: a database in a file SQLite ships with Python. It is perfect for learning, prototypes and small apps. Python Copy import sqlite3, pandas as pd con = sqlite3.connect ( &quot;:memory:&quot; ) # or &quot;shop.db&quot; for a file cur = con.cursor () cur.executescript ( &quot;&quot;&quot; CREATE TABLE customers (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL, city TEXT); CREATE TABLE orders (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, customer_id INTEGER REFERENCES customers(id), amount REAL, order_date TEXT); &quot;&quot;&quot; ) cur.executemany ( &quot;INSERT INTO customers (name, city) VALUES (?, ?)&quot; , [( &quot;Asha&quot; , &quot;Pune&quot; ), ( &quot;Ravi&quot; , &quot;Delhi&quot; ), ( &quot;Meera&quot; , &quot;Kochi&quot; )]) cur.executemany ( &quot;INSERT INTO orders (customer_id, amount, order_date) VALUES (?, ?, ?)&quot; , [( 1 , 1200 , &quot;2025-01-05&quot; ), ( 1 , 450 , &quot;2025-02-11&quot; ), ( 2 , 3000 , &quot;2025-02-14&quot; ), ( 3 , 780 , &quot;2025-03-01&quot; )]) con.commit () cur.execute ( &quot;SELECT c.name, COUNT(*), SUM(o.amount) FROM orders o JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id GROUP BY c.name ORDER BY 3 DESC&quot; ) print ( cur.fetchall ()) cur.exec"}, {"i": "python-24", "t": "APIs, JSON and Web Scraping with requests and BeautifulSoup", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-24-apis-json-and-web-scraping-with-requests-and-beautifulsoup.html", "s": " HTTP in five minutes An API (Application Programming Interface) lets programs ask another system for data. Web APIs use HTTP : the client sends a request (method, URL, headers, optional body) and receives a response (status code, headers, body, usually JSON). A REST API call. Method Meaning Status code Meaning GET Read 200 OK POST Create / send data 201 Created PUT / PATCH Replace / update 400 Bad request DELETE Remove 401 / 403 Not authenticated / not allowed 404 Not found 429 Too many requests (rate limit) 500 – 503 Server error: retry later A practice API To keep this lesson runnable offline, we start a tiny local API that behaves like a real one (paginated orders, an API key, a rate limit). Every call below works the same way against real services. Python Copy import json, threading, time from http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, HTTPServer from urllib.parse import urlparse, parse_qs ORDERS = [{ &quot;id&quot; : i, &quot;customer&quot; : { &quot;name&quot; : n, &quot;city&quot; : c}, &quot;items&quot; : [{ &quot;sku&quot; : f&quot;P{i}&quot; , &quot;qty&quot; : i % 3 + 1 , &quot;price&quot; : 100.0 * i}], &quot;status&quot; : s} for i, (n, c, s) in enumerate ([( &quot;Asha&quot; , &quot;Pune&quot; , &quot;shipped&quot; ), ( &quot;Ravi&quot; , &quot;Delhi&quot; , &quot;new&quot; ), ( &quot;Meera&quot; , &quot;Kochi&quot; , &quot;shipped&quot; ), ( &quot;Kabir&quot; , &quot;Goa&quot; , &quot;cancelled&quot; ), ( &quot;Zoya&quot; , &quot;Pune&quot; , &quot;new&quot; )"}, {"i": "python-25", "t": "Performance and Bigger Data: Vectorisation, Memory, Parquet, Polars and DuckDB", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-25-performance-and-bigger-data-vectorisation-memory-parquet-pol.html", "s": " Measure first \"Premature optimisation is the root of all evil\" (Donald Knuth). Profile, find the slow part, fix that. Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np, time, timeit s = pd.read_csv ( &quot;superstore_sales.csv&quot; , parse_dates=[ &quot;order_date&quot; ]) big = pd.concat ([s] * 20 , ignore_index= True ) # ~126k rows to make timings visible print (big.shape) def t (fn, n= 3 ): return min ( timeit.repeat (fn, number= 1 , repeat=n)) * 1000 loop = lambda : [r.sales * ( 1 - r.discount) for r in big.itertuples ()] apply_ = lambda : big.apply ( lambda r: r[ &quot;sales&quot; ] * ( 1 - r[ &quot;discount&quot; ]), axis= 1 ) vec = lambda : big[ &quot;sales&quot; ] * ( 1 - big[ &quot;discount&quot; ]) print ( f&quot;itertuples loop {t(loop, 1):8.1f} ms\\napply(axis=1) {t(apply_, 1):8.1f} ms\\nvectorised {t(vec):8.2f} ms&quot; ) Output (126460, 19) itertuples loop 1070.5 ms apply(axis=1) 659.5 ms vectorised 0.65 ms The vectorised version is more than a thousand times faster. itertuples and apply(axis=1) take about the same time because both are Python loops ( apply with axis=1 is a loop in disguise), and iterrows is slower still. Vectorising conditional logic Python Copy def band_loop (d): return &quot;high&quot; if d &gt; 0.2 else &quot;mid&quot; if d &gt; 0 else &quot;none&quot; f1 = lambda : big[ &quot;discount&quot; ]. map (band_loop) f2 = lambda : np.select ([big.discount &gt; 0.2 , big.discount &gt; 0 ], [ &quot;high&quot; , &quot;mid&quot; ], default= &quot;none&quot;"}, {"i": "python-26", "t": "DSA I: Big-O, Arrays, Strings, Hashing, Two Pointers and Sliding Window", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-26-dsa-i-big-o-arrays-strings-hashing-two-pointers-and-sliding.html", "s": " Why DSA for data roles? Data interviews (Amazon, Flipkart, Walmart, fintechs, product companies) often include one or two coding rounds with easy–medium problems on arrays, strings, hash maps and SQL. Beyond interviews, complexity thinking explains why a join on a set is instant while a nested loop over two million rows never finishes. Big-O notation Big-O describes how running time (or memory) grows with input size n , ignoring constants. Growth of common complexity classes. At n = 1,000,000 the difference between O(n log n) and O(n²) is days versus a second. Big-O Name Example O(1) Constant Dict lookup, list index, append O(log n) Logarithmic Binary search, balanced-tree lookup O(n) Linear One loop, x in list , sum O(n log n) Linearithmic Sorting ( sorted , Timsort), merge sort O(n²) Quadratic Nested loops over the same data O(2ⁿ) Exponential All subsets, naive recursion for Fibonacci Rules: drop constants (O(2n) → O(n)); keep the dominant term (O(n² + n) → O(n²)); sequential steps add, nested steps multiply. Python Copy import time def has_duplicate_quadratic (a): for i in range ( len (a)): for j in range (i + 1 , len (a)): if a[i] == a[j]: return True return False def has_duplicate_set (a): return len ( set (a)) != len (a) for n in ( 1000 , 2000 , 4000 ): data = list ( range (n)) t0 = time.perf_counter (); has_duplicate_quadratic (data); t1 = time.perf_counter (); has_duplicate_set (data); t2 = time.perf_counter () print ( f&quot;n={n:5d}: nested loops {1000*(t1-t0):7.1f"}, {"i": "python-27", "t": "DSA II: Stacks, Queues, Linked Lists, Recursion, Sorting and Binary Search", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-27-dsa-ii-stacks-queues-linked-lists-recursion-sorting-and-bina.html", "s": " Stacks: last in, first out A stack adds and removes at the same end (LIFO). In Python a list works: append to push, pop() to pop, both O(1). Uses: undo, browser back, function calls, parsing brackets. Stack (LIFO) vs queue (FIFO). Python Copy def valid_parentheses (s): pairs, stack = { &quot;)&quot; : &quot;(&quot; , &quot;]&quot; : &quot;[&quot; , &quot;}&quot; : &quot;{&quot; }, [] for ch in s: if ch in &quot;([{&quot; : stack.append (ch) elif ch in pairs: if not stack or stack.pop () != pairs[ch]: return False return not stack print ([ valid_parentheses (x) for x in [ &quot;()[]{}&quot; , &quot;([)]&quot; , &quot;{[()]}&quot; , &quot;((&quot; , &quot;SUM(IF(a&gt;1,(b),c))&quot; ]]) def eval_rpn (tokens): # reverse Polish notation: &quot;3 4 + 2 *&quot; = 14 st = [] for t in tokens: if t in &quot;+-*/&quot; : b, a = st.pop (), st.pop () st.append ({ &quot;+&quot; : a + b, &quot;-&quot; : a - b, &quot;*&quot; : a * b, &quot;/&quot; : int (a / b)}[t]) else : st.append ( int (t)) return st[ 0 ] print ( eval_rpn ( &quot;3 4 + 2 *&quot; . split ())) Output [True, False, True, False, True] 14 Monotonic stack Keep the stack sorted to answer \"next greater/smaller element\" in O(n) instead of O(n²). Python Copy def next_greater (nums): res, st = [- 1 ] * len (nums), [] # st holds indices with decreasing values for i, x in enumerate (nums): while st and nums[st[- 1 ]] &lt; x: res[ st.pop ()] = x st.append (i) return res def days_until_warmer (temps): res, st = [ 0 ] * len (temps), []"}, {"i": "python-28", "t": "DSA III: Trees, Heaps, Graphs (BFS/DFS) and Dynamic Programming", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-28-dsa-iii-trees-heaps-graphs-bfs-dfs-and-dynamic-programming.html", "s": " Trees A tree is a hierarchy of nodes: one root, each node with children, no cycles. Examples: folders, org charts, JSON documents, decision trees in ML, and B-tree indexes in databases. A binary search tree: every left child is smaller and every right child larger than its parent. Python Copy class TreeNode: def __init__ (self, val, left= None , right= None ): self.val, self.left, self.right = val, left, right def insert (root, val): # BST insert, O(height) if not root: return TreeNode (val) if val &lt; root.val: root.left = insert (root.left, val) else : root.right = insert (root.right, val) return root root = None for v in [ 8 , 3 , 10 , 1 , 6 , 14 , 4 , 7 , 13 ]: root = insert (root, v) def inorder (n): return inorder (n.left) + [n.val] + inorder (n.right) if n else [] def preorder (n): return [n.val] + preorder (n.left) + preorder (n.right) if n else [] def postorder (n): return postorder (n.left) + postorder (n.right) + [n.val] if n else [] from collections import deque def level_order (n): out, q = [], deque ([n]) while q: level = [] for _ in range ( len (q)): node = q.popleft (); level.append (node.val) q.extend (c for c in (node.left, node.right) if c) out.append (level) return out def height (n): return 1 + max ( height (n.left), height (n.right)) if n else 0 def contains (n, x): while n: if x == n.val: return True n = n.left if x &lt; n.val else n.right return False print ( &quot;in-order :&quot; , inorder (root), &quot;(sorted!)&quot; ) print ( &quot;pre-order :&q"}, {"i": "python-29", "t": "Python Capstone: End-to-End Retail Sales Analytics Project", "k": "Python", "f": "lessons/python-29-python-capstone-end-to-end-retail-sales-analytics-project.html", "s": " The brief The sales director of an Indian office-supplies retailer asks: How did 2025 go, where do we make and lose money, how loyal are our customers, and what should we change in 2026? Deliver a short report with charts and an Excel workbook her team can filter. Capstone pipeline. Project layout (Lesson 13): data/raw , src/ (functions below), notebooks/ (exploration), reports/ (outputs), README.md . 1. Load and validate Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np, matplotlib.pyplot as plt pd.set_option ( &quot;display.max_columns&quot; , 20 ); pd.set_option ( &quot;display.width&quot; , 160 ) def load (path= &quot;superstore_sales.csv&quot; ): df = pd.read_csv (path, parse_dates=[ &quot;order_date&quot; , &quot;ship_date&quot; ]) return df def validate (df): checks = { &quot;row_id unique&quot; : df[ &quot;row_id&quot; ].is_unique, &quot;no missing values&quot; : df.isna (). sum (). sum () == 0 , &quot;sales positive&quot; : (df[ &quot;sales&quot; ] &gt; 0 ). all (), &quot;discount between 0 and 1&quot; : df[ &quot;discount&quot; ]. between ( 0 , 1 ). all (), &quot;ship after order&quot; : (df[ &quot;ship_date&quot; ] &gt;= df[ &quot;order_date&quot; ]). all (), &quot;quantity 1-20&quot; : df[ &quot;quantity&quot; ]. between ( 1 , 20 ). all (), } for name, ok in checks.items (): print ( f&quot; {&#x27;PASS&#x27; if ok else &#x27;FAIL&#x27;} {name}&quot; ) assert all ( checks.values ()), &quot;validation failed&quot; return df s = validate ( load ()) print (s.shape, s.orde"}, {"i": "ml-01", "t": "Descriptive Statistics: Centre, Spread, Shape and Relationships", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-01-descriptive-statistics-centre-spread-shape-and-relationships.html", "s": " Statistics in data science Descriptive statistics summarise the data you have. Inferential statistics use a sample to draw conclusions about a larger population (next lessons). Machine learning builds on both. The statistics roadmap for this track. Types of data Variable types decide which statistics and charts make sense. Scale Has order? Equal intervals? True zero? Example Allowed statistics Nominal No – – City Mode, counts Ordinal Yes No – Satisfaction 1–4 Median, percentiles Interval Yes Yes No Temperature °C Mean, sd (no ratios) Ratio Yes Yes Yes Income, price Everything, including ratios Measures of centre Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np from scipy import stats h = pd.read_csv ( &quot;house_prices.csv&quot; ) p = h[ &quot;price_lakh&quot; ] print ( f&quot;mean {p.mean():.1f} | median {p.median():.1f} | mode (rounded to 10) {(p.round(-1)).mode()[0]:.0f} | trimmed mean 10% {stats.trim_mean(p, 0.1):.1f}&quot; ) print ( &quot;bedrooms mode:&quot; , h[ &quot;bedrooms&quot; ]. mode (). tolist (), &quot;| city mode:&quot; , h[ &quot;city&quot; ]. mode ()[ 0 ]) w = pd.DataFrame ({ &quot;city_avg_price&quot; : [ 62 , 88 , 75 ], &quot;houses&quot; : [ 100 , 20 , 50 ]}) print ( &quot;simple mean of city averages:&quot; , w.city_avg_price.mean (). round ( 1 ), &quot;| weighted mean:&quot; , np.average (w.city_avg_price, weights=w.houses). round ( 1 )) Output mean 80.4 | median 78.9 | mode (rounded to 10) 80 | trimmed mean 10% 78.9 bedrooms mode: [2] | city mode: Benga"}, {"i": "ml-02", "t": "Probability and Distributions: Rules, Bayes, Binomial, Poisson and Normal", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-02-probability-and-distributions-rules-bayes-binomial-poisson-a.html", "s": " Probability basics Probability is a number from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain). For equally likely outcomes, P(event) = favourable outcomes ÷ all outcomes. In data, it is a long-run frequency. Python Copy import numpy as np, pandas as pd from scipy import stats rng = np.random.default_rng ( 7 ) rolls = rng.integers ( 1 , 7 , 100_000) print ( &quot;P(six) theory 0.1667 | simulated&quot; , round ((rolls == 6 ). mean (), 4 )) print ( &quot;P(even or &gt; 4) theory&quot; , round ( 4 / 6 , 4 ), &quot;| simulated&quot; , round (((rolls % 2 == 0 ) | (rolls &gt; 4 )). mean (), 4 )) Output P(six) theory 0.1667 | simulated 0.1682 P(even or &gt; 4) theory 0.6667 | simulated 0.6679 Rule Formula Example Complement P(not A) = 1 − P(A) P(no conversion) = 1 − 0.10 Addition P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B) Even or &gt; 4: 3/6 + 2/6 − 1/6 Multiplication (independent) P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B) Two sixes: 1/36 Conditional P(A | B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B) P(churn | month-to-month) Python Copy tc = pd.DataFrame ({ &quot;contract&quot; : [ &quot;M2M&quot; ] * 1690 + [ &quot;1yr&quot; ] * 739 + [ &quot;2yr&quot; ] * 571 , &quot;churn&quot; : [ 1 ] * 607 + [ 0 ] * 1083 + [ 1 ] * 125 + [ 0 ] * 614 + [ 1 ] * 40 + [ 0 ] * 531 }) # counts from the churn EDA joint = pd.crosstab (tc.contract, tc.churn, normalize= &quot;all&quot; ). round ( 3 ) print (joint) print ( &quot;P(churn) =&quot; , round ( tc.churn.mean (), 3 ), &quot;| P(churn | M2M) =&quot; , round (tc[tc.contract == &quot;M2M&quot; ]. churn.mean "}, {"i": "ml-03", "t": "Sampling, the Central Limit Theorem and Confidence Intervals", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-03-sampling-the-central-limit-theorem-and-confidence-intervals.html", "s": " Population and sample Term Population Sample What Everyone/everything of interest The part you observe Summary Parameter (μ, σ, p) Statistic (x̄, s, p̂) Example All 1,500 employees 100 surveyed employees We almost never see the population (all customers, all future orders), so we estimate parameters from samples and state our uncertainty . Sampling methods. Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np from scipy import stats hr = pd.read_csv ( &quot;hr_employees.csv&quot; ) pop_mean, pop_rate = hr.monthly_income.mean (), (hr.attrition == &quot;Yes&quot; ). mean () print ( f&quot;population: mean income {pop_mean:,.0f}, attrition {pop_rate:.1%}, N = {len(hr)}&quot; ) srs = hr.sample ( 150 , random_state= 1 ) strat = hr.groupby ( &quot;department&quot; , group_keys= False ). sample (frac= 0.1 , random_state= 1 ) # stratified 10% print ( f&quot;simple random n=150: mean {srs.monthly_income.mean():,.0f}, attrition {(srs.attrition == &#x27;Yes&#x27;).mean():.1%}&quot; ) print ( f&quot;stratified n={len(strat)}: mean {strat.monthly_income.mean():,.0f}; department shares kept:&quot; , ( strat.department.value_counts (normalize= True ) - hr.department.value_counts (normalize= True )). abs (). max (). round ( 3 ) &lt; 0.01 ) Output population: mean income 82,611, attrition 25.9%, N = 1500 simple random n=150: mean 85,587, attrition 21.3% stratified n=149: mean 81,315; department shares kept: True Watch out Sampling bias beats sample size A huge biased sample is worse than a small ran"}, {"i": "ml-04", "t": "Hypothesis Testing I: The Framework, p-values, z-tests and t-tests", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-04-hypothesis-testing-i-the-framework-p-values-z-tests-and-t-te.html", "s": " From business question to hypothesis Case. A quick-commerce company promises \"delivery in 30 minutes\". Operations claim Pune meets the promise on average. Is the average delivery time in Pune really 30 minutes, or is it slower? Is Hyderabad different from Pune? A hypothesis test checks whether data are consistent with a default claim. Term Meaning Delivery case H₀ (null) No effect / status quo; assumed true until evidence μ_Pune = 30 min H₁ (alternative) What we want evidence for μ_Pune &gt; 30 min (one-tailed) or ≠ 30 (two-tailed) α (significance level) Accepted risk of a false alarm 0.05 Test statistic Distance from H₀ in standard-error units t = (x̄ − 30)/(s/√n) p-value P(data at least this extreme | H₀ true) from the t distribution Decision p ≤ α → reject H₀; p &gt; α → fail to reject H₀ The hypothesis-testing procedure. p-values and critical values Two-tailed test at α = 0.05: the rejection regions are the 2.5% tails beyond ±1.96 (z). The p-value is the tail area beyond the observed statistic. Python Copy print ( &quot;critical values: two-tailed z ±&quot; , round ( stats.norm.ppf ( 0.975 ), 3 ), &quot;| one-tailed z&quot; , round ( stats.norm.ppf ( 0.95 ), 3 ), &quot;| two-tailed t(df=59) ±&quot; , round ( stats.t.ppf ( 0.975 , 59 ), 3 )) for z in ( 1.2 , 1.7 , 2.06 , 3.1 ): print ( f&quot;z = {z}: two-tailed p = {2 * stats.norm.sf(abs(z)):.4f} | one-tailed p = {stats.norm.sf(z):.4f}&quot; ) Output critical values: two-tailed z ± 1.96 | one-tailed z 1.645 | two-tailed "}, {"i": "ml-05", "t": "Hypothesis Testing II: Chi-Square, ANOVA, Non-Parametric Tests and Correlation Tests", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-05-hypothesis-testing-ii-chi-square-anova-non-parametric-tests.html", "s": " Which test? A decision map Choosing a test from the type of variables and the number of groups. Parametric (assumes ~normal) Non-parametric (ranks; fewer assumptions) One-sample / paired t Wilcoxon signed-rank Independent t (Welch) Mann-Whitney U One-way ANOVA Kruskal-Wallis Pearson correlation Spearman correlation Parametric tests are more powerful when their assumptions hold; non-parametric tests are safer for small, skewed samples, ordinal data and outliers. Chi-square test of independence Case. A retailer surveyed 600 customers: is the preferred shopping channel (App, Store, Website) related to age group ? Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np from scipy import stats c = pd.read_csv ( &quot;channel_survey.csv&quot; ) obs = pd.crosstab (c.age_group, c.preferred_channel) print (obs, &quot;\\n&quot; ) print (( pd.crosstab (c.age_group, c.preferred_channel, normalize= &quot;index&quot; ) * 100 ). round ( 1 )) res = stats.chi2_contingency (obs) print ( f&quot;\\nχ² = {res.statistic:.2f}, df = {res.dof}, p = {res.pvalue:.2e}&quot; ) print ( &quot;expected counts if independent:\\n&quot; , pd.DataFrame (res.expected_freq, index=obs.index, columns=obs.columns). round ( 1 )) n = obs.values.sum (); cramers_v = np.sqrt (res.statistic / (n * ( min (obs.shape) - 1 ))) print ( f&quot;Cramér&#x27;s V = {cramers_v:.2f} (effect size: 0.1 small, 0.3 medium, 0.5 large)&quot; ) Output preferred_channel App Store Website age_group 18-25 106 47 65 26-40 72 70 90 41+ 31 56 63 preferred_cha"}, {"i": "ml-06", "t": "A/B Testing and Experimentation: Design, Power, Analysis and Pitfalls", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-06-a-b-testing-and-experimentation-design-power-analysis-and-pi.html", "s": " What is an A/B test? A randomised controlled experiment : users are randomly split into control (A) and treatment (B) ; the only systematic difference is the change you test, so differences in outcomes are caused by the change (up to chance, which statistics quantifies). The experiment lifecycle. Case. An online store tests a new one-page checkout (B) against the current checkout (A). 4,000 users were split 50/50. Hypothesis: the new checkout increases the purchase conversion rate. Primary metric: conversion = purchasers ÷ users. Secondary: revenue per user, average order value. Guardrails: refund rate, page load time, support tickets. Design choice Good practice Randomisation unit User (not session or page view), so one person sees one version Split 50/50 maximises power Duration At least one full business cycle (1–2 weeks, including weekends); fixed in advance Metric Defined before launch; one primary metric Population Who is eligible (new users only? all platforms?) Sample size and power Before launching: how many users do we need to detect the smallest effect worth acting on (the minimum detectable effect , MDE)? Python Copy import numpy as np, pandas as pd from scipy import stats from statsmodels.stats.power import NormalIndPower from statsmodels.stats.proportion import proportion_effectsize base = 0.10 for target in ( 0.11 , 0.12 , 0.13 ): h = proportion_effectsize (target, base) # Cohen&#x27;s h n = NormalIndPower (). solve_power (effect_size=h, alpha= 0.05 , power= 0"}, {"i": "ml-07", "t": "Introduction to Machine Learning: Types, Business Framing and the ML Lifecycle", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-07-introduction-to-machine-learning-types-business-framing-and.html", "s": " What is machine learning? \"A computer program is said to learn from experience E with respect to some task T and performance measure P if its performance at T, as measured by P, improves with experience E.\" (Tom Mitchell) Instead of writing rules by hand, we give the computer examples (data) and let an algorithm learn the rules (a model) that map inputs to outputs. Traditional programming vs machine learning. Statistics Machine learning Main goal Inference: understand relationships, test hypotheses Prediction: accurate outputs on new data Typical question \"Does overtime increase attrition, and by how much?\" \"Which employees are likely to leave next quarter?\" Model choice Interpretable, with assumptions Whatever predicts best (with interpretability as needed) Validation p-values, confidence intervals Held-out test data, cross-validation They overlap heavily: linear and logistic regression belong to both. Types of machine learning The main families of machine learning. Problem Target type Example Algorithms (this track) Regression Number House price, delivery time, demand Linear, Ridge/Lasso, trees, boosting Classification Category Churn yes/no, loan default, spam Logistic, KNN, trees, RF, boosting, SVM, NB Clustering None Customer segments K-means, hierarchical, DBSCAN Dimensionality reduction None Compress 50 features to 5 PCA Time-series forecasting Number over time Next month's sales Seasonal naive, ETS, ARIMA, ML with lags Recommendation / ranking Ranked list Products to "}, {"i": "ml-08", "t": "The scikit-learn Workflow: Train/Test Split, Baselines, Pipelines and Saving Models", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-08-the-scikit-learn-workflow-train-test-split-baselines-pipelin.html", "s": " The estimator API Every scikit-learn model follows the same pattern, which makes swapping algorithms easy. One consistent interface for every estimator. Method Available on Does fit(X, y) Every estimator Learns parameters from training data predict(X) Models Predicted labels or values predict_proba(X) Most classifiers Class probabilities transform(X) Transformers (scalers, encoders, PCA) Converts features using fitted parameters fit_transform(X) Transformers Fit then transform (training data only!) score(X, y) Models Default metric (accuracy or R²) get_params() / set_params() All Hyperparameters X is a 2-D table of features (DataFrame or array, shape n_samples × n_features ); y is a 1-D target. Train/test split We judge a model on data it has never seen . Hold out a test set before doing anything else. Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split tc = pd.read_csv ( &quot;telecom_churn.csv&quot; ) X = tc.drop (columns=[ &quot;customer_id&quot; , &quot;churn&quot; , &quot;total_charges&quot; ]) # total_charges ≈ tenure × monthly: redundant y = (tc[ &quot;churn&quot; ] == &quot;Yes&quot; ). astype (int) X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split (X, y, test_size= 0.2 , stratify=y, random_state= 42 ) print (X_train.shape, X_test.shape, f&quot;churn rate train {y_train.mean():.3f} | test {y_test.mean():.3f}&quot; ) Output (2400, 7) (600, 7) churn rate train 0.258 | test 0.257 Typical data splits. The test set is a fina"}, {"i": "ml-09", "t": "Data Preprocessing and Feature Engineering for ML", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-09-data-preprocessing-and-feature-engineering-for-ml.html", "s": " Why preprocessing matters Algorithms only see numbers. How you represent the data often matters more than which algorithm you choose (\"better data beats fancier models\"). The preprocessing toolbox, applied inside a pipeline. Missing values Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np from sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer, KNNImputer from sklearn.experimental import enable_iterative_imputer # noqa: F401 from sklearn.impute import IterativeImputer h = pd.read_csv ( &quot;house_prices.csv&quot; ) num = h[[ &quot;area_sqft&quot; , &quot;bedrooms&quot; , &quot;bathrooms&quot; , &quot;age_years&quot; , &quot;price_lakh&quot; ]] mask = num.area_sqft.isna (); print ( &quot;missing area:&quot; , mask.sum ()) for name, imp in [( &quot;median&quot; , SimpleImputer (strategy= &quot;median&quot; )), ( &quot;KNN (5 neighbours)&quot; , KNNImputer (n_neighbors= 5 )), ( &quot;iterative (regression)&quot; , IterativeImputer (random_state= 0 ))]: filled = pd.DataFrame ( imp.fit_transform (num), columns=num.columns) print ( f&quot;{name:24s} imputed areas: mean {filled.loc[mask, &#x27;area_sqft&#x27;].mean():7.0f}, sd {filled.loc[mask, &#x27;area_sqft&#x27;].std():6.0f}&quot; ) print ( &quot;SimpleImputer(add_indicator=True) adds a column:&quot; , SimpleImputer (strategy= &quot;median&quot; , add_indicator= True ). fit (num[[ &quot;area_sqft&quot; ]]). get_feature_names_out (). tolist ()) Output missing area: 25 median imputed areas: mean 1240, sd 0 KNN (5 neighbours) imputed areas: mean 139"}, {"i": "ml-10", "t": "Linear Regression: Fitting, Interpreting, Assumptions and Diagnostics", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-10-linear-regression-fitting-interpreting-assumptions-and-diagn.html", "s": " Simple linear regression Find the straight line that best predicts y from x : Ordinary least squares (OLS) chooses β₀ and β₁ to minimise the sum of squared residuals , where a residual is actual − predicted. Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np import statsmodels.formula.api as smf h = pd.read_csv ( &quot;house_prices.csv&quot; ). dropna (subset=[ &quot;area_sqft&quot; ]) pps = h.price_lakh * 1e5 / h.area_sqft # price per sq ft h = h[ pps.between ( pps.median () / 3 , pps.median () * 3 )] # drop 4 impossible prices (data-entry errors found in EDA) print ( len (h), &quot;houses&quot; ) m1 = smf.ols ( &quot;price_lakh ~ area_sqft&quot; , data=h). fit () print ( m1.params.round ( 4 ). to_dict (), &quot;| R² =&quot; , round (m1.rsquared, 3 )) b0, b1 = m1.params print ( f&quot;price ≈ {b0:.2f} + {b1:.4f} × area → each extra 100 sq ft adds about ₹{100 * b1:.1f} lakh&quot; ) print ( &quot;prediction for 1,500 sq ft:&quot; , round ( m1.predict ( pd.DataFrame ({ &quot;area_sqft&quot; : [ 1500 ]}))[ 0 ], 1 ), &quot;lakh&quot; ) Output 1171 houses {&#x27;Intercept&#x27;: -7.0873, &#x27;area_sqft&#x27;: 0.0696} | R² = 0.827 price ≈ -7.09 + 0.0696 × area → each extra 100 sq ft adds about ₹7.0 lakh prediction for 1,500 sq ft: 97.3 lakh The least-squares line and the residuals (vertical distances) it minimises, for 40 sample houses. Multiple linear regression Python Copy m2 = smf.ols ( &quot;price_lakh ~ area_sqft + bedrooms + bathrooms + age_years + distance_to_center_km + parking"}, {"i": "ml-11", "t": "Gradient Descent: How Models Learn (Built from Scratch in NumPy)", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-11-gradient-descent-how-models-learn-built-from-scratch-in-nump.html", "s": " Learning = minimising a cost A model has parameters (weights). Learning means finding the weights that make a cost function as small as possible. For linear regression the usual cost is the mean squared error : (The ½ simply makes the derivative tidier.) OLS has a formula for the minimum, but most models (logistic regression, neural networks) do not, so we search for it. The idea: walk downhill Imagine standing on a foggy hillside: feel the slope under your feet and step downhill; repeat until the ground is flat. The gradient is the slope; we step in the opposite direction. α is the learning rate (step size). For linear regression the gradients are: Gradient descent on a one-parameter cost curve: each step moves against the slope; steps shrink as the slope flattens near the minimum. Implementation in NumPy Python Copy import pandas as pd h = pd.read_csv ( &quot;house_prices.csv&quot; ). dropna (subset=[ &quot;area_sqft&quot; ]) pps = h.price_lakh * 1e5 / h.area_sqft; h = h[ pps.between ( pps.median () / 3 , pps.median () * 3 )] feats = [ &quot;area_sqft&quot; , &quot;bedrooms&quot; , &quot;bathrooms&quot; , &quot;age_years&quot; , &quot;distance_to_center_km&quot; ] X_raw = h[feats]. to_numpy (float); y = h.price_lakh.to_numpy (float) mu, sd = X_raw.mean (axis= 0 ), X_raw.std (axis= 0 ); X = (X_raw - mu) / sd # standardise features def gradient_descent (X, y, lr= 0.1 , epochs= 300 ): n, p = X.shape; w, b = np.zeros (p), 0.0 ; history = [] for _ in range (epochs): y_hat = X @"}, {"i": "ml-12", "t": "Overfitting, Polynomial Regression and Regularisation (Ridge, Lasso, Elastic Net)", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-12-overfitting-polynomial-regression-and-regularisation-ridge-l.html", "s": " Underfitting and overfitting Polynomial models of degree 1, 4 and 15 on the same noisy data. Degree 1 underfits, degree 4 fits well, degree 15 chases noise. Underfitting Good fit Overfitting Training error High Low Very low Test error High Low High Cause Model too simple, missing features Model too complex, too little data, leakage Fix More features, more flexible model, less regularisation More data, simpler model, regularisation, feature selection, early stopping The bias-variance trade-off Bias is the error from wrong assumptions (a line for a curve). Variance is the error from sensitivity to the particular training sample. Total expected error = bias² + variance + irreducible noise. Python Copy from sklearn.metrics import mean_squared_error x_test = rng.uniform ( 0 , 1 , 500 ); y_test = np.sin ( 2 * np.pi * x_test) + rng.normal ( 0 , . 25 , 500 ) for d in ( 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 , 6 , 9 , 12 , 15 ): m = make_pipeline ( PolynomialFeatures (d), StandardScaler (), LinearRegression ()). fit (x[:, None ], y) tr = mean_squared_error (y, m.predict (x[:, None ])); te = mean_squared_error (y_test, m.predict (x_test[:, None ])) print ( f&quot;degree {d:2d}: train MSE {tr:7.3f} | test MSE {te:10.3f}&quot; ) Output degree 1: train MSE 0.271 | test MSE 0.284 degree 2: train MSE 0.271 | test MSE 0.285 degree 3: train MSE 0.057 | test MSE 0.082 degree 4: train MSE 0.053 | test MSE 0.085 degree 6: train MSE 0.042 | test MSE 0.137 degree 9: train MSE 0.041 | test MSE 0.118 degree 12: train MSE 0"}, {"i": "ml-13", "t": "Logistic Regression: Probabilities, Odds Ratios and Credit-Risk Scoring", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-13-logistic-regression-probabilities-odds-ratios-and-credit-ris.html", "s": " The case: who will default? A bank has 700 past borrowers with a known outcome ( default = 1 if they failed to repay) and 150 new applicants with unknown outcomes. Features: age, education level ( ed 1–5), years with current employer ( employ ), years at current address ( address ), income (thousands), debt-to-income ratio % ( debtinc ), credit-card debt and other debt (thousands). Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np b = pd.read_csv ( &quot;bank_loans.csv&quot; ) known, new = b[ b.default.notna ()]. copy (), b[ b.default.isna ()]. copy () known[ &quot;default&quot; ] = known.default.astype (int) print ( len (known), &quot;labelled |&quot; , len (new), &quot;new applicants | default rate&quot; , round ( known.default.mean (), 3 )) print ( known.groupby ( &quot;default&quot; )[[ &quot;age&quot; , &quot;employ&quot; , &quot;address&quot; , &quot;income&quot; , &quot;debtinc&quot; , &quot;creddebt&quot; , &quot;othdebt&quot; ]]. mean (). round ( 2 )) Output 700 labelled | 150 new applicants | default rate 0.321 age employ address income debtinc creddebt othdebt default 0 39.69 7.61 6.29 50.94 8.55 1.47 2.96 1 32.67 3.45 3.29 46.30 13.17 2.31 3.99 Defaulters are younger, have shorter employment and address histories, and much higher debt-to-income ratios. Why not linear regression? A straight line predicts values below 0 and above 1, which are not probabilities. Logistic regression passes a linear score through the sigmoid : Equivalently, the log-odds (logit) is linear i"}, {"i": "ml-14", "t": "Classification Metrics: Confusion Matrix, Precision, Recall, ROC, PR and Business Value", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-14-classification-metrics-confusion-matrix-precision-recall-roc.html", "s": " The confusion matrix Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split from sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler, OneHotEncoder from sklearn.pipeline import Pipeline from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression from sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix, accuracy_score, precision_score, recall_score, f1_score, fbeta_score, classification_report tc = pd.read_csv ( &quot;telecom_churn.csv&quot; ) X = tc.drop (columns=[ &quot;customer_id&quot; , &quot;churn&quot; , &quot;total_charges&quot; ]); y = (tc.churn == &quot;Yes&quot; ). astype (int) Xtr, Xte, ytr, yte = train_test_split (X, y, test_size= 0.3 , stratify=y, random_state= 1 ) pre = ColumnTransformer ([( &quot;n&quot; , StandardScaler (), [ &quot;senior_citizen&quot; , &quot;tenure_months&quot; , &quot;monthly_charges&quot; , &quot;support_calls&quot; ]), ( &quot;c&quot; , OneHotEncoder (), [ &quot;contract&quot; , &quot;internet_service&quot; , &quot;payment_method&quot; ])]) model = Pipeline ([( &quot;pre&quot; , pre), ( &quot;lr&quot; , LogisticRegression (max_iter= 1000 ))]). fit (Xtr, ytr) proba = model.predict_proba (Xte)[:, 1 ]; pred = (proba &gt;= 0.5 ). astype (int) tn, fp, fn, tp = confusion_matrix (yte, pred). ravel () print ( pd.DataFrame ([[tn, fp], [fn, tp]], index=[ &quot;actual: stay&quot; , &quot;actual: churn&quot; ], columns=[ &quot;pred: stay&quot; , &quot;pred: churn&quot; ])) Output pred: sta"}, {"i": "ml-15", "t": "Cross-Validation and Hyperparameter Tuning: K-Fold, GridSearchCV, RandomizedSearchCV and Optuna", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-15-cross-validation-and-hyperparameter-tuning-k-fold-gridsearch.html", "s": " One split is noisy Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, cross_val_score, cross_validate, StratifiedKFold, KFold, GridSearchCV, RandomizedSearchCV from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline, Pipeline from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression from sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score b = pd.read_csv ( &quot;bank_loans.csv&quot; ). dropna (subset=[ &quot;default&quot; ]) feats = [ &quot;age&quot; , &quot;ed&quot; , &quot;employ&quot; , &quot;address&quot; , &quot;income&quot; , &quot;debtinc&quot; , &quot;creddebt&quot; , &quot;othdebt&quot; ] X, y = b[feats], b.default.astype (int) pipe = make_pipeline ( StandardScaler (), LogisticRegression (max_iter= 1000 )) aucs = [] for seed in range ( 20 ): Xa, Xb, ya, yb = train_test_split (X, y, test_size= 0.25 , stratify=y, random_state=seed) aucs.append ( roc_auc_score (yb, pipe.fit (Xa, ya). predict_proba (Xb)[:, 1 ])) print ( f&quot;20 different random splits: test AUC from {min(aucs):.3f} to {max(aucs):.3f} (mean {np.mean(aucs):.3f})&quot; ) Output 20 different random splits: test AUC from 0.745 to 0.865 (mean 0.801) With 700 rows, the test score depends a lot on which rows land in the test set. Cross-validation averages over several splits. K-fold cross-validation 5-fold cross-validation: each fold is the validation set once; the score is the average of 5 runs. Python Copy cv = StratifiedKFold (n_splits= 5 , shu"}, {"i": "ml-16", "t": "K-Nearest Neighbours: Classifier and Regressor (Bank Loans Case Study)", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-16-k-nearest-neighbours-classifier-and-regressor-bank-loans-cas.html", "s": " The idea \"Tell me who your neighbours are, and I'll tell you who you are.\" To predict a new point, find the k most similar labelled points and let them vote (classification) or average their values (regression). There is no training beyond storing the data, so KNN is a lazy, instance-based learner. KNN with k = 5: the new applicant (star) is classified by the majority of its five nearest neighbours. Distances Minkowski distance generalises both ( p=2 Euclidean, p=1 Manhattan). Cosine distance suits text and embeddings; Hamming suits binary features. Python Copy from scipy.spatial import distance x1, x2 = np.array ([ 30 , 45.0 , 8.5 ]), np.array ([ 42 , 60.0 , 12.0 ]) # age, income (thousands), debt-to-income % print ( &quot;Euclidean&quot; , round ( distance.euclidean (x1, x2), 2 ), &quot;| Manhattan&quot; , distance.cityblock (x1, x2), &quot;| cosine&quot; , round ( distance.cosine (x1, x2), 4 )) Output Euclidean 19.53 | Manhattan 30.5 | cosine 0.0003 Why scaling is essential Python Copy b = pd.read_csv ( &quot;bank_loans.csv&quot; ) known = b[ b.default.notna ()]. copy (); known[ &quot;default&quot; ] = known.default.astype (int); new_apps = b[ b.default.isna ()]. copy () feats = [ &quot;age&quot; , &quot;ed&quot; , &quot;employ&quot; , &quot;address&quot; , &quot;income&quot; , &quot;debtinc&quot; , &quot;creddebt&quot; , &quot;othdebt&quot; ] print (known[feats]. describe ().loc[[ &quot;mean&quot; , &quot;std&quot; , &quot;max&quot; ]]. round ( 1 )) Output age ed employ "}, {"i": "ml-17", "t": "Decision Trees: Splits, Impurity, Pruning and Interpretable Rules", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-17-decision-trees-splits-impurity-pruning-and-interpretable-rul.html", "s": " How a tree decides A decision tree asks a sequence of yes/no questions about the features. Each question splits the data into two purer groups; leaves give the prediction (majority class or average value). It is a flowchart learned from data. A decision tree is a learned flowchart of questions. Measuring purity At each node the algorithm tries every feature and threshold and picks the split that most reduces impurity . Python Copy import numpy as np, pandas as pd def gini (p): p = np.asarray (p); return 1 - (p ** 2 ). sum () def entropy (p): p = np.asarray ([x for x in p if x &gt; 0 ]); return float (-(p * np.log2 (p)). sum ()) + 0.0 for dist in ([ 0.5 , 0.5 ], [ 0.8 , 0.2 ], [ 1.0 , 0.0 ]): print (dist, &quot;Gini&quot; , round ( gini (dist), 3 ), &quot;| entropy&quot; , round ( entropy (dist), 3 )) hr = pd.read_csv ( &quot;hr_employees.csv&quot; ); hr[ &quot;left&quot; ] = (hr.attrition == &quot;Yes&quot; ). astype (int) def split_gain (df, mask): parent = gini ([ 1 - df.left.mean (), df.left.mean ()]) l, r = df[mask], df[~mask] child = sum ( len (s) / len (df) * gini ([ 1 - s.left.mean (), s.left.mean ()]) for s in (l, r)) return parent - child for name, mask in [( &quot;overtime == Yes&quot; , hr.overtime == &quot;Yes&quot; ), ( &quot;job_satisfaction &lt;= 2&quot; , hr.job_satisfaction &lt;= 2 ), ( &quot;years_at_company &lt;= 1&quot; , hr.years_at_company &lt;= 1 ), ( &quot;department == Sales&quot; , hr.department == &quot;Sales&quot; )]: print ( f&quot;{name:24s} Gin"}, {"i": "ml-18", "t": "Ensembles I: Bagging and Random Forests", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-18-ensembles-i-bagging-and-random-forests.html", "s": " Why ensembles work If each of many independent models is right 65% of the time, a majority vote of them is right far more often. Errors cancel when models make different mistakes. Python Copy import numpy as np, pandas as pd from scipy.stats import binom for n in ( 1 , 5 , 25 , 101 ): print ( f&quot;{n:3d} independent voters, each 65% accurate → majority correct {binom.sf(n // 2, n, 0.65):.3f}&quot; ) Output 1 independent voters, each 65% accurate → majority correct 0.650 5 independent voters, each 65% accurate → majority correct 0.765 25 independent voters, each 65% accurate → majority correct 0.940 101 independent voters, each 65% accurate → majority correct 0.999 Real models are correlated (trained on the same data), so the gain is smaller. The trick is to make trees diverse . Bagging: bootstrap aggregating Train each tree on a bootstrap sample (rows drawn with replacement, same size as the data), then average predictions (regression) or vote (classification). Each bootstrap sample leaves out about 37% of rows: the out-of-bag (OOB) rows act as a free validation set. Bagging: many bootstrap samples, one deep tree each, averaged predictions. Random forests A random forest = bagging + a random subset of features considered at each split ( max_features ). This decorrelates the trees further: a dominant feature cannot sit at the top of every tree. Python Copy from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, cross_val_score, StratifiedKFold from sklearn.tree import Decisio"}, {"i": "ml-19", "t": "Ensembles II: Gradient Boosting, XGBoost, LightGBM and CatBoost", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-19-ensembles-ii-gradient-boosting-xgboost-lightgbm-and-catboost.html", "s": " Boosting: learn from mistakes Bagging trains trees independently and averages them (reduces variance). Boosting trains small trees sequentially : each new tree focuses on what the current ensemble gets wrong (reduces bias). Gradient boosting adds trees one at a time, each correcting the remaining error. AdaBoost (1995) reweights misclassified rows so the next learner focuses on them. Gradient boosting (Friedman, 2001) fits each new tree to the negative gradient of the loss ; for squared error, that is simply the residual. Boosting by hand Python Copy import numpy as np, pandas as pd from sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeRegressor from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split from sklearn.metrics import mean_absolute_error h = pd.read_csv ( &quot;house_prices.csv&quot; ). dropna (subset=[ &quot;area_sqft&quot; ]); h = h[ h.price_lakh.between ( 5 , 300 )] Xh = pd.get_dummies ( h.drop (columns=[ &quot;house_id&quot; , &quot;price_lakh&quot; ]), drop_first= True ). astype (float); yh = h.price_lakh Xtr, Xte, ytr, yte = train_test_split (Xh, yh, test_size= 0.2 , random_state= 0 ) lr, pred_tr, pred_te = 0.3 , np.full ( len (ytr), ytr.mean ()), np.full ( len (yte), ytr.mean ()) print ( f&quot;round 0 (mean only): test MAE {mean_absolute_error(yte, pred_te):.2f}&quot; ) for m in range ( 1 , 31 ): resid = ytr - pred_tr # negative gradient of squared error tree = DecisionTreeRegressor (max_depth= 3 , random_state= 0 ). fit (Xtr, resid) pred_tr += lr * tree.predict (Xtr); pred_te"}, {"i": "ml-20", "t": "Support Vector Machines and Naive Bayes", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-20-support-vector-machines-and-naive-bayes.html", "s": " Support vector machines: the widest street Among all lines (hyperplanes) that separate two classes, an SVM picks the one with the largest margin : the widest \"street\" between the classes. Only the points on the edge of the street, the support vectors , determine it. A linear SVM: the solid line is the decision boundary, dashed lines are the margins, circled points are the support vectors. Soft margin: the C parameter Real data overlap, so SVMs allow some points inside the margin or on the wrong side, with a penalty controlled by C : C Margin Behaviour Small (0.01) Wide Tolerates errors: simpler, more bias Large (100) Narrow Tries to classify every point: complex, more variance Kernels: non-linear boundaries The kernel trick computes similarities as if the data were mapped into a higher-dimensional space where a straight separator exists, without computing that space explicitly. Linear vs RBF kernels on curved data, and the effect of gamma (how far one point's influence reaches). Kernel Use Key parameters linear Many features (text), linearly separable data C rbf (Gaussian) Default for non-linear problems C, gamma poly Polynomial interactions degree, C Large gamma makes each point's influence very local (wiggly boundary, overfitting); small gamma gives smooth boundaries. SVM on the Bank Loans data Python Copy from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split, GridSearchCV, StratifiedKFold, cross_val_score from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline from sklearn.preprocessin"}, {"i": "ml-21", "t": "Imbalanced Classification: Fraud Detection with Class Weights, Resampling and Thresholds", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-21-imbalanced-classification-fraud-detection-with-class-weights.html", "s": " A rare-event problem Python Copy import numpy as np, pandas as pd rng = np.random.default_rng ( 7 ); n = 20000 df = pd.DataFrame ({ &quot;amount&quot; : np.round ( rng.lognormal ( 7 , 1.1 , n), 0 ), # rupees &quot;hour&quot; : rng.integers ( 0 , 24 , n), &quot;km_from_home&quot; : np.round ( rng.exponential ( 8 , n), 1 ), &quot;new_merchant&quot; : rng.random (n) &lt; 0.15 , &quot;online&quot; : rng.random (n) &lt; 0.45 , &quot;txns_last_24h&quot; : rng.poisson ( 2 , n), &quot;card_age_days&quot; : rng.integers ( 10 , 3000 , n), }) night = df.hour.isin ([ 0 , 1 , 2 , 3 , 4 ]) score = (- 8.4 + 1.35 * np.log1p (df.amount / 1000 ) + 1.5 * night + 0.075 * df.km_from_home + 1.5 * df.new_merchant + 0.75 * df.online + 0.5 * df.txns_last_24h - 0.00135 * df.card_age_days + 3.3 * (night &amp; (df.online == 1 ) &amp; (df.new_merchant == 1 )) # risky combination + 2.2 * (df.txns_last_24h &gt;= 6 ) + rng.normal ( 0 , . 4 , n)) df[ &quot;fraud&quot; ] = ( rng.random (n) &lt; 1 / ( 1 + np.exp (-score))). astype (int) df[[ &quot;new_merchant&quot; , &quot;online&quot; ]] = df[[ &quot;new_merchant&quot; , &quot;online&quot; ]]. astype (int) print (df.shape, f&quot;fraud rate {df.fraud.mean():.2%} ({df.fraud.sum()} frauds)&quot; ) print ( df.groupby ( &quot;fraud&quot; )[[ &quot;amount&quot; , &quot;km_from_home&quot; , &quot;new_merchant&quot; , &quot;online&quot; , &quot;txns_last_24h&quot; , &quot;card_age_days&quot; ]]. median ()) Output (20000, 8) fraud rate 1.38% (276 frauds) amount km_"}, {"i": "ml-22", "t": "Clustering I: K-Means and RFM Customer Segmentation", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-22-clustering-i-k-means-and-rfm-customer-segmentation.html", "s": " Why segment customers? Treating every customer the same wastes money: loyal customers get discounts they did not need, while slipping customers get nothing. Segmentation groups similar customers so each group gets the right message. Two ways to segment: business rules (RFM) and algorithms (clustering). Step 1: RFM table Recency : days since the last purchase (lower is better). Frequency : number of purchases. Monetary : total spend. Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np pd.set_option ( &quot;display.max_columns&quot; , 20 ); pd.set_option ( &quot;display.width&quot; , 160 ) tx = pd.read_csv ( &quot;retail_transactions.csv&quot; , parse_dates=[ &quot;invoice_date&quot; ]) print (tx.shape, tx.customer_id.nunique (), &quot;customers |&quot; , tx.invoice_date.min (). date (), &quot;to&quot; , tx.invoice_date.max (). date ()) snapshot = tx.invoice_date.max () + pd.Timedelta (days= 1 ) rfm = tx.groupby ( &quot;customer_id&quot; ). agg (recency=( &quot;invoice_date&quot; , lambda d: (snapshot - d.max ()).days), frequency=( &quot;invoice_no&quot; , &quot;nunique&quot; ), monetary=( &quot;amount&quot; , &quot;sum&quot; )) rfm[ &quot;monetary&quot; ] = rfm.monetary.round ( 0 ) print ( rfm.describe (). round ( 1 ).T[[ &quot;mean&quot; , &quot;50%&quot; , &quot;min&quot; , &quot;max&quot; ]]) print ( rfm.head ()) Output (4548, 4) 500 customers | 2024-01-01 to 2025-12-22 mean 50% min max recency 217.0 144.0 1.0 722.0 frequency 9.1 6.0 1.0 39.0 monetary 11058.9 5122.0 196.0 76170.0"}, {"i": "ml-23", "t": "Clustering II and Dimensionality Reduction: Hierarchical, DBSCAN, Gaussian Mixtures, PCA, t-SNE and UMAP", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-23-clustering-ii-and-dimensionality-reduction-hierarchical-dbsc.html", "s": " Hierarchical clustering Agglomerative clustering starts with every point as its own cluster and repeatedly merges the two closest clusters. The full merge history is a tree, the dendrogram : cut it at a height to get clusters, with no need to fix k in advance. Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np, matplotlib.pyplot as plt pd.set_option ( &quot;display.width&quot; , 160 ); pd.set_option ( &quot;display.max_columns&quot; , 20 ) vg = pd.read_csv ( &quot;video_game_sales.csv&quot; ) pub = vg.groupby ( &quot;genre&quot; ). agg (titles=( &quot;name&quot; , &quot;nunique&quot; ), na=( &quot;na_sales&quot; , &quot;sum&quot; ), eu=( &quot;eu_sales&quot; , &quot;sum&quot; ), jp=( &quot;jp_sales&quot; , &quot;sum&quot; ), other=( &quot;other_sales&quot; , &quot;sum&quot; ), total=( &quot;global_sales&quot; , &quot;sum&quot; )) shares = pub[[ &quot;na&quot; , &quot;eu&quot; , &quot;jp&quot; , &quot;other&quot; ]]. div (pub.total, axis= 0 ). round ( 3 ) # regional mix per genre print ( shares.join (pub[[ &quot;titles&quot; , &quot;total&quot; ]]. round ( 1 )). sort_values ( &quot;total&quot; , ascending= False ). head ( 6 )) Output na eu jp other titles total genre Adventure 0.401 0.308 0.153 0.137 186 136.7 Sports 0.430 0.348 0.063 0.160 181 133.8 Action 0.413 0.308 0.145 0.135 174 116.3 Racing 0.389 0.321 0.146 0.144 159 113.6 Strategy 0.395 0.320 0.147 0.138 182 111.5 Simulation 0.400 0.313 0.149 0.138 169 108.4 Dendrogram of game genres by regional sales mix (Ward linkage). C"}, {"i": "ml-24", "t": "Time-Series Forecasting: Decomposition, Baselines, ETS, ARIMA and ML with Lag Features", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-24-time-series-forecasting-decomposition-baselines-ets-arima-an.html", "s": " The data Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np, matplotlib.pyplot as plt, warnings warnings.filterwarnings ( &quot;ignore&quot; ) d = pd.read_csv ( &quot;daily_sales.csv&quot; , parse_dates=[ &quot;date&quot; ]). set_index ( &quot;date&quot; ). asfreq ( &quot;D&quot; ) y = d.units_sold print (y.shape, y.index.min (). date (), &quot;to&quot; , y.index.max (). date (), &quot;| missing days:&quot; , y.isna (). sum ()) print ( y.resample ( &quot;YE&quot; ). sum (). to_dict ()) print ( &quot;mean units by weekday:&quot; , y.groupby ( y.index.day_name ()). mean (). round ( 0 ). to_dict ()) Output (1461,) 2022-01-01 to 2025-12-31 | missing days: 0 {Timestamp(&#x27;2022-12-31 00:00:00&#x27;): 85031, Timestamp(&#x27;2023-12-31 00:00:00&#x27;): 100536, Timestamp(&#x27;2024-12-31 00:00:00&#x27;): 116798, Timestamp(&#x27;2025-12-31 00:00:00&#x27;): 132920} mean units by weekday: {&#x27;Friday&#x27;: 289.0, &#x27;Monday&#x27;: 288.0, &#x27;Saturday&#x27;: 320.0, &#x27;Sunday&#x27;: 323.0, &#x27;Thursday&#x27;: 288.0, &#x27;Tuesday&#x27;: 289.0, &#x27;Wednesday&#x27;: 287.0} Components of a time series STL decomposition of daily units into trend, weekly seasonality and remainder. Component Meaning Example Trend Long-term direction Sales growing about 15% a year Seasonality Repeating pattern with a fixed period Weekends higher; December peak; Diwali Cycle Longer, irregular rises and falls Economic cycles Remainder / noise What is left Random variation, one-off events Python Copy str"}, {"i": "ml-25", "t": "NLP and Text Analytics: Cleaning, TF-IDF, Sentiment Classification, Topics and Embeddings", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-25-nlp-and-text-analytics-cleaning-tf-idf-sentiment-classificat.html", "s": " The NLP pipeline A classical text-analytics pipeline. Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np, re pd.set_option ( &quot;display.width&quot; , 160 ); pd.set_option ( &quot;display.max_colwidth&quot; , 90 ) rv = pd.read_csv ( &quot;customer_reviews.csv&quot; , parse_dates=[ &quot;date&quot; ]) print (rv.shape, rv.sentiment.value_counts (normalize= True ). round ( 3 ). to_dict ()) print ( rv.groupby ( &quot;product&quot; ). rating.mean (). round ( 2 ). sort_values (). to_dict ()) print ( rv.sample ( 5 , random_state= 3 )[[ &quot;rating&quot; , &quot;review_text&quot; ]]. to_string (index= False )) rv[ &quot;n_words&quot; ] = rv.review_text.str.split (). str.len () print ( &quot;words per review:&quot; , rv.n_words.describe (). round ( 1 )[[ &quot;mean&quot; , &quot;50%&quot; , &quot;max&quot; ]]. to_dict (), &quot;| by sentiment:&quot; , rv.groupby ( &quot;sentiment&quot; ). n_words.mean (). round ( 1 ). to_dict ()) Output (3000, 8) {&#x27;positive&#x27;: 0.53, &#x27;negative&#x27;: 0.321, &#x27;neutral&#x27;: 0.149} {&#x27;Tablet&#x27;: 3.27, &#x27;Smartwatch&#x27;: 3.29, &#x27;Speaker&#x27;: 3.3, &#x27;Earbuds&#x27;: 3.31, &#x27;Smartphone&#x27;: 3.33, &#x27;Laptop&#x27;: 3.36} rating review_text 4 Superb product. Customer support solved my issue quickly, the app is totally smooth, charges really totally fast :) 5 Price is very very reasonable for these features though the buttons are loose, no issues so far 2 Worst purchase ever. Sound is tinny, not wroth it 5 Highly re"}, {"i": "ml-26", "t": "Neural Networks and Deep Learning: From a Neuron to Transformers", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-26-neural-networks-and-deep-learning-from-a-neuron-to-transform.html", "s": " A neuron An artificial neuron computes a weighted sum of its inputs plus a bias, then applies an activation function : With the sigmoid activation, one neuron is logistic regression. Stacking neurons in layers lets the network learn non-linear patterns. A multilayer perceptron: inputs, two hidden layers and an output. Every connection has a weight learned from data. Activation functions Common activation functions. ReLU is the default for hidden layers; sigmoid and softmax are used for outputs. Activation Where Why ReLU (max(0, z)) Hidden layers Simple, fast, avoids vanishing gradients GELU, SiLU Hidden layers of transformers Smooth variants of ReLU Sigmoid Binary output Probability between 0 and 1 Softmax Multiclass output Probabilities that sum to 1 None (linear) Regression output Any real value Without non-linear activations, any stack of layers collapses into a single linear model. How networks learn: backpropagation Forward pass: compute predictions layer by layer. Loss: measure the error (cross-entropy for classification, MSE for regression). Backward pass: use the chain rule to compute the gradient of the loss with respect to every weight ( backpropagation ). Update: gradient descent step on every weight (lesson 11), usually with the Adam optimiser on mini-batches. Repeat for many epochs (passes over the data). A network from scratch Python Copy from sklearn.datasets import make_moons X, y = make_moons ( 600 , noise= 0.2 , random_state= 0 ); y = y.reshape (- 1 , 1 ) r"}, {"i": "ml-27", "t": "Explainable AI and MLOps: SHAP, Fairness, Deployment, Monitoring and Responsible ML", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-27-explainable-ai-and-mlops-shap-fairness-deployment-monitoring.html", "s": " Why explainability? Stakeholders ask \" why did the model say this?\". Regulators require explanations for credit and insurance decisions; data scientists need explanations to debug leakage and bias. Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np, warnings warnings.filterwarnings ( &quot;ignore&quot; ) from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split from sklearn.ensemble import HistGradientBoostingClassifier from sklearn.metrics import roc_auc_score tc = pd.read_csv ( &quot;telecom_churn.csv&quot; ) X = pd.get_dummies ( tc.drop (columns=[ &quot;customer_id&quot; , &quot;churn&quot; , &quot;total_charges&quot; ]), drop_first= True ). astype (float); y = (tc.churn == &quot;Yes&quot; ). astype (int) Xtr, Xte, ytr, yte = train_test_split (X, y, test_size= 0.3 , stratify=y, random_state= 1 ) model = HistGradientBoostingClassifier (max_iter= 200 , learning_rate= 0.05 , max_leaf_nodes= 15 , min_samples_leaf= 40 , random_state= 0 ). fit (Xtr, ytr) print ( f&quot;test AUC {roc_auc_score(yte, model.predict_proba(Xte)[:, 1]):.3f} | features: {list(X.columns)}&quot; ) Output test AUC 0.801 | features: [&#x27;senior_citizen&#x27;, &#x27;tenure_months&#x27;, &#x27;monthly_charges&#x27;, &#x27;support_calls&#x27;, &#x27;contract_One year&#x27;, &#x27;contract_Two year&#x27;, &#x27;internet_service_Fiber&#x27;, &#x27;internet_service_No internet&#x27;, &#x27;payment_method_Card&#x27;, &#x27;payment_method_Net banking&#x27;, &#x27;payment_method_UPI&#x27;] Scope Question Methods Global Whic"}, {"i": "ml-28", "t": "ML Capstone: End-to-End Churn Prediction and Retention Strategy", "k": "Stats & ML", "f": "lessons/ml-28-ml-capstone-end-to-end-churn-prediction-and-retention-strate.html", "s": " 1. Frame the problem Brief. A telecom operator loses about a quarter of its customers every year. The retention team has a monthly budget to contact 500 customers with an offer worth ₹300 (cost ₹300 per contacted customer, accepted or not). A saved customer is worth about ₹4,000 of future margin. Historically, 35% of churners who receive the offer stay. Which customers should get the offer? Item Decision Decision Rank customers each month; contact the top N within budget Target churn = Yes (left within the observation window) Unit Customer Metric Expected campaign profit; also ROC-AUC and recall in the top N Baseline Current rule: month-to-month customers with 3+ support calls Constraints ≤ 500 contacts; explainable reasons for agents; no use of sensitive attributes for targeting Capstone workflow. 2. Data and features Python Copy import pandas as pd, numpy as np, warnings warnings.filterwarnings ( &quot;ignore&quot; ) pd.set_option ( &quot;display.width&quot; , 160 ); pd.set_option ( &quot;display.max_columns&quot; , 20 ) tc = pd.read_csv ( &quot;telecom_churn.csv&quot; ) y = (tc.churn == &quot;Yes&quot; ). astype (int) print (tc.shape, f&quot;churn rate {y.mean():.3f}&quot; ) def add_features (df): out = df.copy () out[ &quot;is_new&quot; ] = (out.tenure_months &lt;= 6 ). astype (int) out[ &quot;charge_per_tenure_month&quot; ] = out.total_charges / (out.tenure_months + 1 ) out[ &quot;price_change_proxy&quot; ] = out.monthly_charges - out.charge_per_tenure_month # charges n"}, {"i": "genai-01", "t": "How Large Language Models Work: Tokens, Embeddings, Transformers and Training", "k": "GenAI & Agents", "f": "lessons/genai-01-how-large-language-models-work-tokens-embeddings-transformer.html", "s": " An LLM in one sentence A large language model is a neural network trained to predict the next token given the previous ones. Doing that very well on trillions of words forces it to learn grammar, facts, reasoning patterns and styles, which instruction tuning then turns into a helpful assistant. Text generation is a loop: tokenise, predict a probability for every possible next token, sample one, append it, repeat. Tokens Models do not read characters or whole words but tokens : frequent words, word pieces and punctuation. English averages roughly 4 characters (¾ of a word) per token; Indian languages and code usually need more tokens per word. Python Copy import re, collections, pandas as pd, numpy as np corpus = pd.read_csv ( &quot;customer_reviews.csv&quot; ). review_text.str.lower (). tolist () def bpe_train (texts, merges= 60 ): words = collections.Counter (w for t in texts for w in re.findall (r &quot;[a-z&#x27;]+&quot; , t)) vocab = { tuple (w) + ( &quot;&lt;/w&gt;&quot; ,): c for w, c in words.items ()} rules = [] for _ in range (merges): pairs = collections.Counter () for sym, c in vocab.items (): for a, b in zip (sym, sym[ 1 :]): pairs[a, b] += c best = max (pairs, key=pairs.get); rules.append (best) new = {} for sym, c in vocab.items (): out, i = [], 0 while i &lt; len (sym): if i &lt; len (sym) - 1 and (sym[i], sym[i + 1 ]) == best: out.append (sym[i] + sym[i + 1 ]); i += 2 else : out.append (sym[i]); i += 1 new[ tuple (out)] = c vocab = new return rules def bpe_en"}, {"i": "genai-02", "t": "Prompt Engineering in Depth: Patterns, Templates, Few-Shot, Reasoning and Structured Output", "k": "GenAI & Agents", "f": "lessons/genai-02-prompt-engineering-in-depth-patterns-templates-few-shot-reas.html", "s": " From chat prompts to production prompts In chat you can fix a weak answer by asking again. In a product the same prompt runs thousands of times on inputs you never saw, so it must be clear, specific, robust and testable . The building blocks of a production prompt. Be clear and specific Vague Specific \"Summarise this review.\" \"Summarise this customer review in one sentence for a product manager. Mention the product aspect and whether the customer is satisfied.\" \"Analyse the sales data.\" \"Using the table below, find the three sub-categories with the lowest profit margin in 2025 and suggest one cause for each. Use only the data provided.\" \"Write SQL.\" \"Write a DuckDB SQL query over table sales(order_date DATE, region TEXT, sales DOUBLE) that returns monthly sales by region for 2025, ordered by month. Return only the SQL.\" Golden rule (from Anthropic's guide): show the prompt to a colleague with no context. If they would be confused, the model will be too. Separate instructions from data Wrap documents, user input and examples in XML tags or clear delimiters. This helps the model tell instructions from content and reduces prompt-injection risk. Python Copy import json, re, textwrap, pandas as pd, numpy as np SYSTEM = &quot;&quot;&quot;You are a customer-insights analyst for an Indian electronics retailer. You classify customer reviews accurately and never invent facts that are not in the review.&quot;&quot;&quot; TEMPLATE = &quot;&quot;&quot;Classify the review inside &lt;revie"}, {"i": "genai-03", "t": "Calling LLM APIs from Python: Messages, Streaming, Structured Output, Costs and Reliability", "k": "GenAI & Agents", "f": "lessons/genai-03-calling-llm-apis-from-python-messages-streaming-structured-o.html", "s": " Setup Text Copy pip install anthropic # official SDK (also: openai, google-genai) # Never paste keys into notebooks. Set an environment variable instead: export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=&quot;sk-ant-...&quot; # macOS/Linux setx ANTHROPIC_API_KEY &quot;sk-ant-...&quot; # Windows (new terminal afterwards) # or put it in a .env file listed in .gitignore and load it with python-dotenv Get a key from the provider console (console.anthropic.com, platform.openai.com, aistudio.google.com), set a monthly spend limit , and create separate keys for development and production. Your first request Python Copy import anthropic client = anthropic.Anthropic () # reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from the environment response = client.messages.create ( model= &quot;claude-opus-5-5&quot; , # current default model; claude-sonnet-5-5 / claude-haiku-5-5 are cheaper max_tokens= 16000 , # upper limit on output tokens system= &quot;You are a concise data analyst. Answer in at most 3 bullet points.&quot; , messages=[{ &quot;role&quot; : &quot;user&quot; , &quot;content&quot; : &quot;Why might a churn model have high accuracy but low recall?&quot; }], ) for block in response.content: # the reply is a list of content blocks if block.type == &quot;text&quot; : print (block.text) print (response.stop_reason, response.usage.input_tokens, response.usage.output_tokens) Field Meaning model Which model answers system Standing instructions: role, rules, format messages The conversation: alternating user / assistant turns max_t"}, {"i": "genai-04", "t": "Embeddings and Vector Search: Meaning as Numbers, Similarity and Vector Databases", "k": "GenAI & Agents", "f": "lessons/genai-04-embeddings-and-vector-search-meaning-as-numbers-similarity-a.html", "s": " What is an embedding? An embedding is a list of numbers (a vector) that represents the meaning of a word, sentence, image or product. Models are trained so that similar meanings produce nearby vectors . Embeddings place items in a space where distance reflects meaning: complaints about charging cluster together, praise for displays clusters elsewhere. Real embeddings have 256–3,072 dimensions. Uses: semantic search, RAG (next lesson), recommendations (\"similar products\"), clustering and de-duplication, classification features, anomaly detection. Learning word embeddings from scratch \"You shall know a word by the company it keeps\" (J. R. Firth). Words that appear in similar contexts get similar vectors. Word2Vec and GloVe learn this with neural networks; the classic PPMI + SVD method below captures the same idea in a few lines. Python Copy import re, collections, pandas as pd from sklearn.decomposition import TruncatedSVD from scipy.sparse import coo_matrix rv = pd.read_csv ( &quot;customer_reviews.csv&quot; ) docs = [ re.findall (r &quot;[a-z]+&quot; , t.lower ()) for t in rv.review_text] counts = collections.Counter (w for d in docs for w in d) vocab = [w for w, c in counts.items () if c &gt;= 5 ]; idx = {w: i for i, w in enumerate (vocab)} pairs = collections.Counter () for d in docs: ids = [idx[w] for w in d if w in idx] for i, a in enumerate (ids): for b in ids[ max ( 0 , i - 3 ): i] + ids[i + 1 : i + 4 ]: pairs[a, b] += 1 # co-occurrence within ±3 words r, c, v = zip (*"}, {"i": "genai-05", "t": "Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG): Build, Evaluate and Improve a Q&A Assistant", "k": "GenAI & Agents", "f": "lessons/genai-05-retrieval-augmented-generation-rag-build-evaluate-and-improv.html", "s": " Why RAG? LLMs do not know your company's policies, product catalogue or yesterday's report, and they may hallucinate. Retrieval-augmented generation retrieves the most relevant passages for each question and gives them to the model with an instruction to answer only from them, with citations . RAG has an offline indexing pipeline and an online question-answering pipeline. Approach Good for Limits Put everything in the prompt (long context) A few documents, one-off analysis Cost and latency grow with every call; no access control RAG Large, changing knowledge; citations; access control Retrieval quality limits answer quality Fine-tuning Style, format, narrow skills Poor at injecting facts; costly to update Tools / SQL Live numbers and transactions Needs structured sources The knowledge base Our example: NovaMart , an online electronics retailer. The assistant answers customer-support questions from the policy handbook. Python Copy import re, numpy as np, pandas as pd DOCS = { &quot;returns.md&quot; : &quot;&quot;&quot;# Returns policy ## Return window Most products can be returned within 10 days of delivery. Mobiles and laptops have a 7-day replacement-only window for manufacturing defects. ## Conditions Products must be unused, with original box, accessories and invoice. Opened earbuds and personal-care items cannot be returned for hygiene reasons. ## Refund timeline Refunds are issued to the original payment method within 5–7 working days after the returned product passes q"}, {"i": "genai-06", "t": "Tool Use and Function Calling: Letting LLMs Query Data, Call APIs and Take Actions", "k": "GenAI & Agents", "f": "lessons/genai-06-tool-use-and-function-calling-letting-llms-query-data-call-a.html", "s": " Why tools? LLMs cannot see your database, today's exchange rate or your calendar, and they are unreliable at arithmetic. Tool use (function calling) lets the model ask your code to do these things: the model decides which tool to call with which arguments ; your code runs it and returns the result; the model uses the result to answer. The tool-use loop: the model requests a tool, your application executes it, and the result goes back to the model until it can answer. Defining tools A tool is a name, a description (the model reads it to decide when to use the tool) and a JSON Schema for the input. Python Copy import json, re, duckdb, pandas as pd, numpy as np sales = pd.read_csv ( &quot;superstore_sales.csv&quot; , parse_dates=[ &quot;order_date&quot; ]) con = duckdb.connect (); con.register ( &quot;sales&quot; , sales) TOOLS = [ { &quot;name&quot; : &quot;run_sql&quot; , &quot;description&quot; : &quot;Run a read-only DuckDB SQL query on table `sales` (one row per order line) and return up to 20 rows as JSON. &quot; &quot;Columns: order_id, order_date (DATE), ship_date, ship_mode, customer_id, segment, city, state, region, category, sub_category, &quot; &quot;product_name, sales (INR), quantity, discount (0-1), profit (INR). Use this for any question about sales data.&quot; , &quot;input_schema&quot; : { &quot;type&quot; : &quot;object&quot; , &quot;properties&quot; : { &quot;sql&quot; : { &quot;type&quot; : &quot;string&quot; , &quot;description&quot; : &quot;A single SELEC"}, {"i": "genai-07", "t": "AI Agents and Agentic AI: Loops, Planning, Memory, Multi-Agent Systems and When to Use Them", "k": "GenAI & Agents", "f": "lessons/genai-07-ai-agents-and-agentic-ai-loops-planning-memory-multi-agent-s.html", "s": " AI, GenAI and agentic AI Traditional AI / ML Generative AI Agentic AI Does Predicts or classifies Creates content (text, code, images) Pursues a goal through multi-step actions Example Churn score Draft a retention email Find at-risk customers, draft personalised offers, schedule them, report results Control Fixed pipeline One request → one response Model decides the next step in a loop, using tools Workflows vs agents Increasing autonomy: start with the simplest option that works. Workflow: your code decides the path (\"extract → classify → if complaint, draft reply\"). Predictable, cheap, easy to test. Agent: the model decides the path at run time: which tool, which order, when to stop. Flexible for open-ended tasks, but costlier, slower and harder to test. Anthropic's guidance (\"Building effective agents\"): use the simplest pattern that works; reach for agents when the task is open-ended, multi-step, valuable enough to justify cost and latency, and errors can be caught (tests, reviews, rollback). The agent loop Every agent is a loop around a model with tools and memory. A runnable data-analysis agent The agent below answers business questions about the sales data. A real LLM would choose each action; here a transparent rule-based \"brain\" plays that role so the loop runs offline, but the structure (tools, scratchpad, step limit, final answer) is the same as in production frameworks. Python Copy import json, re, duckdb, pandas as pd, numpy as np sales = pd.read_csv ( &quot;su"}, {"i": "genai-08", "t": "Model Context Protocol (MCP): Connecting AI Assistants to Your Data and Tools", "k": "GenAI & Agents", "f": "lessons/genai-08-model-context-protocol-mcp-connecting-ai-assistants-to-your.html", "s": " The problem MCP solves Before MCP, every AI app needed custom code for every integration: N apps × M tools connectors. MCP (introduced by Anthropic in November 2024, now an open standard supported across Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, Microsoft Copilot, VS Code, Cursor and many others) defines one protocol : build a server once, and every MCP-capable assistant can use it, like a USB-C port for AI. MCP architecture: a host application runs one MCP client per server; servers expose tools, resources and prompts over a standard protocol. Role What it is Example Host The AI application the user works in Claude Desktop, Claude Code, VS Code, a custom chatbot Client Connector inside the host, one per server Created by the host Server A program exposing capabilities \"NovaMart sales\" server, GitHub, Slack, Postgres, Google Drive The three primitives Primitive Controlled by Purpose Example Tools The model (with user approval) Actions and computations run_sql , create_ticket , send_email Resources The application/user Read-only context the host can attach A policy document, a table schema, a file Prompts The user Reusable prompt templates (often shown as slash commands) /monthly-review , /explain-query Clients can also offer capabilities to servers, such as sampling (the server asks the host's model to generate text) and elicitation (the server asks the user for input). Build an MCP server The official Python SDK makes a server a few decorated functions. Type hints and docstrings become the "}, {"i": "genai-09", "t": "Evaluating LLM Applications and Building Guardrails: Test Sets, LLM-as-Judge, Injection, PII and Safety", "k": "GenAI & Agents", "f": "lessons/genai-09-evaluating-llm-applications-and-building-guardrails-test-set.html", "s": " Why evaluation is the core skill LLM outputs vary with wording, model version and input. Without measurement, \"it looked good in my three tests\" becomes a production incident. Eval-driven development : define what good looks like, build a test set, measure every change. The evaluation loop for LLM applications. Building the evaluation set Source Why Real user inputs (logs, tickets, emails) Representative Edge cases Mixed sentiment, sarcasm, Hinglish, typos, very long or empty inputs Adversarial cases Prompt injection, requests for disallowed content, PII Unanswerable questions Tests abstention (\"I don't know\") Past failures Every bug becomes a test Start with 30–100 labelled examples ; grow it as you find failures. Keep a held-out set you do not tune on. Metrics by task Task Metric Classification / routing Accuracy, macro F1, confusion matrix Extraction to JSON Schema validity rate, field-level exact match Text-to-SQL Execution accuracy (same result as gold query) RAG answers Correctness, faithfulness, citation accuracy, abstention (lesson 5) Open generation (summaries, emails) Rubric scores by humans or an LLM judge; pairwise preference Agents Task success rate, steps, cost, safety violations Operations Latency p50/p95, cost per task, refusal and error rates Field-level scoring for extraction Python Copy import json, re, pandas as pd, numpy as np gold = [{ &quot;product&quot; : &quot;Laptop&quot; , &quot;issue&quot; : &quot;battery&quot; , &quot;urgency&quot; : &quot;high&q"}, {"i": "genai-10", "t": "Customising Models: Prompting vs RAG vs Fine-Tuning, LoRA, Distillation and Open-Weight Models", "k": "GenAI & Agents", "f": "lessons/genai-10-customising-models-prompting-vs-rag-vs-fine-tuning-lora-dist.html", "s": " The customisation ladder Climb only as far as you need: each step costs more to build and maintain. From cheapest to most expensive ways to adapt an LLM. Need Best first approach Model lacks your knowledge (policies, products, documents) RAG Needs live data or must act Tools / MCP Must follow a format or style consistently Prompt + examples + structured outputs; fine-tune if still unreliable A narrow task at huge volume must be cheap and fast Fine-tune or distil a small model Specialised domain language (legal Hindi, medical codes) Better model choice, RAG; then fine-tune Model must run on-premises / offline Open-weight model (optionally fine-tuned) Watch out Fine-tuning is not a knowledge database Fine-tuning teaches patterns (tone, format, task behaviour). It is unreliable for injecting facts, which change and need citations: use RAG for knowledge. Most teams that \"need fine-tuning\" first need better prompts, examples and evaluation. Preparing fine-tuning data Fine-tuning datasets are examples of the exact behaviour you want, usually in chat format (JSONL: one conversation per line). Python Copy import json, numpy as np, pandas as pd rv = pd.read_csv ( &quot;customer_reviews.csv&quot; ) SYSTEM = &quot;Classify the review&#x27;s sentiment as positive, negative or neutral. Reply with one word.&quot; def to_example (text, label): return { &quot;messages&quot; : [{ &quot;role&quot; : &quot;system&quot; , &quot;content&quot; : SYSTEM}, { &quot;role&quot; : &quot;user&quot; , &q"}, {"i": "genai-11", "t": "GenAI for Data Work: Text-to-SQL, AI Coding Assistants, Data Cleaning and Automated Insights", "k": "GenAI & Agents", "f": "lessons/genai-11-genai-for-data-work-text-to-sql-ai-coding-assistants-data-cl.html", "s": " Where GenAI helps analysts The analyst's workflow with AI assistance at each step, and the human checks that keep it reliable. Task Typical tool Write and explain SQL Claude/ChatGPT/Gemini, BigQuery and Snowflake copilots, Metabase AI, MinusX Write Python, fix errors, build charts Claude Code, GitHub Copilot, Cursor, Gemini in Colab, Jupyter AI Clean messy text and categories LLM calls with validation, fuzzy matching Extract fields from documents (invoices, emails) LLM structured outputs, document AI services Summarise dashboards and write reports Copilot in Power BI, Excel Copilot/Gemini, LLM APIs Ask questions of data in plain English Text-to-SQL apps, BI \"Q&amp;A\" features, data agents Text-to-SQL The model needs the schema , business definitions and rules ; your code must keep execution read-only and validate results. Python Copy import re, json, duckdb, pandas as pd, numpy as np sales = pd.read_csv ( &quot;superstore_sales.csv&quot; , parse_dates=[ &quot;order_date&quot; , &quot;ship_date&quot; ]) con = duckdb.connect (); con.register ( &quot;sales&quot; , sales) schema = &quot;\\n&quot; . join ( f&quot; {c} {t}&quot; for c, t, *_ in con.execute ( &quot;DESCRIBE sales&quot; ). fetchall ()) PROMPT = f&quot;&quot; &quot;You write DuckDB SQL for an Indian office-supplies retailer. &lt;schema&gt; TABLE sales (one row per order line) {schema} &lt;/schema&gt; &lt;definitions&gt; - Revenue = SUM (sales); profit margin % = 100 * SUM (profit) / SUM (sales). - An order is a distin"}, {"i": "genai-12", "t": "Building and Shipping a GenAI App: Architecture, Streamlit/FastAPI, Cost, Observability and Responsible AI (Capstone)", "k": "GenAI & Agents", "f": "lessons/genai-12-building-and-shipping-a-genai-app-architecture-streamlit-fas.html", "s": " Reference architecture Components of a production GenAI application. Capstone brief: NovaMart support assistant Answer customer questions from the policy handbook (RAG, lesson 5), look up order status with a tool (lesson 6), refuse out-of-scope or unsafe requests, hand over to a human when unsure, and measure quality (lesson 9). Requirement Design Accurate policy answers with sources Hybrid retrieval + citations, \"answer only from documents\" Order status get_order(order_id) tool, read-only, customer must be authenticated Refunds above ₹5,000 Tool creates a ticket for a human; the model never approves money PII Redact before logging; no card numbers in prompts Quality 60-question eval set, weekly regression, thumbs up/down Cost Small model for routing/classification, larger model for complex answers, prompt caching Chat front end (Streamlit) Python Copy # app.py -&gt; streamlit run app.py import streamlit as st, requests st.title ( &quot;NovaMart Help&quot; ) if &quot;history&quot; not in st.session_state: st.session_state.history = [] for m in st.session_state.history: st.chat_message (m[ &quot;role&quot; ]). write (m[ &quot;content&quot; ]) if q := st.chat_input ( &quot;Ask about orders, returns, delivery…&quot; ): st.chat_message ( &quot;user&quot; ). write (q) r = requests.post ( &quot;http://localhost:8000/chat&quot; , json={ &quot;session_id&quot; : st.session_state.get ( &quot;sid&quot; , &quot;demo&quot; ), &quot;message&quot; : q}, timeout= 60 ). json () st.chat_mess"}, {"i": "agents-01", "t": "APIs Without Code: How Apps Talk, REST, JSON, Authentication, Webhooks and Postman for PMs and BAs", "k": "Agents", "f": "lessons/agents-01-apis-without-code-how-apps-talk-rest-json-authentication-web.html", "s": " What an API is An API (Application Programming Interface) is a contract that lets one app ask another for data or actions. When the Swiggy app shows a restaurant's live wait time, it calls an API; when Zomato sends you an SMS, it calls an SMS provider's API; when your payment goes through, the app calls Razorpay's API. Every API call is a request and a response. The parts of a request Part Example Meaning Method GET , POST , PUT / PATCH , DELETE Read, create, update, delete Base URL + path (endpoint) https://api.example.com/v1/orders/123 Which resource Query parameters ?status=paid&amp;limit=20 Filters, sorting, paging Headers Authorization: Bearer sk_live_… , Content-Type: application/json Who you are, what format Body {\"amount\": 49900, \"currency\": \"INR\"} Data you send (POST/PUT/PATCH) Status codes every PM should know Code Meaning What to put in the PRD 200 OK / 201 Created Success Show the result / confirmation 400 Bad Request Invalid input Field-level validation message 401 Unauthorized / 403 Forbidden Not logged in / not allowed Ask to log in / hide the action 404 Not Found Resource does not exist Friendly empty state 409 Conflict Duplicate or version clash \"Already exists\" handling 429 Too Many Requests Rate limit hit Retry later, queue, back-off 500 / 502 / 503 Server problem Retry with back-off, show status, alert on-call JSON: the language of APIs Python Copy import json response = &#x27;&#x27;&#x27;{&quot;order_id&quot;: &quot;ORD-1029&quot;, &quot;status&quot;: &q"}, {"i": "agents-02", "t": "MCP (Model Context Protocol) Without Code: Connect Claude to Your Apps and Data", "k": "Agents", "f": "lessons/agents-02-mcp-model-context-protocol-without-code-connect-claude-to-yo.html", "s": " Why MCP exists Before MCP, every AI app needed a custom integration for every tool: M apps × N tools. MCP , an open standard introduced by Anthropic in November 2024 and now supported by Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, Microsoft Copilot Studio, Cursor, VS Code and many others, works like USB-C for AI : build an MCP server for a tool once, and any MCP-capable assistant can use it. MCP turns M x N custom integrations into M + N. The architecture in plain words Part What it is Example Host The AI app the user talks to Claude Desktop, claude.ai, Claude Code, Cursor Client The connector inside the host that speaks MCP to one server One per connected server Server A small program that exposes a system's capabilities GitHub, Google Drive, Postgres, your CRM, an n8n workflow Tools Actions the model can call create_issue , search_files , send_message Resources Read-only data the app can attach as context A file, a database schema, a ticket Prompts Reusable prompt templates offered by the server \"Summarise this sprint\" Transport How client and server talk Local (stdio) or remote over HTTP (Streamable HTTP) with OAuth What happens when you ask Claude a question that needs a tool. Using MCP in Claude without code 1. Built-in connectors (easiest) In Claude (web or desktop): Settings → Connectors (or the \"+\" / tools menu in a chat). Connect Google Drive, Gmail, Google Calendar, GitHub, Slack, Notion, Asana, Atlassian (Jira/Confluence), Linear, Canva, Figma and others from the directory. Each con"}, {"i": "agents-03", "t": "How AI Agents Work and How to Build One Without Code", "k": "Agents", "f": "lessons/agents-03-how-ai-agents-work-and-how-to-build-one-without-code.html", "s": " What makes something an agent A chatbot answers. An agent pursues a goal: it decides which steps to take, uses tools, looks at the results and keeps going until the goal is met or it needs a human. The agent loop. Python Copy # A toy agent loop: the &quot;model&quot; chooses tools until the goal is met. Real agents let the LLM make these choices. inventory = { &quot;SKU-1&quot; : 0 , &quot;SKU-2&quot; : 14 } def check_stock (sku): return inventory[sku] def create_po (sku, qty): return f&quot;PO created for {qty} x {sku}&quot; goal_skus, log = [ &quot;SKU-1&quot; , &quot;SKU-2&quot; ], [] for sku in goal_skus: # plan: check each SKU stock = check_stock (sku); log.append ( f&quot;check_stock({sku}) -&gt; {stock}&quot; ) # act + observe if stock &lt; 5 : log.append ( create_po (sku, 50 )) # decide next action from the observation log.append ( &quot;Done: low-stock items reordered; summary sent to the manager&quot; ) print ( &quot;\\n&quot; . join (log)) Output check_stock(SKU-1) -&gt; 0 PO created for 50 x SKU-1 check_stock(SKU-2) -&gt; 14 Done: low-stock items reordered; summary sent to the manager The building blocks Block What it is Example Model The LLM that reasons Claude Sonnet for most agents, a smaller model for routing Instructions Role, goal, rules, tone, when to stop or escalate \"You are a refunds assistant. Never approve above ₹5,000; create a ticket instead.\" Tools Actions via APIs or MCP Search orders, create ticket, send email Knowledge Documents the agent retriev"}, {"i": "agents-04", "t": "Connect All Your Apps with Claude, n8n, Make, Zapier and Replit", "k": "Agents", "f": "lessons/agents-04-connect-all-your-apps-with-claude-n8n-make-zapier-and-replit.html", "s": " Four ways to connect apps with AI Four integration patterns, from simplest to most flexible. Pattern Best for Example Claude + connectors One person, ad-hoc questions and actions \"Draft replies to customer emails using our refund policy\" Automation calls Claude Scheduled or event-driven, many records Classify every support ticket as it arrives Claude calls automations Conversational control of business systems \"Add this lead to the CRM and book a demo\" Custom app A team tool with its own UI and data Internal \"contract review\" portal Choosing the platform Need Pick Fast, simple, many SaaS apps, non-technical team Zapier Visual branching, data mapping, good price per operation Make Self-hosting, code steps, AI agents, high volume n8n A full app with UI, database and logic Replit (or Lovable, Bolt) Engineering-owned integration in your codebase Claude Code Claude as your integration designer Ask Claude to do the hard thinking before you click: Copy I need an automation: when a Typeform demo request arrives, enrich the company from Clearbit-like data, score the lead (company size, role, budget), add to HubSpot, alert #sales in Slack for scores above 70, and email a booking link. Platform: n8n Cloud. 1) Draw the flow as a list of nodes with what each does. 2) Give the field mapping table. 3) List failure cases and how to handle each. 4) Give me the n8n Code node for the scoring logic. Claude can also read an API reference page and produce the HTTP Request settings, explain an err"}, {"i": "agents-05", "t": "Claude Code Complete Tutorial: From First Prompt to Agents, MCP, Hooks and Automation", "k": "Agents", "f": "lessons/agents-05-claude-code-complete-tutorial-from-first-prompt-to-agents-mc.html", "s": " What Claude Code is Claude Code is an agent that works inside your project: it reads files, runs commands, edits code, runs tests and opens pull requests, while you watch and approve. It runs in the terminal , in VS Code / JetBrains , in the Claude desktop app , on the web (cloud sessions connected to GitHub) and on mobile, and it is used by non-engineers too: PMs build prototypes and analyses, analysts clean data and build reports. Where you can use Claude Code. Install and first session Step by step Get started Install: follow the current command on the Claude Code docs page (native installer for macOS/Linux/Windows, or npm install -g @anthropic-ai/claude-code with Node.js 18+). Open a terminal in your project folder and run claude . Sign in with your Claude subscription or an API key. Ask: \"Explain this project's structure and how to run it.\" Claude reads files and summarises. Ask for a small change: \"Add input validation to the signup form and a test for it.\" Review each proposed edit and command, then approve. Run /init to create a CLAUDE.md with build commands and conventions for future sessions. Context: CLAUDE.md and how to brief Claude CLAUDE.md files are loaded automatically: the project one (shared in git), personal ones ( ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md ) and folder-level ones. Put in them: how to build and test, code style, architecture notes, \"never do\" rules, and how to publish. Keep them short and specific. Good prompts give the goal, constraints and how to verify: Copy "}, {"i": "agents-06", "t": "Voice Agents and Voice Cloning: Tools, Architecture, Build Steps and Responsible Use", "k": "Agents", "f": "lessons/agents-06-voice-agents-and-voice-cloning-tools-architecture-build-step.html", "s": " How a voice agent works A voice agent pipeline. Speech-to-speech models merge some of these steps. Conversations feel natural only when the reply starts in well under a second and the agent handles turn-taking (knowing when the caller has finished), barge-in (stopping when interrupted) and background noise. Python Copy budget = { &quot;telephony + network&quot; : 120 , &quot;speech-to-text (streaming)&quot; : 200 , &quot;LLM first token&quot; : 350 , &quot;text-to-speech first audio&quot; : 150 } total = sum ( budget.values ()) for k, v in budget.items (): print ( f&quot;{k:28s}{v:5d} ms&quot; ) print ( f&quot;{&#x27;time to first audio&#x27;:28s}{total:5d} ms -&gt;&quot; , &quot;feels natural&quot; if total &lt;= 1000 else &quot;feels slow&quot; ) Output telephony + network 120 ms speech-to-text (streaming) 200 ms LLM first token 350 ms text-to-speech first audio 150 ms time to first audio 820 ms -&gt; feels natural Ways to cut latency: streaming at every step, a faster model for the conversation (a bigger one only for hard cases), short replies, prompt caching, and servers close to your callers (India region where available). Platforms Tool What it is best for ElevenLabs Very natural text-to-speech, voice cloning, dubbing; ElevenLabs Agents platform for voice agents with tools and knowledge Vapi Developer-friendly voice-agent platform: bring your own STT/LLM/TTS, tools, phone numbers, squads of agents Retell AI Production phone agents with a visual flow builder, call analy"}, {"i": "tools-01", "t": "Figma Complete Tutorial: Interface, Auto Layout, Components, Variables, Prototypes, Dev Mode and Figma AI", "k": "Tools", "f": "lessons/tools-01-figma-complete-tutorial-interface-auto-layout-components-var.html", "s": " What Figma is and why every product and data team uses it Figma is a browser-based design tool where many people work on the same file at the same time. Product managers sketch flows, designers build interfaces and design systems, developers inspect measurements and code, and data analysts design dashboards before building them in Power BI or Tableau. The Figma family. One account, one file format, different products for different jobs. Plan (2026) Who it is for Starter (free) Learning: a limited number of Figma Design and FigJam files, unlimited personal drafts, free viewers Professional Freelancers and small teams: unlimited files, team libraries, Dev Mode Organization / Enterprise Companies: design-system analytics, branching, SSO, admin controls Education (free) Verified students and teachers get Professional features Tip Start free Sign up at figma.com with Google, then install the desktop app (optional; the browser works the same). Students should apply for the free Education plan. The interface The Figma Design editor. Essential shortcuts: Action Windows Mac Frame / Rectangle / Ellipse / Text F / R / O / T F / R / O / T Duplicate Ctrl + D Cmd + D Group / Ungroup Ctrl + G / Ctrl + Shift + G Cmd + G / Cmd + Shift + G Add auto layout Shift + A Shift + A Create component Ctrl + Alt + K Cmd + Option + K Show measurements hold Alt and hover hold Option and hover Zoom to fit / to selection Shift + 1 / Shift + 2 Shift + 1 / Shift + 2 Quick actions (search any command) Ctrl + "}, {"i": "tools-02", "t": "n8n Complete Tutorial: Workflow Automation, Data Pipelines and AI Agents", "k": "Tools", "f": "lessons/tools-02-n8n-complete-tutorial-workflow-automation-data-pipelines-and.html", "s": " What n8n is n8n (\"n-eight-n\", from nodemation ) connects apps and data with visual workflows. Each workflow starts with a trigger (a form is submitted, a schedule fires, a webhook is called) and passes data through nodes that read, transform and send it. Compared with Zapier and Make, n8n can be self-hosted (your data stays on your server), lets you write code in any step, and has strong AI-agent nodes. Zapier Make n8n Hosting Cloud only Cloud only Cloud or self-hosted (Docker, npm) Pricing unit Tasks (each step) Operations Workflow executions (cloud); free to self-host under its fair-code licence Code Code steps (limited) Limited Full JavaScript/Python Code node, npm packages when self-hosted Best for Quick app-to-app zaps Visual scenarios with routers Data pipelines, AI agents, privacy-sensitive automations Getting n8n Option How n8n Cloud Sign up at n8n.io → free trial → you get yourname.app.n8n.cloud Docker (recommended self-host) docker volume create n8n_data then docker run -it --rm -p 5678:5678 -v n8n_data:/home/node/.n8n docker.n8n.io/n8nio/n8n and open http://localhost:5678 npm (quick local test) npx n8n (needs Node.js) Production self-host Docker Compose with PostgreSQL, a domain with HTTPS (Caddy/Nginx), N8N_ENCRYPTION_KEY set and backed up, queue mode with Redis for scale The editor and the core ideas An n8n workflow: a trigger, then nodes that pass items from left to right. Concept Meaning Workflow The canvas of connected nodes; Active workflows run on their tri"}, {"i": "career-01", "t": "Portfolio, GitHub and Resume for Data Roles", "k": "Career", "f": "lessons/career-01-portfolio-github-and-resume-for-data-roles.html", "s": " What hiring managers look for A portfolio answers three questions for a recruiter in under two minutes. Target role Must-have evidence Data / business analyst SQL case study, Excel model, Power BI dashboard, EDA with recommendations Data scientist EDA, A/B test analysis, ML model with business threshold, explainability Data engineer Pipeline (ingest → transform → warehouse), dbt/SQL models, orchestration, tests GenAI / AI engineer RAG assistant with evaluation, tool-using agent, deployed demo, cost analysis Pick 3–5 projects (from this handbook) SQL: end-to-end sales case study (SQL lesson 15). Power BI: Adventure Cycles dashboard with DAX and drill-through (Power BI capstone). Python EDA: telecom churn EDA with recommendations (Python lesson 22) or retail analytics capstone (Python lesson 29). ML: churn prediction with profit-based threshold and SHAP (ML lesson 28). GenAI: NovaMart RAG support assistant with evaluation and guardrails (GenAI lessons 5, 9, 12). Make at least one project your own: a dataset from data.gov.in, Kaggle or your workplace (anonymised) about a domain you care about. GitHub structure and README Text Copy churn-prediction/ ├── README.md ← the most important file ├── data/README.md ← source, licence, how to download (do not commit large/private data) ├── notebooks/01_eda.ipynb ├── src/features.py, train.py, evaluate.py ├── reports/figures/ ← 2–4 key charts used in the README ├── requirements.txt └── app/ (optional Streamlit demo) README template: Proble"}, {"i": "career-02", "t": "Data Analyst Interview Preparation: SQL, Excel, Power BI, Statistics and Case Questions", "k": "Career", "f": "lessons/career-02-data-analyst-interview-preparation-sql-excel-power-bi-statis.html", "s": " The interview loop A typical analyst hiring process. SQL patterns you must know Python Copy import duckdb, pandas as pd sales = pd.read_csv ( &quot;superstore_sales.csv&quot; , parse_dates=[ &quot;order_date&quot; ]) print ( duckdb.sql ( &quot;&quot;&quot; -- Top 2 products by sales in each region (window function) WITH r AS ( SELECT region, product_name, SUM(sales) AS sales, RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY region ORDER BY SUM(sales) DESC) AS rnk FROM sales GROUP BY region, product_name) SELECT region, product_name, ROUND(sales) AS sales FROM r WHERE rnk &lt;= 2 ORDER BY region, rnk; &quot;&quot;&quot; ). df ()) Output region product_name sales 0 East Apex Copier 347 4191180.0 1 East Zenith Copier 719 3323372.0 2 North Orbit Copier 746 3790940.0 3 North Zenith Machine 401 3566453.0 4 South Orbit Copier 746 5405517.0 5 South Vertex Copier 262 4258600.0 6 West Zenith Copier 719 4087068.0 7 West Zenith Machine 401 4023468.0 Python Copy import duckdb, pandas as pd sales = pd.read_csv ( &quot;superstore_sales.csv&quot; , parse_dates=[ &quot;order_date&quot; ]) print ( duckdb.sql ( &quot;&quot;&quot; -- Month-over-month growth with LAG WITH m AS (SELECT DATE_TRUNC(&#x27;month&#x27;, order_date) AS month, SUM(sales) AS sales FROM sales GROUP BY 1) SELECT month, ROUND(sales) AS sales, ROUND(100.0 * (sales - LAG(sales) OVER (ORDER BY month)) / LAG(sales) OVER (ORDER BY month), 1) AS mom_pct FROM m ORDER BY month DESC LIMIT 4; &quot;&quot;&quot; ). df ()) Output month sales mom_pct 0 2025-1"}, {"i": "career-03", "t": "Data Scientist, ML and GenAI Interview Preparation: Questions and Model Answers", "k": "Career", "f": "lessons/career-03-data-scientist-ml-and-genai-interview-preparation-questions.html", "s": " Python and pandas (quick answers) Question Answer List vs tuple vs set vs dict? Mutable sequence / immutable record / unique unordered / key→value; sets and dicts give O(1) lookups apply vs vectorisation? Vectorised column operations run in C and are much faster; avoid apply(axis=1) on large data merge vs join vs concat ? merge on columns, join on index, concat stacks How to handle missing values? Understand why, then drop, impute (median, group-wise, model) or flag; impute inside CV Generator vs list? Generators are lazy and memory-efficient, consumed once Statistics and ML fundamentals Question Model answer Bias-variance trade-off? Simple models underfit (bias), complex models overfit (variance); choose complexity by validation error L1 vs L2? L1 zeroes coefficients (selection); L2 shrinks smoothly; Elastic Net mixes Why cross-validation? A single split is noisy; k-fold averages and supports tuning without touching the test set Precision vs recall? Precision: flagged that are right; recall: positives caught; choose by error costs ROC-AUC vs PR-AUC? PR-AUC is more informative for rare positives Bagging vs boosting? Parallel averaging (variance) vs sequential error correction (bias) How does gradient descent work? Iteratively step weights against the loss gradient with a learning rate What is data leakage? Using information unavailable at prediction time; prevent with pipelines, time-based splits and feature cut-offs p-value? Probability of data at least this extreme if H0 w"}, {"i": "career-04", "t": "Your 90-Day Plan: Learning Roadmap, Job Search Strategy and Growing in Your First Role", "k": "Career", "f": "lessons/career-04-your-90-day-plan-learning-roadmap-job-search-strategy-and-gr.html", "s": " 90-day roadmap Weeks Analyst track Data science / ML track GenAI track 1–2 Excel lessons 1–6 Python 1–10 Python 1–12 3–4 Excel 7–12 + SQL 1–5 Python 15–22 (NumPy, pandas, EDA) Python 15–19, 23–24 (APIs) 5–6 SQL 6–15 + SQL case study Stats ML 1–6 (statistics, A/B) GenAI 1–4 (LLMs, prompts, APIs, embeddings) 7–8 Power BI 1–6 + dashboard project ML 7–15 (workflow, regression, metrics, CV) GenAI 5–8 (RAG, tools, agents, MCP) 9–10 Python 1–16, 20–22 ML 16–25 + one capstone GenAI 9–12 + capstone app 11–12 AI analytics 1–4, portfolio polish ML 26–28, portfolio polish Portfolio, deployment, write-ups 13 Interview prep (Career 2) and applications Interview prep (Career 3) Interview prep (Career 3) A sustainable weekly routine. Job-search funnel Stage Target per week Tips Research companies and roles 15 Match your projects to their domain Referral / recruiter outreach 10 Personalised messages; alumni and community contacts Applications 10–15 Tailor resume keywords; apply within 3 days of posting Screens and tests 2–4 Track results; review mistakes Interviews 1–3 Prepare company-specific cases Referral message template: Text Copy Hi &lt;Name&gt;, I&#x27;m a data analyst (SQL, Power BI, Python) who built &lt;project link&gt; on &lt;domain&gt;. I saw the &lt;role&gt; opening at &lt;company&gt; (job id). Could you refer me or share what the team values most? Happy to send my one-page resume. Thank you! Track everything in a simple sheet: company, role, date, source, status, next step, not"}, {"i": "sfmc-01", "t": "Salesforce Marketing Cloud (SFMC) Complete Guide: Email Studio, Data Extensions, SQL, AMPscript, Journey Builder and Certification", "k": "SFMC", "f": "lessons/sfmc-01-salesforce-marketing-cloud-sfmc-complete-guide-email-studio.html", "s": " What Salesforce Marketing Cloud is Salesforce Marketing Cloud Engagement (often called SFMC , formerly ExactTarget) is an enterprise platform for sending personalised email, SMS, push and WhatsApp messages and running automated customer journeys at scale. Banks, airlines, retailers and telecom companies in India use it to manage millions of customers, which is why SFMC specialists, developers and consultants are in steady demand. SFMC studios and builders and what each does. Role Typical work Email/campaign specialist Builds emails, segments and sends; runs A/B tests SFMC developer AMPscript, SSJS, SQL, CloudPages, APIs, integrations Administrator Business units, users and roles, sender profiles, deliverability set-up Consultant/architect Data model, journeys, Salesforce CRM integration (Marketing Cloud Connect) The account structure Enterprise account with business units (e.g. India Retail, India Cards) that share or separate data and content. Sender authentication package (SAP) : a branded sending domain, dedicated IP and private domain for better deliverability. Sender profiles , delivery profiles and send classifications (commercial vs transactional) decide who an email is from, the footer and whether unsubscribed people can receive it. Users and roles control who can create, approve and send. Data: subscribers, lists and data extensions Object What it is Use All Subscribers Master list of email subscribers with status (Active, Unsubscribed, Held, Bounced) Global unsubsc"}, {"i": "marketing-01", "t": "Digital Marketing Complete Guide: Strategy, SEO, Google and Meta Ads, Social, Email, WhatsApp, Analytics and AI", "k": "Marketing", "f": "lessons/marketing-01-digital-marketing-complete-guide-strategy-seo-google-and-met.html", "s": " The marketing funnel and strategy A simple full-funnel model with the main channels at each stage. Step Questions Business goal Revenue, leads, app installs, store visits? By when? Audience and personas Who buys, their problems, where they spend time, language (English, Hindi, regional) Positioning Why you vs alternatives in one sentence; proof points Offer Price, trial, bundle, guarantee, urgency Channels Where your audience is and what you can afford to test KPIs North-star metric plus channel metrics (CPL, CAC, ROAS, conversion rate) Budget and timeline Test budget, scale budget, 30-60-90 day plan SEO (search engine optimisation) Area What to do Keyword research Google Keyword Planner, Search Console, Ahrefs/Semrush; group by intent: informational, commercial, transactional, navigational On-page One topic per page; keyword in title (≤ 60 characters), H1, URL, first paragraph; meta description (≈ 155 characters); internal links; FAQs Content quality (E-E-A-T) Experience, expertise, authoritativeness, trust: author bios, original data, examples, updated dates Technical Fast pages (Core Web Vitals: LCP, INP, CLS), mobile-first, HTTPS, XML sitemap, robots.txt, canonical tags, structured data (schema.org), no broken links Local SEO Google Business Profile, consistent name/address/phone, reviews, local pages (\"PM course in Gurgaon\") Off-page Links from relevant sites, PR, guest articles, directories, partnerships AI search (AEO/GEO) Clear answers early on the page, FAQ and how-"}, {"i": "content-01", "t": "Content Creation Complete Guide: Strategy, Writing, Short and Long Video, Design, AI Tools, Distribution and Monetisation", "k": "Content", "f": "lessons/content-01-content-creation-complete-guide-strategy-writing-short-and-l.html", "s": " Start with strategy The content strategy stack. Niche formula: who + problem + your angle . Examples: \"Excel and Power BI tips for finance freshers in Hindi\", \"Product case studies explained in 60 seconds\", \"Honest reviews of AI tools for small businesses\". Writing that people finish Hooks Hook type Example Result \"This one prompt saves me 5 hours a week.\" Mistake \"Stop using VLOOKUP. Use this instead.\" Curiosity \"The PM interview question 90% of candidates get wrong\" Story \"I failed 11 interviews. Here's what changed on the 12th.\" Contrarian \"You don't need a data science degree to work in AI.\" List \"5 free tools I use every day as a PM\" Structures Short video script (30–60 s) : hook (0–3 s) → context (why it matters) → 3 steps or points → payoff → CTA (follow, comment a keyword, save). LinkedIn post : hook line → 1-line story or problem → 3–5 short points → lesson → question to readers. Use white space, no walls of text. Long-form article / blog : search-intent title → summary at the top → sections with examples → FAQ → CTA. Newsletter : one big idea, one tool, one link, one question. Python Copy import re post = &quot;&quot;&quot;I failed 11 product interviews. Then I changed one thing: I started every answer with the user, not the feature. Here is the framework I use now: 1. Who is the user and what is their goal? 2. What is painful today? 3. What would success look like in numbers? The 12th interview became my offer. What changed your interviews?&quot;&quot;&quot; words"}, {"i": "fde-01", "t": "Forward Deployed Engineer (FDE) Complete Guide: The Role, Skills, AI Deployments, Integrations and Interviews", "k": "FDE", "f": "lessons/fde-01-forward-deployed-engineer-fde-complete-guide-the-role-skills.html", "s": " What a Forward Deployed Engineer does A Forward Deployed Engineer (FDE) is a software engineer who works directly with customers to make a product succeed in their real environment. Instead of building one feature for thousands of users, an FDE builds the integrations, data pipelines, workflows and AI applications that make the platform solve one important customer's problem, and feeds what they learn back into the core product. FDEs sit between the customer and the product team. Role Focus Writes production code? Customer-facing? Software engineer Core product for many users Yes Rarely Forward Deployed Engineer Deploy and extend the product for specific customers Yes Yes, deeply Solutions / sales engineer Demos, technical sales, proofs of concept Some Yes (pre-sale) Consultant Advice, process, change management Rarely Yes Customer success manager Adoption, renewals, relationship No Yes The model was popularised by Palantir and is now used widely by AI companies (OpenAI, Anthropic and others hire FDEs or similar \"applied AI engineer\" roles) and by data and automation platforms deploying into banks, governments, healthcare and manufacturing. The skills stack Skills an FDE combines. The deployment lifecycle Phase What the FDE does Output 1. Discovery Interview users, shadow workflows, map systems and data, find the costly problem Problem statement, current-state map, baseline metrics 2. Scoping Define the smallest valuable deployment, success metrics, constraints, risks Statem"}, {"i": "speaking-01", "t": "Public Speaking Complete Guide: Confidence, Structure, Storytelling, Delivery, Slides, Meetings, Interviews and Video", "k": "Speaking", "f": "lessons/speaking-01-public-speaking-complete-guide-confidence-structure-storytel.html", "s": " Why speaking skills decide careers Product managers present roadmaps, analysts explain numbers, engineers demo work, and every interview is a speaking test. People who communicate clearly get trusted with bigger problems. The good news: speaking is a skill, not a talent, and it improves quickly with structure and practice. The four parts of a great talk. Managing nerves Technique How Prepare the first 60 seconds word-perfect The opening is when nerves peak; a rehearsed start carries you Box breathing Breathe in 4 s, hold 4 s, out 4 s, hold 4 s; repeat four times before you speak Reframe \"I'm excited\" instead of \"I'm nervous\": the body feels the same Audience focus Ask \"what do they need?\" instead of \"how do I look?\" Rehearse aloud Three full out-loud rehearsals beat ten silent read-throughs Arrive early Test the room, mic, screen share and clicker; meet a few people first Friendly faces Speak to a few engaged people in different parts of the room Structure: frameworks that always work Framework Use for Shape PREP Opinions, quick answers in meetings Point → Reason → Example → Point STAR Interview stories Situation → Task → Action → Result (with numbers) What? So what? Now what? Updates and reviews Facts → why they matter → next steps Problem → Solution → Benefit Pitches and proposals Pain → your idea → value Pyramid principle Leadership updates, written and spoken Answer first → 3 supporting points → details Past → Present → Future Strategy and roadmap talks Where we were → w"}];